[eventsheet-whatsnew v1]
{
"markdown": "# What's new

What changed in the build you have installed (v0.20.0), in the words the release notes use. Older releases live in the project's own CHANGELOG.

## Unreleased

### Added: an Inspector look is one script file

- A look is now a script that extends `EventSheetInspectorLook`, a thin layer over Godot's `EditorProperty`. Drop one in `res://eventsheet_addons/looks/` and it is a look: nothing is registered. The Variable window and the Inspector Designer list it, ask its questions, and write its marker; the Inspector draws it. The look's name, hover, the types it works on, its questions and the line it writes are all read from the file's text, so nothing is run to list it. A file in the project with a shipped look's name replaces it. Make your own with the new guide \"Make Your Own Inspector Look\".
- The progress bar is the first look to move onto a file, `addons/eventsheet/looks/progress_bar.gd`. The marker it writes is unchanged byte for byte (a generated corpus of 200 bound pairs proves the two spellings write the same line), and the Inspector draws it with the same pixels as before. Its two questions in the Variable window now read `min` and `max`, the names as written in the file.
- A marker naming a look the project does not have still opens, saves back byte for byte and is kept by the Variable window as \"Look not installed\", instead of being dropped.
- The compiler and the importer gained one generic spelling for a look with no structured attributes of its own (`look` and `look_args` on a variable). No existing branch changed, and markers already in scripts never do.
- \"Save and show\": a choice made in the Inspector Designer is saved to the script the way Save does it, with the same guards, and Godot is told the file changed. One `update_file` is all it needs: the selected node's Inspector redraws with the new look at once. The parsed decor lines are forgotten too, so an edit that keeps the script's length is not drawn from the old map.
- `eventsheet_addons/looks/` is skipped by the scan of `eventsheet_addons/` that finds ACE providers, so no reader of that list ever sees a look.
- Seven more looks moved onto files, one script each in `addons/eventsheet/looks/`: Swatch row, Texture preview, Curve preview, Four corners, Direction dial, Min-max range and Pick a row of a data table. Each file wraps the drawing widget it always used, so the Inspector shows the same pixels (a before and after photograph of the real Inspector differs by none), and the marker each writes is unchanged: a generated corpus proves the structured spelling and the file spelling write the same line, and that the line reopens and saves back byte for byte. The window lists them in the same places; their detail fields are now named as the file writes them (`max` for the dial's reach, `table` and `name_column` for the row picker).
- A look can answer `_can_draw()`: when the values the marker carried make no sense (a row picker whose table is not a variable name), the Inspector keeps Godot's plain field, as it did before.
- Number with its unit, Toggle buttons and Editable table moved onto files too (`unit.gd`, `toggle_row.gd`, `table.gd`). Their compiler and importer codes did not change at all: the file supplies the tile, the question (`units`, `choices`, `columns`, the same keys the Variable window always used) and the drawing, and reads the marker's own grammar from a new `marker_args` (the text after the look's name, split on `:` as written), so a hand-written spelling such as `eventsheet:unit:time` or an icon source holding colons still draws exactly as before. The Toggle buttons file keeps offering itself for text only, and still draws an int a script already marked (`const ALSO_DRAWS_ON`).
- A look file can state the sentence the Designer's preview card says under it (`const PREVIEW_SENTENCE`), and every shipped look now does: \"Shown as a progress bar.\" and the other pinned sentences come from the files, byte for byte as before.
- The card list (`eventsheet:cards`) stays on its original code for now: it has no tile in the Variable window today, and giving it one is a new feature, not a move.

### Fixed: searching Clone object finds the copy rows

- Typing \"Clone object\" in the picker, the sheet's own spelling of Duplicate, found nothing. It now lands on Clone Into first and also finds Duplicate Node.

### Fixed: Add Sprite starts with a picture you can see

- The Picture field of Add Sprite and Add Sprite (3D) started as `PlaceholderTexture2D.new()`, which is 1 by 1 and nearly invisible. It now starts as a 64 by 64 solid square made in code (`ImageTexture.create_from_image(Image.create_empty(64, 64, false, Image.FORMAT_RGB8))`), so the sprite shows the moment the row is dropped and still needs no file. A `GradientTexture2D.new()` was measured and is not used: with no gradient it draws Godot's magenta error square. No template or ace_id changed; only the field's starting text did.

### Fixed: an empty curve slot draws nothing

- The Curve preview look drew a sample S-curve under an empty slot, which read as if a curve existed. An empty slot now shows the quiet word \"empty\", and the look's hover says the picture is of the curve in the slot.

### Fixed: the Inspector look hovers name the marker the compiler writes, and the Doctor reads .gd sheets for Required

- Ten look hovers in the Look list said `es_drawer:` where the written marker is `eventsheet:`; a test now holds every hover to its own marker word.
- The Doctor's required-field check only read `.tres` sheets. It now reads `.gd` sheets too, the default format, so a Required variable left empty in a scene is reported in a project made of scripts.

### Fixed: the no-collision-shape help strip fits beside its two doors

- The sentence about a node with no collision shape lost its tail behind \"Add a shape\" and \"Show me in the scene\". It now says what is wrong and that the row never fires, and leaves the two options to the two doors.

### Fixed: the Manual has one door

- Manual... was in the Menu and again at the end of Tools. It stays at the foot of the Menu, where the unread mark is, and leaves Tools. F1 and Ctrl+F1 are unchanged.

### Changed: a spawn row reads with Godot's word

- A row that instantiates a scene and adds it now reads \"Instantiate\" and the row that copies a node already in the scene reads \"Duplicate\", the words Godot uses. \"Create object\" and \"Clone object\" are the sheet's own spelling of the same two rows, shown when the Words page has the sheet's verbs on, and still find them in the picker search. No ace_id, template or saved byte changed.

### Fixed: Add Sprite starts with a picture that exists in any project

- The Picture field of Add Sprite and Add Sprite (3D) started as a `load` of `res://icon.svg`, which turned the row amber in a project without that file. It starts as `PlaceholderTexture2D.new()`; choosing a file still writes the `load` of that file.

### Added: Building rows in a tool sheet own what they build, and a rebuild clears the last result

- Dropped into a Tool sheet, an Add New Node, Add Behaviour, Add Sprite, Add Shape or Add Timer row also gives the new node the scene being edited as its owner (`get_tree().edited_scene_root`, only under `Engine.is_editor_hint()`) so it is saved with the scene, and marks it so it can be found again. A new node goes under the scene being edited by default. In a game sheet the same rows write exactly what they wrote before. This is not an editor undo step.
- A new row, Remove What This Sheet Built, frees the marked direct children of a node. A tool event that builds without clearing first wears the quiet amber state, the help strip and the Doctor's Tool edits section say a rebuild adds another copy, and Clear what it built first puts the clearing row at the top of the event in one undo step.

### Added: hand-written code that makes a node opens as the Building row

- Add New Node starts with a name (NewNode), and clearing the field still leaves the node the name Godot gives it. A RemoteTransform made and parented in one run is left to the Copy Place reading it already has, so no existing reading changes for it.
- A person's own lines now open as the row that writes them: a node made from a type and given a parent (named or not), a behaviour looked up and added when it is not there, a behaviour removed, a sprite, a circle, rectangle or capsule shape (and the 3D shapes), a timer, and a branch packed into a variable. Each is read as one run, the values are the ones the lines said, the editor's own baked ids are kept as the author's spelling, and saving the sheet untouched writes the same bytes back. A run short of the whole shape (a node made with an argument, a bare `add_child`, ids that disagree) keeps the ordinary rows it always read as. Add Behaviour with Allow another is three lines identical to Add New Node with the class's own name, so that run opens as Add New Node and writes the same bytes.

### Changed: a node that plays is paused, a behaviour is turned off

- A process-mode write now reads by what the object is: a behaviour or any class of the project is turned off and on, a Timer, an AnimationPlayer, an AnimationTree or a rigid body is paused and resumed, and anything else keeps \"Set disabled\" and \"Set enabled\". The line written is exactly the same; a picked Pause Node or Unpause Node row and a hand-written `process_mode` line read alike. Measured in a running game: Disabled stops a node's code, timers, animations, physics and bound tweens, a sound keeps playing and signals still arrive, which the two rows' descriptions and the Scenes, Pausing And Turning Nodes Off guide now say.
- Search finds the existing rows under the board words: turn off, turn on, disable or enable behavior (Pause Node, Unpause Node, beside the rows that are already called Turn Off), has behavior (Has Child Of Type), change behavior, spawn from a prefab (Instantiate) and save a prefab (Save Data Asset). The picker's code preview shows a Building row as a game sheet writes it, without the lines a tool sheet adds.

### Changed: a new copy is added under the running scene, and what a row makes is an object for the rows after it

- The Under field of a new Instantiate row (and its 3D, crowd, formation, launched and free-spot spellings) now starts as the running scene, `get_tree().current_scene`, instead of the node that spawned the copy: a bullet added under its shooter turns, scales and is freed with it. A row already in a sheet keeps whatever it stored, and no template changed, so nothing already written compiles differently.
- The name an Instantiate, Make A Copy, Add New Node or Add Behaviour row leaves behind is now an object in the Add action picker for the rows after it, typed by what it made: the type of Add New Node, the behaviour of Add Behaviour, the class of the root of the scene file an Instantiate names. One table (made_names.gd) now feeds both these objects and the name chips, which also gains the 3D Instantiate that was missing from the chips.

### Added: Building, the rows for making nodes while the game runs

- A new Building page in the picker: Add New Node (any node type, optionally named, added under the running scene and kept in a variable), Add Behaviour (a script node added as a child and named after its class, with Only one or Allow another), Remove Behaviour, Add Sprite, Add Circle, Rectangle and Capsule Shape (and Sphere, Box and Capsule in 3D), Add Timer, Add To Group (Saved With Scene) and Keep As Prefab. Each one writes the plain Godot lines a person would type (`Type.new()`, `add_child`, `get_node_or_null`, `PackedScene.pack`) and needs nothing from the plugin at run time.
- The type field on Add New Node, Add Behaviour and Remove Behaviour completes class names as you type and has a Choose button that opens Godot's own Create New Node window; a script class goes in by its class_name.
- Keep As Prefab lends ownership to the nodes it packs and gives it back (the same walk Save Branch As Scene File uses, now one shared piece of code), so it can run twice on the same branch; its description says a plain Add To Group is not kept by a pack and points at the saved-with-scene row.
- Search finds the page under the other ways of saying it: add node, new node, create node, add behavior, attach behavior, give behavior, remove behavior and prefab.

### Changed: sheet shortcuts are rebound in Godot's Editor Settings > Shortcuts

- The authoring keys (add event, add condition, collapse and the rest) are now registered with Godot's own Editor Settings > Shortcuts, where you search and rebind them like any editor key, and the editor saves the change. The plugin's own Tools > Keyboard Shortcuts window and its `eventforge_shortcuts.cfg` file are gone; keys you had changed are carried into Editor Settings once, and the Output says so. The \"Another event-sheet editor\" preset went with the window - rebind X, Ctrl+E and the rest there by hand.

### Removed: the plugin's own History panel (Godot's History dock lists the edits)

- Every sheet edit already went through Godot's own undo manager under its plain name (\"Add Group\", \"Extract to Function\"), so Godot's History dock lists each one (tick its Global history). The plugin's View > History window repeated that list, so it is gone, along with the \"(undone)\" markers and the lit-up rows it drew. Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y are unchanged.

### Changed: new quality presets go in eventsheet_settings/quality/

- A bare top-level `settings/` folder is a name a project may already use for its own things, so new quality presets are written under `res://eventsheet_settings/quality/`. Presets already in `res://settings/quality/` are still read and listed, by the editor and by the Game Settings pack in a running game. Nothing is moved for you.

### Changed: the plugin speaks Godot's editor language; the separate Language picker is gone

- The plugin's interface now follows Editor Settings > Interface > Editor > Editor Language, so there is one place to choose it. View > Language and the Welcome window's Editor language dropdown are removed. A language you chose before is respected as saved, the Editor Setting `eventsheets/interface/language_override` (empty by default) is the way to differ from the editor on purpose, and dropping a translation CSV into the folders still works. View > Preview In Language is unchanged: it previews the game's languages, not the editor's.

### Removed: Tools > Register Autoload; New autoload is named in Godot's word

- Godot lists and manages autoloads in Project > Project Settings > Globals > Autoload, so the menu item that registered the open sheet's script is gone, and the Doctor and the guides send you there. The New global form stays, since it makes the script and registers it as before, and it is now called **New autoload...** in the menus, the form title and the start page. The Words page still offers \"Global\" for readers from another editor (the `global_singleton` word).

### Removed: the Preview scene and Preview project buttons

- Godot already has Run Current Scene (F6) and Run Project (F5) in the bar at the top right, and the strip's two Preview buttons were the same runs under other words. They are gone from the play button's dropdown, its Main button choice, the expanded strip and the shortcut list; the guides say F5 and F6. The runs that carry sheet debugging stay: Run Scene, Debug scene, Run with profiler and Play as host + client. A project that had chosen a Preview run as its Main button falls back to Run Scene.

### Fixed: an Ask key is no longer saved into your project

- The key for View > Ask (and the older in-editor generator) was a project setting, so a typed key landed in project.godot and was committed with the repository. It now lives in Editor Settings (per user, shown as a password field) as `eventsheets/ask/api_key`, with the `EVENTSHEETS_ASK_API_KEY` environment variable as the fallback (`EVENTSHEETS_AI_API_KEY` for the generator). A key an earlier version saved into the project is moved to Editor Settings and cleared from project.godot the first time the plugin starts, and the Output says so once.

### Changed: enabling the plugin no longer adds an autoload to your project

- The plugin used to write an `EventForgeBridge` autoload into project.godot, and the game exported it although nothing in a shipped game needs it. The provider registry is now a plain class: code-registered providers and blocks reach the editor with no autoload, and every `EventSheets.*` method works as before. A project that has the old line loses it the first time the plugin starts, and the Output says so (an autoload you made yourself under that name is left alone). A script of yours that named the old singleton should call `EventForgeBridgeRuntime` instead. The uninstall guide is updated.

### Fixed: a project made from addons/ alone runs the rows that call a runtime script

- A free-spot spawn, a retire, a bus mix, a world-look, a haptic or a sequencer row calls a small script that used to live only in this repository's `eventsheet_addons/`, so a project that copied the three addons folders met a parse error the first time such a row ran. The plugin now carries those scripts inside its library and copies the one a row needs into `eventsheet_addons/` the moment the row is applied. The Doctor names any script that calls one the project lacks and offers \"Add it to the project\". The README no longer asks you to copy them by hand.

### Added: a collision row about a node with no shape says so

- A row that asks about another node (its On node, or the body or area it names) where that node has no collision shape now goes amber, like the check on the sheet's own node did. The help strip says which node and why, and offers Add a shape (a rectangle or box shape under the node, owned by the scene, one undo step) and Show me in the scene. The Doctor files it as collisions-target-no-shape with the same two doors. Nothing is said when the node cannot be found in the one scene that runs the sheet.

### Changed: opacity takes a percent

- Typing 50% or 50 into a percentage field (Set opacity, and the Move toward weight) stores 0.5; 0.5 stays 0.5, and an expression is left exactly as written. The help strip says \"0 to 100, or 0 to 1\". Rows already in sheets are untouched.
- The everyday object rows are now translated into the nine editor languages.

### Changed: the Family Arena strike hits every member

- The Family Arena showcase's group strike now damages every Enemy in the group each time it fires (it used to give each a 15% chance), so the showcase shows groups plainly.

### Changed: the sheet reads in Godot's own words by default

- A trigger now says the Godot callback it runs on: On ready (on any node, the scene's root included), On enter tree, On exit tree, On process and On physics process, where it used to say On start of layout, On created, On destroyed, Every tick and Every physics tick. The scene rows say scene: Change scene, Reload scene, Add scene on top, Is outside screen. The Inspector is the Inspector on both sides of the Words page.
- The words other event-sheet editors use are one opt-in away, in Settings > Words > Familiar words, and the lifecycle and tick words now live in the Words table, so the page lists and controls them (five new rows: scene_ready, scene_exit, node_enter, node_exit, every_physics_tick). Typing an old word in the picker or the quick-add bar still finds the row (start of layout, every tick, every frame, on created, destroyed, go to layout, restart layout). A row's tooltip names its other word and the Godot function.
- The Welcome answers no longer switch anyone to the other editors' words: all three set Godot words. The answer for people coming from another editor keeps the Classic theme and opens the migration guide, which says where the Familiar words are.
- Wording only: ace ids, templates, emitted GDScript and stored sheets are untouched. Guides, the tutorial, the tour, the Doctor and the nine translations read in the new words, and the README pictures are retaken.

### Changed: the nouns and verbs read in Godot's words too

- Removing a node says Queue free (Queue Free Now, Queue Free After Seconds, Fade Out Then Queue Free), making a copy of a scene says Instantiate (Instantiate, Instantiate Safely, In A Formation, Myself, In A Free Spot, Into The Crowd and the scene rows), a rotation says rotation, an overlap test says Overlaps, and the group triggers say On Body Entered, In Group and On Body Exited, In Group. A row written as code (`queue_free()`, `look_at(p)`, `overlaps_body(b)`) reads the same way. Group and node are the words everywhere; the group lists say Members and Nodes where they said Entities and Instances.
- The other editors' words are the Familiar preset on Settings > Words (Destroy, Spawn a copy of, angle, Is overlapping, On collision with) and the event-sheet-editor words preset for the verb rows. Typing either word in the picker finds the row, and an exact phrase such as go to layout, destroy or spawn now ranks its row first instead of behind every row that shares a word with it.
- Hovering a word says what the other editors call it, or what Godot calls it, and the Godot call or property it writes (\"Godot writes this as modulate.a\" on Opacity).
- A blank event, the process switches and the pack descriptions say every frame, On Process or process where they said every tick; the guides say scene where they meant a scene. Wording only: ace ids, templates, emitted code and stored sheets are untouched. The translations follow in all eight languages.
- Six guide pictures are retaken in the new words (the Words page, the lifecycle readings, the scene readings, the pause pair, the save-branch pair); the pictures that still show old words are listed in the pictures audit, not hidden.

### New: the everyday object rows, under the names people look for them by

- Set Opacity and Opacity (shown as a percentage, written as Godot's 0 to 1 `modulate.a`), Set Scale, Set Size (a Control's size), Set Sprite Size (the scale that gives a size in pixels), Width, Height, Left, Right, Top and Bottom for a sprite, Instance Id, Marker Position (a Marker2D child), Is Outside The Level Bounds and Is Outside The Camera Limits. Each writes the plain Godot line and its description names the property.
- Once Ever: run an event one time for the life of the object. It keeps one plain true-or-false variable on the script. Trigger Once keeps its meaning (it fires again each time the condition goes false and then true).
- Is On Screen now adds a VisibleOnScreenNotifier2D under the node in the scene when you add the row, as one step of the scene's own undo, and says so on the status line. Ctrl+Z takes both back.

### Changed: Is On-Screen and Is Outside Layout are retired

- Both tested the window rectangle, so they were wrong as soon as a camera moved or zoomed. They keep compiling in the sheets that use them, are hidden from the picker, and point at Is On Screen (Rendering).

### New: press Preview to watch a behaviour in the editor

- Eighteen behaviours that are tuned by eye now carry a Preview button in the Inspector: Sine, Sine 3D, Rotate, Orbit, Orbit 3D, Wave, Flash, Hit Flash, Fade, Light Flicker, Light Pulse, Spring, Juice, Juice 3D, Follow Path, Move To, Move To 3D and Tween. Press it and the behaviour plays on its node in the 2D or 3D view while you drag its values; the button then reads Stop preview. Nothing plays when a scene opens, and every other behaviour is exactly the script it was, with no `@tool`.
- The scene is never changed by a preview. The host's stored values are written down when Preview starts and put back on Stop, on leaving the scene (another scene tab, closing it), and before every save, which includes pressing Play. The values are set straight on the node, so the scene tab never shows as modified and the undo history gains nothing.
- Spring, Juice and Juice 3D, which only move when set off, have a second Nudge button beside Preview. Several behaviours on one node can preview at once.
- Right-click a node in the Scene dock: Preview its behaviours presses Preview on every behaviour under it that has the button, and Stop previews puts it all back. It presses the very same buttons, so it only appears for behaviours that carry one.
- The preview is the behaviour's own code, so it works in a project with no plugin installed: copy the pack's folder in and the button is there. A behaviour declares it in its builder (`previews`), which is the whole list.
- A preview never writes into a material other nodes share. Wave and Hit Flash with Own Material off work on a copy while previewing, and Stop puts the original back, so the other nodes keep their look. This is in the pack's own code, so it holds with no plugin.
- The older Tools > Preview Behaviors on Selected Node item (and its Command Palette entry) is gone, and so is the sampler function the Sine, Rotate, Orbit, Orbit 3D and Bullet 3D packs carried for it: with the Preview button it wrote to the host a second time. `EventSheets.register_editor_preview` and `EventSheets.preview_behaviors` still exist so older code runs, and do nothing. Editor gizmos are unchanged.

### Changed: a node dragged from Godot's Scene dock starts an event on it

- Since the Objects list no longer lists the rest of the scene, Godot's Scene dock is where an unused node comes from. Dragging one onto the empty canvas or onto an event now does exactly what dragging the same object off the Objects list does: the picker opens on that object (Add condition, or Add action when it lands in an event's action lane), with the same offers as before for a node in the secret or water group or one that exports needs_key. A node the sheet already uses lands under the name the sheet gives it. Dropped on a parameter value it still fills that parameter with the node reference, as it did.
- The picture of the Objects rail in the guides is drawn again, now showing only the objects the sheet uses.

### Changed: a behaviour is added with Godot's own Create Node window

- Every Add behavior... door (the Objects list's right-click, the head's Behaviors folder, the picker's suggestion) now opens Godot's own Create Node window, titled \"Add a behaviour to <object>\". Every behaviour is a plain script with a class name and an icon, so Godot already lists it there. What you choose goes under that object in the open scene as one undo step (Ctrl+Z takes it out) and is selected, so Godot's Inspector shows its values. If the object is not in the open scene, the status line says so and nothing happens. A behaviour that ships a scene or a shader of its own is still added the way its guide says.
- The old Add behaviour window is gone: its shelves of tiles, the Properties card of values (set them in the Inspector now), and the Where choice. Nothing in the editor writes a behaviour into a script any more, because that choice lived only there.
- New: Sheet > Behaviour library... lists the behaviours the plugin carries that the project does not hold yet, each with its icon, what it is and what else comes along with it. One button, Add to project, copies its folder in and lets Godot import it, then says: Now add it with Add Child Node (Ctrl+A) under the node it should act on. A second tab lists the ones the project holds, with Remove from project... as before.
- Sheet > New behaviour... and a behaviour's file opened as a sheet (the head's Behaviors folder) are where they were. The Welcome page, the guides and the Addons index now say: a behaviour is a node, add it with Add Child Node, set its values in the Inspector, and add it from the Behaviour library if it is not listed.

### Fixed: a signal can be offered as a trigger again

- The signal window (Declare > Signal... or double-clicking a signal row) has one tick, \"Other sheets can start an event from this\". Ticked, every other sheet's picker lists the signal as a trigger; the code is the same plain signal line with the short trigger comment above it. A new signal on a behaviour or a global starts ticked, on an ordinary sheet unticked, and reopening the row changes it later in one undo step. It replaces the Add trigger button of the removed Properties and actions window.

### Removed: the Properties and actions window

- Sheet > Properties and actions... is gone, and a new behaviour or global no longer opens it. It only listed what the open file offers and had Add buttons that opened the ordinary windows, so nothing it did is lost: Add variable is on the Add menus, New Function > Action / Condition / Expression publishes a function, and the Anatomy tab of the side rail lists a behaviour's properties, actions, conditions and triggers. Turning a global on is Godot's own Project Settings > Globals > Autoload.
- The one thing the window alone did was add a published trigger (a signal row marked as a trigger); that now lives as a tick in the signal window.

### Removed: the Project bar

- The Project strip at the foot of the Objects rail is gone, with its View > Project bar item and the saved on/off choice. It listed scenes, scripts, classes, sounds and files, which Godot's FileSystem dock and Quick Open already do. A scene, script or sound is opened, found or dragged the Godot way. Dragging a class, sound or scene out of that strip onto the canvas is gone with it; drag from the Objects list or the FileSystem dock instead.
- The Welcome page's \"coming from another event-sheet editor\" answer no longer switches the bar on, and its summary lines no longer mention it. The two Project bar colours are gone from the theme tokens. The word the familiar vocabulary used for the FileSystem dock is now \"FileSystem dock\", and the editor-tool row that reveals a file reads \"Show in FileSystem dock\" (its id and the code it writes are unchanged).
- The Start page's \"This editor\" card now just opens the plugin's own script as a sheet.

### Removed: the Objects list no longer lists the rest of the scene

- The \"Also in the scene\" section is gone. It repeated Godot's Scene dock. The list keeps the objects the sheet itself uses, with their counts, the filter, the right-click menu and dragging onto the sheet. The tab's hover no longer counts the scene's other nodes.

### Removed: object marks - Godot's groups and exported variables decide the canvas offers

- The plugin's own per-project \"marks\" (secret, water, needs key) are gone, together with the store that held them. Dropping an object on the canvas now asks Godot itself: a node in the group `secret` is offered the secrets counter, a node in the group `water` is offered the two rows that raise and lower the in-water flag, and a node whose script exports `needs_key` is offered the door event. Add a node to a group in the Node dock, Groups; the answer lives in the scene file, so it travels with the scene and the plugin keeps nothing. An object the open scene does not hold is offered nothing extra. Old projects that still hold the three metadata keys load as before and ignore them. The Secrets guide, Working In 3D, the Mark Secret Found and Try Door descriptions and the Keycard door starter note say how. The boomer_level showcase puts its SecretRoom in the group `secret`. No emitted GDScript, `ace_id` or block `kind_id` changed.

### Changed: \"Instance variables\" now reads \"Variables\"

- The head folder, the Add menu and context menu items, the Add variable shortcut label, the variable list headings in the picker (\"Player - variables\"), the guided tour, the tutorials and the guides all say \"variable\" and \"Variables\" now. The pack folder \"Internal state\" is unchanged. Only wording changed: no name in the emitted code, no `ace_id` and no block `kind_id` moved. The nine translation files carry the new words.

### Removed: the Properties bar

- The panel to the right of the sheet that showed the selected condition, action, group, class or object as fields is gone, along with its View > Properties Bar item, its drag handle and edge strip, and its width setting. The sheet now uses the full width. Every edit it offered has a place that stays: double-click a parameter for the Edit Parameter dialog, edit a group's name and switch on its own row, and edit the class name, base type, icon, tool switch and description on the sheet's head row. A selected function still opens in Godot's own Inspector. Layouts saved with the bar open or tucked load as before and simply ignore its width.
- The \"Change type\" question that listed the members an event uses and the new type lacks was only in the bar, so it is gone too.

### Removed: the Object properties window

- Clicking an object's name in a row no longer opens a window. A click there now only selects the row, like a click anywhere else on the cell. The window repeated what Godot already shows: the Scene dock for the hierarchy, the Inspector for values. It also carried a Hierarchy section (add a child by dragging, unparent, flags), a variables table and the buttons Add condition, Add action, Highlight rows, Select in scene and Show in code. Add condition, Add action, Highlight rows and Select in scene are still on the Objects list (right-click or click an entry). Show in code has no other door. Double-clicking an entry in the Objects list or a class in the Project bar opens nothing now, and View > Object Properties is gone. The flags chip on an Add child row stays.

### Removed: the Inspector's variables table

- Selecting a node whose script came from a sheet no longer shows an \"Instance variables\" table at the top of the Inspector. Godot's own Inspector already lists every `@export` variable of the script as an ordinary row, so the table repeated them. The \"Shape ...\" link and the Edit Event Sheet button stay.

### Changed: more air between events in the Editor Native look

- Two events in the Editor Native look are now 6 px apart instead of 1, so each reads as its own event and not as a continuation of the one above. The conditions and actions inside one event are exactly as tight as before, and a sub-event still sits 1 px under its parent. The README pictures of the workspace and of the Generated GDScript panel were retaken to show it.

### Changed: clicking beside the sheet clears the selection

- A click in the strip left of the sheet (the side panel's rail) or right of it, outside every event and variable, now clears the selection, the way a click on the empty canvas does. The sheet keeps keyboard focus, so Delete after that cannot reach the Scene tree. Ctrl and Shift clicks there change nothing.

### Added: a behaviour says what it changes on its parent

- A behaviour that writes into the node it works on now says so in its description, which shows in the Add Node window and the Inspector. For example \"Changes on its parent: up_direction, velocity, move_and_slide().\" A behaviour that only reads its parent says nothing. The line is read from the behaviour's own code each time the pack is built, so it cannot fall out of date.

### Added: Acts on, for a behaviour that is not a direct child

- Every behaviour that works on a node of a certain type (Platformer Movement, Bullet, Anchor, Sine and the rest) now has an Acts on field at the top of its Inspector. Leave it empty and the behaviour works on its parent, exactly as before. Set it, and the behaviour can sit anywhere in the scene, for example under a Behaviours folder node.
- The Doctor and the warning in the Output both say \"or set Acts on\" as the second way out. Scenes saved before the field existed load with it empty, so nothing in them changes.

### Added: the Doctor says when a behaviour is under the wrong node

- Check the project now reads every scene and says it when a behaviour sits under the wrong kind of node, before the game runs. For example \"Platformer Movement is under a Node2D; it needs a CharacterBody2D: put it under one.\" The finding offers \"Move it under <the nearest node that fits>\" and \"Show in scene\".
- A behaviour that cannot find its host says the same words in the Output while the game runs, in place of \"PlatformerMovement behavior requires a CharacterBody2D parent.\" The behaviour scripts stay ordinary scripts that work in any project without the plugin.

### Changed: the Family Arena showcase teaches call down, signal up

- Each Enemy now moves itself: its own sheet has an Every tick row that moves it by its own fall_speed and wraps it back to the top. The arena sheet no longer reaches into the enemies to move them; it spawns them and runs the one rule that is about the whole family, a strike that walks every Enemy with a typed For each (`for enemy: Enemy in ...`) and may hit each one through Enemy: Take Damage. The showcase plays the same: enemies fall, the strike hits them, they die.

### Added: a picked instance is an object in Add action and Add condition

- Inside a pick (a For each, Pop back / front into, Pick nearest / first where / random, a typed local, or a signal that hands the event its instance) and every sub-event under it, Add action and Add condition list the picked instance at the top of the objects page, by its type (Unit, Enemy; its name as written when two picks share a type). Choosing it offers that class's own rows and those of what it is a kind of (Node2D: Set position, Set tint, Hide), each already aimed at the picked name. Outside the pick it is not offered.
- Rows on a picked instance read as that object's rows, whether hand-written or picked: `unit.start_turn()` reads \"Unit | Start turn\", and a test such as `unit.health < 20` reads \"Unit | health < 20\". A For each written with a typed item (`for unit: Unit in selected`) is understood as a pick, and a sub-event or an Else under it keeps it.
- Where a row needs a value, the expression picker has a \"Picked here\" section with the instances the event picked or was handed by its trigger, so Push back, Delete value and Contains do not need the name typed.

### Changed: findings draw no frame, and long sentences are read whole

- A row with a finding is a faint tint and an amber event number (red for one that cannot run). The amber frame round the whole event is gone.
- A finding's sentence that does not fit the status bar is said whole, wrapped, on a line of its own above it, with the doors left in the bar.
- A For each or other loop cell wraps in the condition lane like any other long condition instead of being cut mid-word.

### Added: a pick lasts for its event and its sub-events, and nowhere else

- A For each, a Pop back / front into and a typed local are picks. A row under one that acts on the picked name now shows the picked TYPE in the object column (Unit, Enemy); when an event holds two picks of one type (an attacker and a target) each shows its name as written instead. A sub-event inherits the pick, and a sibling event starts with nothing picked.
- A row that acts on a name another event picked, where nothing by that name is picked, is an amber row. The help strip and the Doctor say \"No Unit is picked here\", with the doors Pick the Unit from a group (for a declared family) and Pick the Unit from a list (when the sheet has an Array of that type); each adds the pick to the event in one undo step.

### Changed: the pick rows can choose their set from a group or a list

- Pick Nearest, Pick A Random One, Pick Where, Pick First Where, Pick Last Where, Pick Top and Pick Bottom have a \"Choose a group or list\" dropdown beside their From box. It lists the project's groups first (a group is Godot's way to keep an unordered set of nodes, and it drops a destroyed one by itself), then the sheet's own list variables, then the lists of nodes below the sheet's node. Choosing writes the expression into the box, which stays free text. A pick never removes from the list; to take the picked one out, follow with Delete value.

### Added: a group is Godot's way to keep a set of nodes, and the sheet says so

- An Array of nodes that the sheet never reads by position or order (only push, delete, contains and For each) is an amber row. The help strip and the Doctor say a group is the Godot way and that it drops a destroyed instance by itself. The door Use a group instead rewrites the rows in one undo step: Push back becomes Add to group, Delete value becomes Remove from group, Contains becomes Is in group, For each over the list becomes For each in group, and the list's declaration goes. The door is only offered when every use of the list is one of those.
- An ordered list of nodes that is looped over and never has a destroyed instance taken out of it anywhere on the sheet is amber too, with the door Drop freed instances first, which adds an event above the loop with that row.
- A row that names a list of a parent, a sibling or an Autoload gets a note (never an error): it keeps working, and the words suggest asking that object through a function or a signal.

### Added: three rows for lists of instances

- Push back, once adds a value only when the list does not hold it already (`if not selected.has(unit): selected.append(unit)`). Push back is unchanged, so a deck can still hold two of a card.
- Set to the instances in a group where a test fills a list from a group in one row (`selected.assign(get_tree().get_nodes_in_group(\"units\").filter(func(one): return box.has_point(one.position)))`). When order does not matter, the group itself is Godot's own tool: it forgets a destroyed instance by itself.
- Drop freed instances from a list removes the entries whose instance was destroyed (`selected.assign(selected.filter(is_instance_valid))`), for ordered lists that nothing else cleans.
- Each spelling, written by hand, opens as its row and saves back to the same bytes.

### Changed: a list row reads under its list

- Every row on the Variables: Array page (Push back, Pop back into, Move back to, Clear, Is empty, For each popped...) now shows the list it acts on in the object column, written as the row writes it (`undo_stack`, `$Enemy.draw_pile`), with the variable mark, where it used to say System. Only the reading changed: the rows, their words and the code they write are the same.

### Changed: the Array dropdown offers only the sheet's own lists and those of nodes below it

- The list fields used to offer the arrays of any object in the scene and of every Autoload. They now offer the sheet's own arrays and the arrays of nodes below the sheet's node (`$Child.items`, `%UniqueChild.items`). A parent, a sibling or an Autoload is asked through a function or a signal instead. A row that already names one keeps working and keeps its text when edited.

### Fixed: the shipped-pack warning and the swap refusal say it once, in full

- The warning on the first edit of a shipped behaviour file (\"is a shipped behaviour: adding a newer version of the pack replaces this file and your edits.\") used to share the status bar with the counts and was cut off. It now has a line of its own above the status bar, at the dock's whole width, wrapped, with the Edit it anyway door beside it.
- Swapping two typed arrays of different types said the same sentence under the field, in the help card and beside Done. It is now said once, under the field; the help card keeps describing the field, nothing is written beside Done, and Done stays blocked.

### Changed: the help card in every window is a plain panel

- The card that explains the focused field (and the Doctor and row help strips built from it) no longer has a coloured outline or a thick coloured left edge. It keeps the thin hairline every inset panel has, like Godot's own docks. Its heading is the muted caption colour; only a warning (amber) or an error (red) colours the heading.
- The one-sentence note under a field (a name already in use, a node not in the scene yet) lost its coloured left edge too. It keeps the faint tint of its tone.
- The small badges beside a page title (\"Action\", a pack's name) and the chosen cell of the animation frame strip lost their coloured outlines; each keeps its tint. A field that cannot be accepted still shows its red outline, because that one is a state.
### Added: Pop back into and Pop front into, and Stack and Queue groups in the picker

- Taking an item off a list used to be an expression (`Set last to undo_stack.pop_back()`), so a pop hid inside another row, and a pop used twice in one row, or in a condition that runs every frame, silently ate items. The Variables: Array page now has two actions that keep what they took in a name you choose: Pop back of a list into a name (`var last = undo_stack.pop_back()`) and Pop front of a list into a name (`var job = jobs.pop_front()`). Push back is the row it always was.
- The same page gains two groups that only help you find the words: Stack (a stack pushes and pops at the back: last in, first out) lists Push back, Pop back into and Back, and Queue (a queue pushes at the back and pops at the front: first in, first out) lists Push back, Pop front into and Front. A row says the same thing wherever it is listed.
- A hand-written `var name = list.pop_back()` or `pop_front()` opens as its row and saves back to the same bytes.

### Added: an If empty field on the pops, and Back / Front with a fallback

- Popping an empty array prints an error in Godot and hands back nothing. Pop back into and Pop front into now have an optional If empty field. Filled, the row writes `var last = undo_stack.pop_back() if not undo_stack.is_empty() else Vector2i(-1, -1)` and needs no guard. Left blank it is the plain pop, and the row's help says to guard it with Array Is Empty, inverted (the cross), the guard that already exists. The guarded one-line pop opens as the same row.
- Two new expressions, First Item (or a fallback) and Last Item (or a fallback), read the front or the back without removing it and give your own value when the list is empty. First Item and Last Item are the rows they always were.

### Added: Move back / front to, Move all to and Swap, and the Array dropdown offers another object's arrays

- Moving items between two lists is one row now instead of two hand-joined rows and a temporary: Move back of a list to another (`redo_stack.append(undo_stack.pop_back())`), Move front of a list to another (`pop_front()`), Move all of a list to another (`other.append_array(list)` then `list.clear()`, in order), and Swap two lists (a temporary named `__swap_` plus the row's own id, then the two writes, so two swaps in one event never collide). Each opens again from the same hand-written lines and saves back to the same bytes; a swap through any other temporary stays the plain code it is.
- Every list field is the Array variable dropdown, and it now also offers the arrays of the other objects in the scene after the sheet's own: `$Enemy.draw_pile`, `%Deck.cards`, an Autoload's list. A row that already names one keeps it when you edit it, even with that scene closed.
- Swapping two typed arrays of different element types is refused in the parameters window with one line naming both types (\"undo_stack is Array[Vector2i] and scores is Array[int], and a typed array only holds its own type.\"). An untyped Array on either side is not refused.

### Added: For each popped from the front / back of a list

- Working through a queue used to be a While and a take kept in step by hand, where a forgotten pop is a frozen game. Two loop conditions on the Variables: Array page, For each popped from the front of a list as a name and For each popped from the back, run their event once per item and take each item off the list as it is used (`while not spawn_queue.is_empty():` then `var scene = spawn_queue.pop_front()`). The list is empty when the loop ends, and items pushed while it runs are handled too.
- A hand-written `while not L.is_empty():` whose first line takes from the same list opens as this loop and saves back to the same bytes; a while with any other body, `remove_at(0)` instead of `pop_front`, or a take from another list stays the While it is. The \"While nothing inside can end\" warning stays quiet for it, because the take changes the list. The For Each dialog gains the two matching collection choices so editing the loop never turns it into a plain walk.

### Added: Push back, keeping the last N

- A trail, a replay buffer, an input buffer for combos or the last few chat lines was an append and a trim loop written separately. One action does both: Push back a value to a list, keeping the last N (`trail.append(point)`, then `while trail.size() > 20:` and `trail.pop_front()`). The oldest items drop off the front, so the newest stays. The three-line run opens as this row; a trim written with `if`, or `remove_at(0)` instead of `pop_front`, stays the rows it is today.

### Fixed: a pop knows its type

- Taking an item off an `Array[Vector2i]` left the local untyped, so autocomplete and the type check knew nothing about it. Pop back into and Pop front into over a typed array now write `var last: Vector2i = undo_stack.pop_back()`, and the loop For each popped from the front / back types its item the same way, so `last.` offers `x` and `y` and a wrong use is caught as you type. An untyped Array gives the untyped local it always did, and a hand-written `var last = undo_stack.pop_back()` keeps its spelling (the type is added only when the row is made on the sheet); a hand-written typed pop opens as the row with its type kept.
### Changed: your own scripts have no lock on their functions

- A function in a script you opened (an `On function` event) is now editable like any event from the first click: drag an action, double-click it, Add action, Delete, with no menu step first. Before, every hand-written function opened read-only until you found Make Function Body Editable in its right-click menu. An edit still rewrites only the lines of the rows it changes, and an untouched function still saves byte for byte. The menu item stays for anyone who wants a function read-only, renamed Lock this function / Unlock this function; a locked function reads grey, and a drag on it says which menu item unlocks it.

### Added: a shipped behaviour pack asks once before its first edit

- A script in one of the plugin's own pack folders (under `eventsheet_addons/`, including a pack copied into your project from the library) keeps its function bodies locked. The first drag, double-click, Add action or Delete on one says in the help strip: \"<file> is a shipped behaviour: adding a newer version of the pack replaces this file and your edits.\", with one door, Edit it anyway. The door unlocks every function in that file; the gesture that asked is not performed, so you repeat it. Undoing an edit never locks the file again.

### Added: lock and unlock choices are remembered for the project

- A function you lock or unlock, and a pack file you unlock with Edit it anyway, stay that way after you close and reopen the editor. They are kept in the project's editor settings, the same place folded regions are, and never in the `.gd`, so the file and a teammate's copy are untouched.

### Fixed: dragging an action inside a function's body moves it

- In a function you wrote by hand (such as an \"On function hit\" event), dragging one of its actions to reorder it, or onto another event, did nothing at all and said nothing. The drop marker now shows inside the function, the action moves (with its quiet note, if it has one), the file saves with only those lines moved, and Undo puts it back in one step.

### Fixed: expression conditions that name `delta` keep their colour

- Once `delta` stopped being respelled `dt`, a condition such as `entry.integrate(delta)` or `_separate(delta)` stopped being drawn as the coloured expression it had always been (the reader only coloured an expression it had reworded). It reads exactly as written and wears the expression colour again.

### Changed: a `#` note among a function's statements is the note of the action below it

- A `#` line written directly above a statement inside a function is now that action's quiet note (it reads after the action, muted) instead of a separate comment row in the action lane. Moving the action moves its note, copying it copies it, and the file saves back to the same lines. A TODO or FIXME note, a note kept apart from the statement by an empty line, the note that opens a body, a commented-out statement and a `##` documentation line stay comment rows.

### Changed: an untyped variable reads `any`

- `var score` with no type reads `any score` on every row look (the Inspector-style row used to read `Variant score`), with the quiet note \"no starting value\" where its value would be. A variable that spells `: Variant` keeps the word it wrote.

### Fixed: the Godot-call draw rows start with a capital

- Draw Line, Draw Rectangle, Draw Circle, Draw Arc, Draw Texture and the rest of the Godot-call draw rows read \"draw line from a to b\" in lower case beside rows that start with a capital. They now read \"Draw line from a to b in red\", \"Set draw transform to ...\" and \"Redraw\" like every other action. Reading only: ids, templates and the code they write are unchanged.

### Changed: the two sets of Draw rows have names that say which is which

- Searching \"draw\" under an On Draw event listed two Draw Line, two Draw Circle, two Draw Arc and so on: Godot's own `draw_*` calls (picker group \"Drawing: Godot calls\") and the older rows that paint onto ANOTHER node's canvas. The older rows now read Draw Line On Node, Draw Circle On Node, Draw Rect On Node, Draw Arc On Node, Draw Polygon On Node, Draw Polyline On Node, Draw Text On Node and Draw Texture On Node. Their ids and the code they write are unchanged.

### Fixed: a restored session reads every tab as events, not just the last

- Reopening the editor with several scripts open used to bring back only the last tab as events; the others stayed as raw `function` blocks, because opening each tab dropped the background read of the one before it. The restore now lets each script finish reading before it opens the next, so a restored tab reads exactly like a freshly opened one.

### Fixed: the parameters window no longer writes a call with a gap in the middle

- Setting a later optional argument of a Godot-call row (`draw_line`'s Antialiased, `draw_rect`'s Width) while an earlier optional one was blank used to write `draw_rect(r, c, , 2.0)`. The window now fills every blank optional argument before the last given one with its own default (`draw_rect(r, c, true, 2.0)`), so the call is always valid GDScript; a call with nothing given after a blank still writes the short call, and a lifted short call re-emits byte for byte.

### Changed: a statement written over several lines opens as one row

- A call, an assignment or a `\\`-continued line that you wrote over several lines (a `draw_rect(` with its `)` on a line of its own, `bullet.position = Vector2(` with one argument per line) is now one action instead of a block of code. The row shows the statement on one line; double-click it and the parameters window shows the value as you wrote it, line breaks and indents included, and keeps whatever layout you give it. Saving writes the same lines back.

### Changed: constants, plain variables and annotated declarations open as variable rows

- `const SPEED = 160`, `const TAN30DEG = tan(deg_to_rad(30))` and `const BULLET_SCENE = preload(\"res://player/bullet.tscn\")` are variable rows marked as constants, reading the type Godot infers for them (`float`, `PackedScene`) while the file keeps writing none.
- `var score` with no type and no value is a variable row that reads `any` and has no starting value. A type written with a space before its colon (`var tween : Tween`), a hinted export that infers its type (`@export_range(1, 1000) var row_size := 100`) and a `@warning_ignore(...)` line above a variable or a signal stay with their declaration and are written back as they were.

### Changed: the `#` notes of a script open as comment rows

- A note above a function, over a declaration or between two blocks is a comment row written back exactly where it sits, and the notes among a function's statements are comment rows between its actions. `##` documentation lines stay with the function they describe, and a `#region` fence stays a fence.

### Changed: a note at the end of a line stays with its line

- `hide() # why` is the action Hide with the comment \"why\", `const MOTION_SPEED = 160 # Pixels/second.` shows its note in the variable row's note column, and `if alive: # player only` is the condition Is alive with that note. The spacing before the `#` is kept exactly as you wrote it, and editing the row's values keeps its note.

### Changed: `signal name()` opens as a signal row

- A signal declared with empty brackets is a signal row that remembers the brackets and writes them back; a signal made in the sheet with no values is still written without them.

### Added: Godot's own drawing calls as actions under On Draw

- A new Drawing: Godot calls group holds Godot's `draw_*` calls by their plain names: Draw Line, Draw Rectangle, Draw Rectangle Outline, Draw Circle, Draw Circle Outline, Draw Arc, Draw Polyline, Draw Polygon, Draw Text, Draw Texture, Draw Texture Rect, Draw Texture Region and Set Draw Transform, plus Redraw for `queue_redraw()`. Every field carries Godot's own argument name, and the ones Godot lets you leave off can be left blank, so a hand-written `draw_line(a, b, Color.RED)` opens as the same row and saves back to the same bytes.
- The drawing rows are only offered under an On Draw event, because Godot refuses `draw_*` calls anywhere else. Redraw works anywhere: it asks the node to paint itself again, which runs On Draw.
- Rows that already existed for drawing on another node's canvas (Draw Line, Draw Circle, Draw Rect and the rest) are unchanged.
- A sentence can now carry a piece that shows only while its field has a value, so Draw Line reads \"Draw line from a to b in red\" and gains \", width 2\" once a width is set.

### Fixed: the kind check no longer builds a broken test script from a match arm

- A `match` arm, an `elif` or an `else` that stayed in a code cell was handed to the kind check as if it were a statement of its own, which Godot cannot read, so a parse error was printed every time such a sheet opened and the lines judged after it in the same pass could go unchecked. Those cells are now left out of the check.
- A row whose code has an optional trailing argument (a width you may leave blank) no longer makes the same check print a parse error while you edit one of its fields.

### Changed: the two tick triggers read for what they are

- A `_process` event now reads **Every tick** and a `_physics_process` event reads **Every physics tick**. They used to read \"Every tick (draw)\" and \"Every tick (physics)\", and `_process` has nothing to do with drawing. Only the words changed: the trigger ids, the templates and the code a sheet compiles to are exactly what they were. The same words are used where a hand-written `set_process(false)` / `set_physics_process(false)` reads (\"Set Every tick deactivated\", \"Set Every physics tick deactivated\"), in the muted note on a conditionless physics tick, and in the Doctor's handler list.

### Fixed: `delta` reads as `delta`

- A row that says `Vector2(speed * delta, 0)` in the code used to read `Vector2(speed * dt, 0)` on the sheet. Rows now read your identifiers exactly as you wrote them, so `delta` stays `delta` everywhere (conditions, actions, expressions, a pack's rows). A bare `get_process_delta_time()` reads `delta` too, and a call on another object (`host.get_process_delta_time()`) keeps its own spelling.

### Changed: the README's player example is real Godot, and its four pictures show it

- The example in \"What it compiles to\" now takes its hits from an `Area2D` child named `Hitbox` whose `body_entered` signal is wired in the scene, because a `CharacterBody2D` has no `body_entered` signal of its own and the old `_on_body_entered` would never have run. The sheet reads that handler as a real **On collision with** trigger on the `Hitbox`, and the hit is `health -= 10`. The example and its `enemy` scene live in `demo/readme_example/`, the scene opens with \"No problems\", and the README's bullets use the words the editor shows.
- The workspace and Generated GDScript pictures (Default and Light themes) were retaken on that example at a comfortable editor zoom, so every word reads at the README's width.
- The mistake-check tests that pin the nearest group name to a typo now judge groups against their own fixtures only, so a group elsewhere in the project (the example's `enemy`) cannot change which name they offer.

## 0.20.0 - 2026-11-07 - Feel Native

This release makes the editor sit inside Godot like one of its own docks, and makes it sturdier after a heavy overhaul of how it looks and behaves. Nearly every window, row and menu was reworked to say plainly what it does, to work in Godot's light and dark themes, and to stay quick on big sheets. If your daily driver is Construct 3 or Godot, the picker, the sheet and the fields now answer to names you already know.

### In brief

#### A look that belongs in Godot

- A new default look, Editor Native, that reads like Godot's own docks: variables listed the way the Inspector lists a property, blue condition lanes and green action lanes, and each event drawn as one panel with its nesting as lines in the margin.
- Words, names and values sit in aligned columns, the parameter band hangs under a function's line, and sub-events pack tight under their parent.
- It follows Godot's Light and Default editor themes from the moment the editor starts, keeps its toolbar words readable on any theme base, and switching the editor theme while a sheet is open re-colours the sheet with no restart.
- A sheet opens on one thin head bar, the side panel rests as a rail, and every line leads with its icon instead of a word in front of it.

#### Familiar names, familiar flow

- Type a name from another engine or from Construct 3 and the picker finds the verb that already ships; expression fields read `Mathf.Lerp` and `Random.Range`.
- The Welcome window asks how you usually make games and offers a first game you already know as a showcase with a ten-step tour.
- Picking is offered in Godot's shape: write a condition on another object's sheet and the sheet offers the For each that goes with it.
- Click a script in the FileSystem and its sheet opens, and the Manual opens on the page it names from nearly any row, window or menu.

#### Variables, functions and your own objects

- A variable's Inspector options each open as the variable they belong to, export groups read as boxes, and the variable window is four cards where every look is a described choice.
- One Save choice per variable (Never, Auto, Load only, Save only, Manual), and values players already saved keep loading through a rename or an update.
- Functions are drawn as events on every sheet and edited in Godot's own Inspector, with every choice described and a name that would break the script refused before it is written.
- Shape a field in the Inspector Designer: groups as tabs, two fields side by side, a dropdown filled by a function, picking a table row by name, and search, sort and pages for big tables.
- New behaviour and New global are forms you can reopen, Add behaviour is a shelf of tiles with your own first, New Data Table is filled in like a spreadsheet, and a script class is made like inserting an object.
- An object's states work project-wide, with one state row that carries a default and a description for each state.
- A project holds only the behaviours it asked for: the packs ship in a library folder Godot never imports, and adding one copies a folder.

#### Your own code opens as rows

- Functions written inside a call, classes inside a script, values over several lines and statements continued with `\\` open as rows and save back byte-exact.
- The plugin's own scripts open as rows too, with no block of code on screen.
- Hand-written `.gd` scripts open as tidy, readable rows, and every way a `.gd` save used to drop something (disabled events, groups, regions, class descriptions) is closed.
- Position rows say which space they mean: Set X / Y / Z read relative to the parent, and world twins sit beside them for setting, reading, spawning, wrapping, bounding and gliding.
- New rows: Tell Children, First Parent Of Type, Next / Previous Sibling, Reset Variable(s), Pick First / Last Where, Has Been True For, and pause requests that keep the game paused until everyone who asked lets go.
- New packs: Flock and Flock 3D (birds, fish and swarms that steer by their neighbours), and a Platformer Pathfinding that learns every way a character gets around.

#### Catching mistakes as you type

- Type checking in every field: Godot's own analyzer answers in a plain sentence plus its own words, underlines the exact span, and blocks OK on an error.
- Autocomplete follows the code the way Godot's script editor does: dot completion through chains, argument hints, names inside quotes, and descriptions from the class reference.
- Colour values show a square of the colour and read the way you typed them: click the square for Godot's own ColorPicker with no window in between, right-click it to Copy As hex, RGB, HSV or code, and pick how every colour reads with Read Colours As (your code never changes).
- Nine mistakes Godot only reports when the game runs are caught on the row with a fix beside each, and a broken script marks its row with Godot's own words on save.
- A run-time error in a `.gd` sheet or a hand-written script links to its row, in a game started from the editor, with nothing added to the game.

#### Studios and windows

- The Save studio gains file, slots, look-inside and player-settings tabs, and the Translation Studio reads as four steps, stays fast at a million lines, and never rewrites a catalog you wrote by hand.
- Every dialog says what it will do before you press OK, opens at the size of what it holds, and reads in a light theme.
- Renaming a variable shows everything it touches first, and project-wide find, replace and rename now see `.gd` sheets.
- The MCP server edits `.gd` sheets, works through the open editor, and edits single rows.

#### Faster, and cleaner when it leaves

- The editor starts in about 11 seconds instead of 30, and the picker lists every pack verb (it showed 142 of 4,477).
- Dropping a drag on an 803-row sheet takes about 0.75 s (it was 2.8 s), and a fold click on a large sheet is under 50 ms (it was about 0.5 s), and hover and drag repaint only the row they touch.
- Disabling the plugin frees its whole workspace instead of leaving it in memory.

### Fixed: the Editor Native look on Godot's Light editor theme, and a live switch of the editor theme

- The open sheet tab was a near-black block with unreadable words on a light editor: a tab bar that is not in the tree yet asks the engine's defaults rather than the editor's theme, and the defaults' selected tab is a 60% black plate. A light editor now waits for the bar to enter the tree and borrows the editor's own selected-tab box, keeping the accent edge. A dark editor is untouched.
- Variable rows were pale green with a green edge in the light hand; they are now the design's neutral band (#d2d2d2 with a #c4c4c4 edge), as the dark hand's are neutral greys. A new test holds every fill of the light hand to be light, every fill of the dark hand to be dark, the variable cards to be neutral, and every ink-on-fill pair the sheet draws (sentences on both lanes, notes, group titles, badges, include and class cards) to a contrast ratio at four editor bases.
- Switching the editor between dark and light with the workspace open left every icon the plugin had fetched in the old theme's colours (white strokes on pale buttons, a pale gear on every row). The editor's icon set is now noticed when it is replaced: the dock's buttons, fields, tabs, lists, trees and menus are re-pointed at the same names in the new set, the row builder's and the row marks' icon caches are dropped, the retained row paint's frame signature carries the icon generation so rows that did not otherwise change are painted again, and the strip and tab edge are restyled. Nothing needs a restart. Windows opened after the switch read the new theme as they are built; a dialog already open keeps what it was built with until it is reopened.
- An editor whose icon and font colour is set to Light on a light base hands every plain button white words on a white box (Godot's own buttons included), which left the sheet strip's Menu, overflow, Code and Both buttons blank and the open tab's name pale. Every word of the strip and of the sheet tabs that does not stand out from its box by a contrast of 3 is now drawn in a dark or light ink, asked again on every theme change; a word the editor already draws readably is left as it is, so a dark editor and Godot's Light preset are unchanged. `strip_ink_readable_test` pins all four bases.
- A switch of the editor theme reimported the plugin's icon from a deferred call, once per theme notification of the switch (eleven): the editor refused each with a progress dialog error and could close outright, and a reimport running frames of its own could start a second one inside it. The check now runs once, from a timer after the switch settles, at most once per icon colour.
- Switching the editor theme with a sheet open could freeze the editor outright, most often while it was still reading the project's scripts after a first start (the screen stopped and nothing used any processor time). The sheet's follow-up to a theme switch re-read every row, which compiles small stand-in scripts to check the kinds of values, and that compile never came back when it ran from inside the engine's own theme notification. The dock now only notes the switch there and follows it a tenth of a second later, once for the whole burst of notifications. Measured on a sheet with its answers dropped: the editor froze on every one of 6 switches before, on none of 5 after.

### Fixed: the first sheet tab no longer compiles the dialogs, the picker and the Doctor

- With a scene open in the editor (the ordinary case), a comment row asked for the scene's node list through the parameters dialog, which compiled the dialog, the picker, the public API and the Doctor into the first open. The node lists now live in `EventSheetSceneUniqueNames`. Measured in the real editor with a scene open, medians of three fresh processes: `boomer_level` 22.2 s and 538 scripts before, 17.4 s and 455 scripts after; `platformer_pathfinding` 36.8 s before, 33.8 s after.
- A later session's `tools/measure_editor_boot.gd` runs (scene open, three after two warm-up runs) read 20.0 to 22.9 s on `boomer_level` with none of the click-only scripts compiled by the open and the public API still uncompiled after the first click on Add; this machine reads a few seconds apart between sessions, so the before and after pair above is the comparison. A run with no scene open never showed this, and neither can a headless process.
- The picker, the parameters dialog, the variable dialog and the row diagnostics no longer name the public API or the Doctor (`pack_directories.gd`, `extension_registries.gd`, `EventSheetVariableOwners`), and the picker's scripts compile on a worker during the idle warm. First picker build 1.5 s (0.75 s when the open had already paid for it), first parameters dialog 0.7 s, first Doctor about 21 s (19 s).
- The idle warm takes no step while a sheet is opening or its lift is running, then waits thirty quiet frames.
- The earlier split of the row builder into a helper loaded on demand is reverted: 0.25 s on the small sheet (19.12 to 18.88 s), nothing on the large one (34.8 to 34.9 s), and the first fold paid the helper's compile.
- Measuring: an editor with no `.godot/editor/editor_script_doc_cache.res` runs its own walk of every script on a worker, plugin or no plugin, which made a measured open read 3 to 5 s slower and looked like a door in the row build. `tools/measure_editor_boot.gd` now opens a scene first, says `doc_walk=` and takes `-- no-doc-walk`, and `editor_boot_budget_test` pins that the first open compiles none of the click-only scripts.

### Fixed: disabling the plugin

- The Parameters dialog and its two sub-helpers each held the other; the dock now lets go of the loop as it is freed. A static thumbnail handler kept the UI builder alive and could call into a freed dock; it is cleared.
- The workspace and docs dock are queued for deletion again. The earlier change to `free()` answered orphans that exist only under `--editor --script`, whose main loop is not the tree an out-of-tree `queue_free` goes to. In a plain `--editor` run the count stays flat over four cycles (313 orphans, 21528 nodes), and `free()` raises an engine error when a disable comes from a button inside the workspace. About eight small objects per cycle remain.

### Fixed: run-time errors with two game instances, the undo icon after a theme switch, and the Events switch on a light editor

- Two copies of a game connecting in the same poll never began a new Run, so the second Run's errors were dropped as repeats of the first's (0 reports, now 2). Measured with two games failing every frame: two reports, editor frames 8 ms against 7 ms idle.
- The undo arrow, a flipped copy of the redo arrow, kept the old theme's colour after a live switch. The Events, Code and Both switch now darkens its accent on a light editor and follows a live switch.

### Changed: the README pictures come in a light and a dark version

- Every README picture now has a `-light` twin beside it under `docs/previews/` (the workspace, the Generated GDScript panel, the picker, the sheet rows, the type check, the autocomplete list and the colour squares), taken in the real editor on Godot's Light theme, and the README shows each through a `<picture>` element so GitHub serves the one that matches the reader's colour scheme. The dark pictures were retaken from the same script, so the pair has the same content.

### Changed: the README and doc pictures show the editor as it looks now

- Every picture under `docs/previews/` was retaken in the real editor in the Editor Native look: the workspace, the Generated GDScript panel beside the sheet, the picker, and the sheet rows on their own. Three new ones sit beside the colour values, type checking and autocomplete bullets in the README. The four pictures nothing referenced any more were removed.

### Fixed: a runtime error in a `.gd` sheet reaches its row

- An error in a running game found its row only for a `.tres` sheet with a debug compile, because the game itself announced the failure. A `.gd` sheet (the default format, whose file must stay byte-identical) and a hand-written script got nothing. The editor already receives every script error of a game it started, so the strip now reads those: nothing is added to the game, the file, the project settings or an export. The strip, the Output line, Jump to event and Explain are the same as before, for any open tab; a sheet edited since its file was written points at no row rather than the wrong one.
- A failure that arrives twice (the `.tres` debug compile's own announce and the editor's) is said once per file and line per run.
- Jump to event on a tab that was not in front walks outward from the line's innermost row (a statement inside a function has no row of its own) until it lands on one, as the Code panel's line click does.

### Fixed: the two interaction stalls - a drop that ended a drag, and a fold on a large sheet

Measured in the real editor (simulated mouse) on the juice pack (803 rows once opened for editing) and the 32-row boomer level, medians of three runs.

- A fold on a large sheet rebuilt every row when the row was a code card (\"Script block - 12 lines\"): a card says the same words folded and open, so it now folds in place like a group does (`can_redress_fold`). Clicking a card on the pack: a full rebuild of about 0.8 s before (first click, real editor), a reflow of about 16 ms now.
- A fold also measured every row of the sheet again. A fold keeps every row it did not re-dress, so each keeps its measurement and the sheet is only laid out again from the rows above (`ViewportRowMetrics.rebuild(kept)`); a changed canvas width keeps nothing. On a sheet of 1,800 rows (150 groups of 10 notes) one fold went from about 200 ms to about 27 ms, and Fold All / Collapse All are the same walk. `fold_reflow_test` compares the reflow with a full rebuild on twelve sheets (now also the juice pack, read as a page, and with both views settled so a lazily measured row on a large sheet is not mistaken for a difference).
- A rebuild of the rows stated the sheet's patterns twice: the file-level walk and the nearest-in-family loop were claimed, then thrown away by the registry's clear a few lines later, and the walk was paid for again by the first reader (the Objects rail, a tooltip, the first drawn row). One rebuild now runs the walk once, and the nearest-in-family (turret) claim, which no built view held until now, survives the build (`rebuild_claims_test`).
- The grammar's scanners answered every question by walking a line character by character: where the first top-level ` = ` is, where the first call opens, where an argument list splits. Every whole-file reading asks them of every line, and they are most of what a rebuild spends on a large sheet. A line with nothing to be careful about (no quote or bracket before the operator) is now answered by a string search, and the walk is kept for the rest (`scan_fast_paths_test` holds the two to each other over 2,000 real and awkward lines). The pattern facts of a file went from about 340 ms to about 65 ms on the pack, and are no longer read twice per rebuild with the same lines; the two code-line lists the sentence context reads a dozen times are read once per call.
- Together, on the pack: the drop that ends a drag went from about 3.0 s to about 0.9 s, and the second drop (the sheet is editable by then) from about 2.8 s to about 0.75 s; the next frame after a drop costs about 55 ms either way. The 32-row level is unchanged within the run-to-run spread (about 0.2-0.5 s). Rebuilding the 900 rows of the pack as an authored sheet after an event moved: 3.8 s before, 1.6 s now (loaded machine). What is left is the sentence reading of every event, which builds the words of all of them up front on a sheet this size; reusing those words across a drop needs the sheet-wide facts they were read with proved unchanged, which is not done yet.

### Fixed: the review of colour values, autocomplete, mistake checks and type checking

- A field was judged against whatever scene happened to be open, read from its root: a `$Score` that is a Label in another scene refused a value that is right in this sheet's. It is now read from the node that runs the sheet, and a scene that does not run it says nothing.
- Valid values that were refused: a `$Node` written inside a string, a longer name beginning with a node's name (`$Sprite2` beside `$Sprite`), a member of a script that answers names of its own (`_get`), `script`, and `self` on a sheet whose host is a project class (the stand-in now extends that class, so its members compile too). A member a node lacks is now found on an ordinary script in the editor, where it was never asked because a script that is not a tool cannot be instantiated.
- Godot's name for the stand-in script (`gdscript://-123.gd`) no longer reaches a sentence: `self` is said by its class.
- On a sheet opened from a `.gd`, the Variable dropdown of Set value, Compare and the like read \"No variables available\" and refused every value, because it asked the old table and not the variable rows.
- In the editor as it opens, a colour value still read in the older words (\"black at 50% opacity\", \"2, 0.5, 0\", `Color(\"#ff8000\")`) instead of as the board says, and the project setting that chooses how colours read changed nothing there. Every row now reads the project's choice. A line written by hand gets the square and the reading a verb's row gets, in actions and conditions, including `Color.RED.lerp(Color.BLUE, 0.5)`, whose inner colour was half translated and lost its square.
- The colour picker opened away from its square when the editor window was not at the top-left of the desktop: the anchor left out the window's own position.
- A completion list in an embedded dialog or popup measured its room against that small window and landed at its corner; it now hangs from the word, flips above it when there is no room below, and never covers it.
- `\"red\"`, `\"#ff8000\"` and `0xff8000ff` in a Color slot are values Godot converts, and no longer wear the amber state.
- Mistake checks: a local of a function read as a verb's property (`amount = 2` on a row that reads like Set Amount) was called a member the node lacks; a handler with no rows yet was not checked; a While was called endless while its body called a method of the node itself; a local named like a value its trigger hands it was missed.
- The doors the mistake checks were missing: Use the closest file on the row that declares it too, and Pick a file; Add the group to a node; Pick another node and Pick another action; a real menu for Pick an existing action; Add the missing value (by name when one is in scope, else the type's empty value) and Remove the extra one; Open the signal; Drop a handler's extra value (not offered when the body reads it) and Add it to the signal; Add: Subtract 1 from the variable and Make it an If on a While, and Stop after 1000 rounds on an event with a trigger or a condition, which keeps those and moves the loop one level in; Rename the local and Remove the local; Open the value; Show row on every Doctor entry. A door pressed after an edit finds the live row. The Doctor's missing-control finding now carries its row.
- `class_reference.gd` never starts the dump outside the editor's own binary, where the executable would be the game.
- `existing_project_edits_test` flaked because it trusted one look at the one file every test process shares; reproduced with a process writing that file beside it, and fixed.

### Added: mistake checks - nine things Godot only says when the game runs

- Nine mistakes that pass in silence today are now found on the sheet itself. A row that makes one wears the quiet row state and nothing is drawn inside it: amber for a warning, red for an error. Select the row and the help strip has the sentence and the doors; the Doctor lists every one in the project in a Mistake checks section, and the sentence a save prints counts them. The state is recomputed when a sheet opens and after each edit, never on a timer, and the project-wide facts it leans on are read once per session and dropped when the filesystem changes.
- Every sentence was checked against a real Godot 4.7 run and says only what the engine does. An action on a node of the wrong class (error: \"Invalid call. Nonexistent function\", only when a scene runs the sheet and names the node). A literal `res://` path nobody has (error: `preload` is a parse error and the script does not load, `load` prints errors and returns nothing; `user://` paths and paths built at run time are never judged). A group no scene and no script puts a node in (warning: the loop is silently empty). A `$Path` or `%Name` the scene lacks (warning: \"Node not found\" and the next line stops). A signal emitted or handled with the wrong number of values (error: parsing is fine, but when a handler is connected Godot prints \"Error calling from signal\" and the handler does not run; a handler with a default for the extra value is fine). A While nothing inside can change (warning: the game stops responding; quiet when the body calls a function of the sheet, awaits, breaks, or the condition calls something). A local hiding a sheet variable (warning: Godot's SHADOWED_VARIABLE, the change lands on the local) or a function parameter (error: the script does not load). A division or remainder by a literal zero (error for whole numbers, warning for a decimal, where Godot gives inf).
- A control the Input Map lacks now also colours the row that names it, with the same doors as the Doctor's existing finding (use the near name, add it, pick another).
- Doors on the strip and in the Doctor, each one undo step: use the near name (a group, a file, a node, a control), point the row at the scene's own node of the right class, stop a While after 1000 rounds (a counter local called `rounds`, one added each round, and the condition and-ed with `rounds < 1000`, all plain GDScript that reads back as rows), and \"It is added / spawned at run time\", which is remembered per sheet in the project setting `eventsheets/mistakes/known_at_run_time` rather than in the sheet file, so a `.gd` sheet gains not one byte.
- The corrected wording: an action description, two guides and the Object Pool page said Godot \"refuses\" to add (or reparent) a node inside a collision handler. A real run shows a 2D physics body or area added or reparented there makes Godot print \"Can't change this state while flushing queries\" and is still added and works; a plain node or a 3D node prints nothing. The descriptions now say that.
### Added: type checking - a field says when the kind of value is wrong, in plain words

- Godot's own analyzer now judges the KIND of every expression field. The stand-in script a field is checked inside declares each sheet variable with the type it was declared with and each sheet function with its real parameters and return type (written the way the compiler writes them, with a bare declaration for anything whose type the stand-in cannot spell), and the line the verb writes is compiled there. Text in a whole-number slot, a number compared with a text variable, the wrong count or kind of values to a sheet function, a number for a Vector2, text for a node: each is refused as you type, and a value that is fine (a whole number in a number slot, a fraction into a whole number, text where a StringName goes, an enum, a lambda, a local, a Variant call) says nothing.
- The finding is said as a sentence (\"score holds a whole number. \"ten\" is text. Write a number, or an expression that gives a number.\") with Godot's own message under it on a GODOT SAYS line of the help strip, the exact characters marked with a wavy underline in the field instead of the whole text turning red, and the reason beside OK naming the mistake. An error blocks OK and Enter; the check runs about 0.3 s after the last key, and a neighbouring field's change re-judges the value. One-click rewrites (remove the quotes, Vector2(5, 0), str(score), $Name) are offered as doors in the strip, never applied by themselves.
- A value Godot cannot type - the member of an untyped local - says \"Worked out while the game runs.\" as a note and is never called an error. A `$Node.member` the open scene's node does not have is named (\"Player has no jump_hieght. Did you mean jump_height?\"), read from the node's class and script; Godot's analyzer does not report an unknown property of an object, so this one answer is the editor's own and quotes nothing from Godot. A sheet whose stand-in cannot be written for a reason that is not the value's shows \"compile check paused\" beside OK instead of a red field.
- The sheet's inline value editor and the Properties bar run the same check through the same gate: the inline editor says what Enter would refuse under the field once typing pauses, and nothing is written.
- A wrong kind of value on a row that is already on the sheet now wears the quiet red state too, with the same sentence in the strip (Godot's words on its hover) and an entry in the Doctor, with the doors the field has: the one-click rewrite, Open the parameters, Go to the variable. Every line of the sheet is compiled in one small stand-in script, each line once per set of the sheet's types, so an edit costs about 10 ms on the biggest pack and Godot prints a line's refusal in the Output once rather than once an edit. A false-positive sweep over all 222 shipped scripts finds nothing.
- Not done: Godot's warnings (NARROWING_CONVERSION and the rest) are not reported while a script is only loaded, so neither the field nor the row can show them, and a value that only narrows stays quiet. The row's amber state for a warning is therefore not reachable from this check.

### Added: colour values lead with a square

- Every colour value on the sheet - a variable's default, an action's or a condition's colour parameter - now leads with a small square of its colour at text height, drawn as part of the value's own text run (never a widget in a row). Each colour parameter gets its own square; a see-through colour sits on a small checkerboard, a colour brighter than the screen can show is drawn held at 1 with a corner mark, and hovering the square or the value opens a card saying what the colour is and anything the reader should know about how it is written. Every form is read: all of Godot's named colours, `Color(\"#hex\")` in any case or length, `Color(\"name\")`, `Color(` three or four numbers `)`, `Color.html`, `Color.hex`, `Color8`, `Color.from_rgba8`, `Color.from_hsv`, `Color.from_ok_hsl`, `Color(base, alpha)`, HDR values, and expressions the editor can fold from constants (`Color.RED.lerp(Color.BLUE, 0.5)`). A value only the running game knows (a variable, a node's colour) gets no square and says so on hover.
- Clicking the square on any condition or action, or pressing Enter on the selected colour value, opens Godot's ColorPicker anchored under it with no parameters window; double-clicking the words still opens the window. The row's square and words follow the picker while it is dragged, closing it (or Enter) keeps the colour as one undo step, and Esc puts the old colour back and writes nothing. The picker opens in the value's own mode (HSV, OKHSL, or RGB with Intensity for an overbright colour).
- A pick is written back in the form the value was typed in, by one writer shared by variable rows and parameters: the same call, argument count, hex length, case and \"#\". A named colour picked to an unnamed one is written as `Color(\"#hex\")` and the picker and hover say so. Colour values read as written by default (`Color(\"#ff8000\")` reads `#ff8000`, `Color.ORANGE` reads `orange`), which moves variable rows off uppercase hex.
- New project setting `eventsheets/editor/colour_values_read_as` (As written, Hex, RGB 0-255, RGB 0-1, HSV, Name) and a Read Colours As submenu on the square's right-click menu change the words a row says and never the file; a form that cannot say the colour (hex and 0-255 stop at 1) falls back to as written, and a slot with no alpha never reads or picks one. The same menu has Copy As for every form that can say the colour (clipboard only, no undo step).
- The parameters dialog shows Godot's colour button beside a text field: an expression is kept byte for byte instead of being overwritten with white, the button is hatched for a value only the game can work out, and text that is not a colour is said so under the field with the forms a Color slot takes. A bare `#ff8000` or `orange` typed in a Color slot is written as `Color(\"...\")`. Text that is not a colour in a Color slot puts the row in the quiet amber state, and the help strip says what the slot takes.
- Fixed: a hex-string colour variable could draw a white square, and a parameter swatch was only found for the four-number form. One colour reader now serves rows, variables, the picker and the dialog.
### Added: autocomplete in every expression field completes like Godot's script editor

- The list shows an icon for what each entry is, its name, its type on the right in Godot's spelling, and one line under the list saying what the highlighted entry is. It opens on the first letter, on a dot, on a quote and on Ctrl+Space, in the parameters dialog and in the inline value editor on the canvas, and the order is the sheet's names, the event's locals, the host's members, then the engine's globals.
- Dots follow the type through chains (`$Sprite.sprite_frames.`), built-in values (`velocity.`, Vector2, Color, String, Rect2, Array), `self.`, singletons (`Input.`, `Time.`), class statics and enum values (`Vector2.ZERO`, `Node.PROCESS_MODE_ALWAYS`), `%Unique.` nodes, constructor calls and method results. The members come from ClassDB and from the class reference the running engine publishes about itself (read once in the background into the project's user folder, never typed out by hand); a step with no type (an untyped variable) offers nothing and says \"worked out while the game runs\".
- Inside quotes the list is the names the call wants, decided by what the engine says the argument is: Input Map actions, the animations of the receiver's own SpriteFrames or AnimationPlayer, groups (scene and `add_to_group` in scripts, marked \"code only\" when only code adds them) and scene and resource paths for `load`, `preload` and `change_scene_to_file`.
- Member descriptions come from the engine's class reference, first sentence, translated where the editor's language has it, with its credit under the list. Global enum values (`KEY_SPACE`) appear with their enum and number.
- The signature hint follows the receiver (`$Sprite.play(` is AnimatedSprite2D's, `velocity.move_toward(` is Vector2's), shows defaults as Godot spells them and marks the argument under the caret. The inline editor has it too.
- An event's locals and the values its trigger hands in (`delta`, `body`) are offered inside that event and its sub-events, and no other event's.
- Fixed: the inline editor asked with the whole text instead of the text before the caret, so a caret in the middle of an expression completed its last word. Accepting replaces only the word under the caret.
- Fixed: a method with arguments is inserted with the caret between its parentheses.

### Fixed: the review of the retained row paint and the fold reflow

- A state row's live token kept the seconds it was recorded with while the game ran, because the renderer asks the running game at draw time and the seconds change with no rebuild. A colour changed in place on a sheet's look stayed on the rows for the same reason. Both are in a row's paint signature now.
- While a drag closed the gap a row left, the lifted row and its veil were drawn over the row sliding into its place instead of under it. A row a drag changes is recorded on its own item, so it keeps its place in the row order.
- A canvas that grew or shrank (an event opening its add lines) recorded every visible row again. Only the canvas' width reaches a row's paint.
- One frame with every row on it (zooming out while scrolled deep asks for all of them) left that many nodes behind for good. The pool of spare row items is bounded, and an offset past the end of the rows asks for the last row.
- Folding an event whose head is a split ternary opened again on the next rebuild, and before the reflow it did nothing at all. The head takes its fold from the same place every other row does.

### Fixed: the review of the save checks and the commit gate

- A failure in a game started from the editor reached the sheet only after pressing Continue in the debugger, and never if the reader pressed Stop: the debug compile's reporter sent it deferred, and the game stops at the error first. It is said at once now, so the strip, Jump to event and the tab focus work while the game is stopped.
- The compile check refused values that are good where their verb writes them, in the dialog and now in the inline editor and the Properties bar too: a test on the element a sibling field names (Filter's own `x > 0` could not close its window), the statement a body runs, a list between brackets, the name a lambda binds. A value that does not compile by itself is asked again in the verb's template, with the sibling names it declares.
- A local, a loop's counter or a function's parameter named in a variable field was refused as \"not a variable\", which locked the dialog of a row that writes one.
- The completion popup of a code box was a sliver under one line of text, cut off by the box, and its new detail pushed the name out of the line. The box makes room while suggestions are open, and the detail is shortened to fit behind the whole name.
- A save counted the engine marks of the file it had just replaced, so a fixed sheet could read \"1 row(s) need attention\"; a fix saved inside the same second as the broken file was answered from the cache with the old errors; an answer overtaken by a newer save was still said; and an answer that landed while another tab was up was dropped. The write clears the old answer, the cache forgets the file, the newer save wins, and the tab says it when it comes back.

### Added: budgets for what a pointer on the sheet may cost

- `sheet_interaction_budget_test` pins what a drag move, a fold, a hover crossing and the per-frame paint check may touch and how long they may take, at three times the median measured on the 800-row pack.

### Fixed: a look that shows add lines on hover no longer relays out the screen on every hover

- Moving the pointer between two events grew one and shrank the other, and the re-measure walked every row and discarded every cached layout. Only the two rows are looked at now, the rows below shift only while the gain and the loss have not cancelled out, and only the layouts of the rows that moved are dropped. The hover flag moves between two rows instead of walking all of them. The metrics generation renews on any change of height. Pinned by `add_lines_incremental_test`, which walks hovers and selections and compares the metrics with a fresh measure at every step.

### Changed: a hover, a keystroke or a selection repaints the row it touched, not every visible row

- Every visible row now lives on a retained canvas item (`EventSheetRetainedPaintItem`, a pooled `Node2D`: no layout, no input, no theme) that is recorded again only when the row's paint signature changes (the row's plain fields, hover, selection and the edit in progress, the layout's identity, and the frame's zoom, size, look and font). The items draw behind the canvas's own paint, so the stacking is unchanged: background and fills, the rows, then the event cards, selection frames, brackets and ghost. A row a drag only moves is repositioned, not repainted; a row the drag changes is still drawn inline. Screenshots of the real editor are pixel-identical to the old paint at rest, on hover, on selection and in a held drag, in the dark and the light look. `EVENTFORGE_NO_RETAINED_PAINT=1` turns it off. Measured on boomer_level, 240 frames, median of 3: hover frame p90 40 -> 8 ms, drag frame median 28 -> 11 ms (p90 97 -> 18 ms); on the juice pack, drag p90 63 -> 15 ms. Pinned by `retained_row_paint_test`.

### Fixed: folding a row no longer rebuilds every row of the sheet

- A fold changes what the rows show, not what they are. Folding or unfolding now flattens the rows already built and re-dresses the words of the rows whose fold flipped (a folded group names what it holds, a folded region counts its events) with the same builders a full build uses. A row of a kind that wears its fold state in other words (an enum's sentence, a moment's steps, a variable section) still goes through the full rebuild, so the answer is always the one a rebuild gives. Measured in the real editor, the longest frame of a fold click: boomer_level (32 rows) 360 - 420 ms -> 45 - 70 ms; juice pack (803 rows) 6.8 s -> 0.9 s. Pinned by `fold_reflow_test`, which folds and unfolds rows of real sheets both ways and compares every row's identity, indent, numbers, height, words and selection.

### Fixed: a drag slide asks for one draw a frame, not two

- The pointer move that drives a drag queues a redraw and the per-frame slide step queued a second one that ran after it, so a slide drew the whole canvas twice in a frame. The slide is a function of the clock, so a frame that has already drawn skips the slide's own redraw while it is still moving; the frame the slide stops is never skipped, so the last picture drawn is the rest position. 327 -> 241 canvas draws per 240 drag frames. Pinned by `drag_redraw_coalesce_test`.

### Fixed: a drag no longer relays out every visible row on each move

- The layout cache key carried the whole drag signature (target row, drop mode, ACE target lane and slot) in EVERY row's key, so each change of the drop target missed the cache for every visible row. Only the row the drop would land on and the row an ACE drop would land in read the drag state, so only those two carry it now; every other row's key holds just whether a drag is live. Measured in the real editor on the boomer_level showcase (32 rows), 240 drag frames: layout misses 1,215 -> 254. Pinned by `layout_drag_key_test`.

### Fixed: the Doctor no longer warns about adding a node from `_physics_process`

- The spawning check \"adds a node to the tree while the physics server is busy\" also fired on rows under On Physics Process (it filed Starfall, Platformer Shooter and Draw Lab). A real run shows Godot adds a body, an area or a plain node from `_physics_process` without a word, so that trigger is no longer checked. The collision callbacks (body and area entered and exited) still are: there a 2D body or area added makes Godot print \"Can't change this state while flushing queries\" (the node is still added and works), while a plain node or any 3D node prints nothing. The sentence now says exactly that instead of claiming Godot refuses the add, and has fresh translations in all nine files. The three showcases are back to their plain, readable spawn rows.

### Fixed: a sheet whose compile check cannot run no longer paints every expression red

- When a sheet variable shadows a member of the host class, the compile check cannot run; the parameter window used to colour every expression field red and say \"fix X first\" for the probe's fault. It now leaves the fields alone and says \"compile check paused\" beside Done (the reason on hover). A save that finds both an engine error and compiler warnings keeps the warning count on the status line.

### Fixed: a save no longer drops what it learned about the file it wrote, and runtime errors find their row without the Code panel

- After a save, the engine's own verdict on the whole script the sheet wrote is read on a worker thread (a second Godot parses the file, about 0.4 s, cached by file time, so the save itself is not slowed). A failure no row's lint explained - a template's output, a pasted block - now marks the row that wrote the failing line (the red marker, whose hover is the engine's sentence) and the status line says the engine's words with the line. The lint's generic \"does not compile\" on a row is replaced by the engine's reason when it names the same row.
- A sheet that is a .gd had its parse check only on open; the same check now re-runs after every save and refreshes the row marks and the head bar's count.
- The compiler's warnings (it always returned them; only the MCP server read them) now set the quiet amber state on the event they belong to (`warning_rows` beside the unchanged `warnings` list, emitted bytes untouched), are counted on the status line after a save, and are listed in the Doctor under Compiler.
- A runtime error from the running game is mapped to its row for ANY open sheet, with the Code panel closed: the tab whose script the error names is found, its source map is computed on demand (kept while the file is unchanged), and Jump to event brings that tab forward. A sheet edited since its file was written points at no row rather than the wrong one. The game still reports errors only through the debug build's announce, so this works for a game started from the editor; nothing is added to emitted code.

### Fixed: a health check or a Doctor run no longer wipes the open sheet's pattern claims

- The sheet health card and the Doctor's pattern smells cleared the pattern registry of the sheet they were asked about and re-claimed for their own read. On the OPEN sheet that threw away the claims its rows made while their words were built (some are made only by the row build), so the Project bar's \"N patterns\", an object's \"could adopt\" and the Manual's \"Patterns using this\" went wrong until the next rebuild. Both now take a snapshot first and put it back after their read.

### Fixed: the remembered lifts in .godot/ no longer grow without bound

- An entry for a renamed or deleted .gd was never removed. The first use of the lift cache in a session now sweeps the folder: entries whose source file is gone, entries nothing names (an index of path digests is kept beside them), stale tmp files, and then the oldest entries beyond 1,000 entries or 64 MB. Measured by opening all 181 shipped pack .gd files with the cache on: 9.7 MB in all, 53 KB on average, 623 KB the largest, so the caps hold about six whole shipped sets and never evict a live project. With the cache off nothing is written or removed.

### Fixed: a row that got its words as it came into view was drawn into a height that did not fit it

- Since rows past the first screenful build their words lazily (every shipped pack, not only sheets over 1,500 rows), their height was a guess from the line count, which knows nothing of a cell that wraps or of the add lines a resting or inert row hides - and a row's height was never measured again once its words arrived. In a real editor at 760 px the fps controller pack drew \"capture_mouse_on_re\" cut off under the next row and long actions clipped mid-sentence; measured headless at a 640 px canvas, 65 of its 74 lazy rows had a guessed height that was not their built one. The rows a draw is about to paint are now measured again with their words and the rows below move by what they gained (`settle_built_rows`), and the sheet-as-one-picture export measures every row before it reads the total height.
- Building a row's words is also when its readings claim the patterns they recognise, so the counts over the whole sheet (the Project bar's \"N patterns\", an object's \"could adopt\", the Manual's \"Patterns using this\", the coverage chip's click-through walk) answered from however far the reader had scrolled - 4 claims of the fps controller pack's 12. The rest of the rows now get their words a few milliseconds per frame while the editor is idle (`viewport_word_filler.gd`), after which every row is measured again with the view kept where it was; the click-through walk builds them at once.
- A hover that re-measured a row under an add-lines-on-hover look moved the tops without moving the layout's generation, so the held margin brackets went on being drawn at the old tops. Every change of the tops now moves it.
- Smaller readers of words that skipped the build: a row's spoken name (the screen reader and Speak This Row) left out sub-events not drawn yet, the hover's other-uses highlight missed uses in rows not drawn yet, and finding the rows behind a selection for Copy as text built the words of every row of the sheet to answer it. Pinned in `lazy_row_heights_test`.

### Fixed: a remembered lift answered from a stale scene and named a file in .godot/ as its own

- What the project's scenes say about a file is read by its lift (a signal handler a .tscn wires lifts as a trigger with no connect line in the text; the nodes wearing a shader material; the class of each node a light row names), and none of it is in the file's text, so the lifted-sheet memory handed back what an OLD scene said after the scene changed - across editor restarts, for as long as the .gd itself stayed unchanged. The byte check cannot see it, because those readings emit nothing. The key now carries a digest of the same three scene answers the lift asks for.
- A remembered sheet came back with the entry's path in `resource_path` (and its parts' `<entry>::<id>`), where a fresh lift has none, so the readers that ask resource_path (the secret marks are keyed by it, an unnamed compile writes beside it) were pointed into `.godot/eventsheets_lift_cache/`. The paths are taken off on the read. A deep compare of every stored property of a fresh lift and a remembered one over 11 shipped files found nothing else apart (the event uids, which every lift rolls fresh).
- The code stamp is taken once per session; a filesystem change (a pack provider edited and saved) now drops it so the next open stamps again. Pinned in `lift_cache_test`.

### Fixed: a state machine flipping every frame could lay the sheet out on every flip

- The live state row lays the sheet out at most once per 250 ms window, but the window was stamped when a layout STARTED, so a layout slower than the window (a big sheet, a loaded machine) left no window after it and every flip laid the sheet out again. The window now opens when the layout ends. `states_project_wide_test` pins it, and no longer lets the once-per-session cold start of the live-values panel and the state trail (215 ms of the 250 ms window measured cold, all of it under a parallel run) decide whether its three flips land inside the window - the red its \"three more flips\" check went in a loaded parallel run. The file also declares itself PARALLEL_UNSAFE, so its real-time window is measured in the serial tail.

### Fixed: five checks that already existed in the parameter editors were skipped, hidden or never run

- The Edit Parameter dialog's OK and Apply & Add Another now refuse a required value left blank (or a name that is not a variable) - the same error the reason beside OK was already showing - and say why there. Warning-level notes (a literal of the wrong kind) still never block.
- When the compile-check probe cannot run for a sheet (a sheet variable named like a member of the host class), the dialog says so: \"compile check paused\" beside OK, the full reason (which variable, which class) on hover and under an expression field, instead of every field silently looking checked.
- A vector's axis boxes get the same structural and compile check an expression field gets: a bad axis tints red as it is typed and blocks OK, and a blank axis (which wrote `Vector2(, 3)`) is refused.
- The sheet's inline value editor and the Properties bar ask the dialog's own gate (`blocking_problem_for`) before writing: a refused value keeps its text, shows the reason under the field or on the status line, writes nothing, and Esc still cancels.
- A code box's completion popup shows each suggestion's type or description after its name (\"hp    int\"), as the sheet's other completion popup does; the inserted text is still the exact name.
### Changed: the first sheet open compiles about 37 fewer scripts, and the first-run welcome no longer compiles the picker and the Doctor

- The block-kind scan followed a pack script's `extends` one hop and loaded anything that extended another script, so the vector shapes and post effects (28 scripts, about 5,500 lines) were compiled on every editor build only to learn they are not block kinds. It now follows the chain by reading text down to an engine class (`tests/lift_perf_test.gd` pins a shape false and the demo note block true).
- The refactor menu named Wrap, Inline and Duplicate as Variant as classes, which compiled them and the snippet and paste readers behind them when the context menus were built; they load by path where a gesture asks. Six readers only an action reaches (shader dials, file arguments, script members, sheet descriptions and prose, one-line function folding) load by path too.
- The welcome window's tour, preferences and docs buttons named the public API class, which compiled the picker, the parameters dialog and the whole Doctor (about 20,000 lines) on a project's first open. They reach it by path now.
- Findings: the scripts loaded by the end of a first open fall from 444 to 406 (186,000 to 178,000 lines) in a bare process, and from 479 to 442 in the real editor. Wall time moved less than the machine's noise (small sheet median 21.9 s -> 21.4 s), because the other 94% of the compiled code is called during the open.
- `tests/cold_boot_probe.gd` now keeps these scripts, and everything the welcome window would pull in, out of the loaded set.

### Fixed: an Else row's \"neither of N\" note stayed blank until the row was scrolled to

- With words built as rows come into view, an Else row that had not been drawn yet had no span to write its \"neither of N\" note into when the rows were numbered, so the note read blank (106 rows across the shipped packs) until it was scrolled to. Numbering now builds the words of an Else row first. Found by the reading gate: a dump of every shipped sheet's readings with the old eager limit and with the new one differed in exactly those cells, and is byte-identical (44,728 readings, same sha) with this fix. Pinned in `lazy_span_window_test`.

### Changed: opening an unchanged .gd again reads its lifted sheet back instead of lifting it again

- The lift (every function reverse-matched against the vocabulary, the whole sheet recompiled to verify it) is linear in the file and was paid in full on every open. `addons/eventforge/importer/lift_cache.gd` now keeps the lifted sheet of a file in `.godot/eventsheets_lift_cache/` as a binary resource, one per source file, and the open job reads it back when the key still holds: the md5 of the source text, the compiler version, the engine, the name and modified time of every script under `addons/eventforge/`, `addons/eventsheet/ace/` and `eventsheet_addons/` (the importer, the lifter, the compiler and the vocabulary), and the number of ACEs the vocabulary holds. One changed byte, a bumped version, an edited lifter or a pack that adds a verb is a fresh lift. A file changed outside the editor has another md5, so it lifts again.
- THE LOSSLESS CONTRACT IS CHECKED ON EVERY READ: the remembered sheet must re-emit the source byte for byte or it is deleted, not trusted again this session, and the file is lifted fresh. A corrupt, half-written or foreign entry reads as a miss, and a lift cut short by \"Show as code instead\" is never remembered.
- Measured on the 3,580-line pathfinding pack, worker stage of an open in fresh headless processes, median of 5 and 6: lift and verify 10,608 ms (10,217 - 11,205) -> remembered 1,314 ms (1,241 - 1,349), which includes the byte check (about 0.75 s) and the engine's own compile check. The sheet reads back in about 66 ms. In the real editor the lift runs on a worker beside the main thread's own work, so the end-to-end open moves less than the stage does and the figures there were too noisy to claim (open-to-lifted 31.5 - 48.8 s on a loaded machine, session-restore tabs opening beside it). Pinned by `lift_cache_test` (a second open starts no lift; a changed byte, a changed compiler version, a changed vocabulary and an edited lifter each lift again; corrupt, foreign and keyless entries are ignored; an entry that does not re-emit its source is discarded; 600 ms wall on the remembered stage of a 346-row pack, measured 174 ms). The test runner turns the memory off for the suite, so no test is handed another run's lift.

### Fixed: three small leftovers

- A hand-written `global_position = Vector2(10, 20)` (or any property the class the script extends really has: `position`, `rotation`, a Control's `size`, a method, a signal, a constant, a variable an inherited script class declares) no longer shows the red \"is not a variable of System\" note on its row, in the Set value dialog's field note or in the Doctor's unknown-variable finding. A made-up name stays red, and a behavior or autoload sheet (a plain Node) still checks against Node only.
- Importing the source engine's `angle(...)` now writes degrees from point 1 to point 2, the unit its Angle property and rotation rows already use: `angle(x1, y1, x2, y2)` becomes `rad_to_deg(Vector2(x1, y1).angle_to_point(Vector2(x2, y2)))` and the object form `angle(a, b)` becomes `rad_to_deg(a.global_position.angle_to_point(b.global_position))`. It used to write `a.get_angle_to(b.global_position)`, which is radians and relative to the node's own rotation. The 4-number form was flagged before and now translates.
- A Spawn A Copy (3D) row inside a physics or collision callback is now offered the safe spawn row too, swapping to Spawn A Copy Safely (3D) in the world, exactly as the 2D row already was.

### Changed: rows get their words as they come into view on every shipped pack, not only on huge sheets

- `EAGER_SPAN_LIMIT` falls from 1,500 to 100. The sentence reading of every row used to be built up front for any sheet of up to 1,500 rows, which is every shipped pack (the limit sat 8 rows above the biggest), although only a screenful is ever drawn. Rows now build their words when a layout, hit-test, selection, search or fold reaches them. The windows and exports that read a row's text without laying it out (the bookmarks list, the sheet as text and Markdown export, Copy as text, an error's address, the picker's landing row) ask for the words first through the new `ensure_words_in`.
- Measured on the 1,492-row pathfinding pack in fresh processes, median of 3: a row rebuild 13,731 ms (12,251 - 14,702) -> 9,374 ms (8,974 - 9,558), and the first open's row build 17,018 ms (16,780 - 19,965) -> 13,969 ms (13,836 - 14,489). Pinned by `lazy_span_window_test` (a sheet over the limit leaves event rows unbuilt; a lazy row's precomputed line count equals its built spans' for every event of a real pack; selecting, searching and `ensure_words_in` build words; a sheet under the limit still builds all) beside the existing rebuild budget.
- Driven in the real editor on pathfinding: 401 of 1,038 event rows have words at open, a scroll to row 750 builds the window as it is drawn, click selects, hover answers, Find returns 116 hits and go-to lands on the last, a drag lifts a row, the minimap reads all 1,516 rows, and Copy as text of rows never laid out lists their conditions and actions.
### Fixed: scrolling a long sheet no longer walks every row of it on every frame

- Each draw worked out the margin brackets (one dictionary per row of the whole sheet) and the variable-folder runs (two more walks of every row). They are held now, keyed to the rows' generation, the layout, the width, the font, the zoom and the look's colours, so a frame that changed none of those reuses them; a rebuild (an edit, a fold, a diagnostics change), a resize, a zoom or a look change computes them again.
- Measured in the real editor on the 1,516-row pathfinding pack, 400 scroll frames per run, median of the per-run `_draw` medians over 3 fresh processes: 44.6 ms (38.4 - 56.2) -> 24.4 ms (21.0 - 24.5). Pinned by `group_memo_test` with call counters (30 unchanged asks compute once; a new width, a rebuild, a fold, a zoom and a look change each compute again).
### Fixed: saving a sheet with nothing to report no longer rebuilds every row

- `set_row_diagnostics` rebuilt the whole canvas on every save, even when the problem list was empty and had been empty before. It now rebuilds only when the list changed (a flag appears, moves, changes its words or clears); an equal list changes nothing on screen, because rows built since the last list already carry their marks.
- Measured on the 1,492-row pathfinding pack in fresh processes, median of 3: `_run_diagnostics` after a save 21,095 ms (14,388 - 22,397) -> 6,659 ms (6,219 - 6,659) the first call of a session, and 12,439 ms (9,260 - 14,367) -> 797 ms (745 - 824) every call after it, with the row builds per call going 1 -> 0. The machine was shared and loaded, so read the before figures as a band; the 13 s rebuild is gone and the ~0.8 s that remains is the analysis. Pinned by `diagnostics_repaint_test` (row builds unchanged across a clean `_run_diagnostics`, an equal empty list and an equal non-empty list; a changed list still rebuilds; a 600 ms wall on a 346-row pack, measured 178 ms).
### Fixed: a sheet that saves no longer compiles the Project Doctor to draw its \"saved\" badge

- The first row build of any sheet whose source mentions `save_` asked the Project Doctor which names the Save System keeps, and loading the Doctor is a multi-second GDScript compile. The question is now answered by `addons/eventforge/save_key_scan.gd`, a small file with no dependencies that the Doctor itself delegates `save_key_usage` to, so a badge and a finding still read the same text the same way.
- Measured on the health pack in a fresh process, median of 3: `saved_names` 6,136 ms (5,548 - 6,662) -> 9 ms (9 - 12), and the Doctor is no longer in the loaded set after the sheet opens. Pinned by `save_names_badge_test` (60 ms wall, value parity with the Doctor, the badge's source never names the Doctor) and by `cold_boot_probe.gd`, which now also opens the health pack and lists `project_doctor.gd` as compiled if it ever is.

### Fixed: the world twins read like the lines they write, and the place words stay on nodes

- A hand-written `global_position = global_position.move_toward(...)`, `.clamp(...)` or `wrapf(...)` line on this node reads in its behaviour's words again (\"Move To\", \"Bound To\", \"Wrap\") with the object it belongs to, instead of \"System\" and the raw template; a picked world twin row reads the same. A twin that moves ANOTHER node (`host.global_position = host.global_position.move_toward(...)`, as the Drag And Drop and Home Leash packs write it) reads \"host | Set world position to ...\", as it did before the twins existed.
- Set Position To Another Object (2D) no longer claims a hand-written `global_position = marker.global_position`: its 3D twin writes the same line, so the 2D row was landing on 3D scripts and reading \"System\". It stays a picker row.
- A hand-written `crate.global_position = spawn.global_position` reads \"Set world position to spawn\" (a `position` copy still reads \"Set position to\").
- A rectangle, a box or an input event has a `position` too: `area.position.x = 0` on a variable the sheet declares as one of those keeps \"Set position.x\" and gets no parent-node hover. The hover names the parent when the node path says it: `$Player/Sword.position.y` says \"measured from the parent node, Player\".
- A picked Set Control Size reads \"Set size to (100, 100)\" on its own control, as a typed `size = ...` does.
- An imported Move At Angle adds the step to `global_position` (the angle is a direction in the level) instead of Move By, which added it in the parent's axes.

### Added: the world-space twins, the place readers and the Control rows

- Eight verbs that wrote the node's place inside its parent where the thing they work with is a place in the level are deprecated (hidden from the picker, still compiling and reading byte for byte, their descriptions and hover pointing at the twin): Spawn A Copy Safely and its 3D twin (`SpawnNewCopyDeferred`, `SpawnNewCopyDeferred3D`), Wrap Inside The Screen (`WrapInsideScreen`), Wrap Around Layout Horizontally / Vertically (`WrapAroundLayoutX`, `WrapAroundLayoutY`), Bound To Layout (`BoundToLayout`), Move Toward Position (`GlideToward`) and Apply Pushes (`ApplyPushes`). Their twins keep the same names and sentences and write `global_position`: `SpawnNewCopyDeferredWorld`, `SpawnNewCopyDeferredWorld3D`, `WrapInsideScreenWorld`, `WrapAroundLayoutXWorld`, `WrapAroundLayoutYWorld`, `BoundToLayoutWorld`, `GlideTowardWorld`, `ApplyPushesWorld`. No shipped id or template moves, and no stored row is rewritten.
- Set World Position (`SetWorldPosition2D`, `SetWorldPosition3D`) reads \"Set world position to ...\"; the local Set Position rows stay and now say in their description that they measure from the parent. Set Position To Another Object gets its 2D twin (`SetPositionToObject2D`), and both read \"Set world position to ...\".
- The place read back: Get X / Get Y (and Z in 3D) for the place inside the parent, Get World X / Y / Z for the place in the level, and Get World Position, in 2D and 3D (`GetPositionX2D` ... `GetWorldPosition3D`). Place Of stays as it was.
- UI Controls get Set Control Position (relative to the parent box), Set Control World Position and Set Control Size, and Set Anchors Preset now offers the whole LayoutPreset enum (the four missing presets are added).
- Spawn Scene In The World (`SpawnSceneInWorld`) spawns a scene file into the current scene at a place in the level.
- Readings that did not say their space now do: Move In Direction (3D) says \"in world space\", Move By (3D) says \"along its own axes\", Move Cursor To says \"in screen pixels\", and Spawn Scene At / Spawn Scene (Full) say \"as a child of this node\" (Spawn Scene As says \"placed from its parent\"), because the copy is measured from the spawner. A tween on `global_position` reads \"world position\". Words only: no template moves.
- The axis hover is decided from the row's own data (the member path it writes) and not from the words on the row, so a script variable called X or Y gets no hint. The importer's `distance()` and `angle()` between two objects now measure `global_position`, like the imported X and Y. All new words are in all nine translation files.

### Changed: a write to a node's place tells local from world

- `$Tree.position.y = -8` reads \"Tree | Set Y to -8\" and `position.x = 5` reads \"Set X to 5\" (Z too, for 3D nodes; Controls read the same), instead of \"Set position.y to ...\". A place in the world reads \"Set world X / Y / Z\" for `global_position.x/.y/.z` and \"Set world position to ...\" for the whole `global_position`. A whole `position` and every other member keep the words they had. The words live in a small table of their own in the reading code.
- Hovering one of these cells adds a line saying the axis is relative to the parent node and naming the world version (or the reverse for a world axis). New words are in all nine translation files. Readings only: no ace id, template or file byte moves.
- Imported X and Y are world coordinates, so the importer now maps them to `global_position`: `Player.X` in an expression becomes `$Player.global_position.x`, Set Position writes `global_position = Vector2(...)` through the plain property row (the frozen Set Position template is untouched), and Set X / Set Y rows, which had no entry, write `global_position.x` / `.y`.
- Set Position (3D) and Get Position (3D) no longer claim a \"world position\": both work on the position relative to the parent. Descriptions only.

### Changed: Editor Native closes the event alignment board

- A line's words stand where the board puts them: 118 px into the block in the conditions lane and 154 px into the actions lane (they stood at 145 and 171), the object's name 26 px in (it stood at 31) after the bare icon, and the divider between the lanes 6 px further right at the board's width.
- Quoted text reads in the normal text colour like every other value (it was orange), and a condition or action line is drawn in one weight: the names and values inside it (\"Set magnitude to\", \"exponent < 3\") are no longer bold or brighter. Classic Sheet keeps its tints and emphasis.
- A function's parameter lines sit on a band a shade darker than a condition line's strip, with no rule under them and no cell round their words, and its head leads with the trigger arrow in the words' ink instead of the function icon.
- An inverted condition's cross is drawn at the board's size, and a local's value stands the typed name's own 7 px gap after its name.

### Fixed: tabs, the head's Add action, the Doctor's numbers and capitalised names

- Switching back to a tab kept without a rebuild no longer answers with the last sheet's numbering: Go to Event, the status bar's event N of M, Find and the repeated-rows numbers read the tab's own branches again.
- A function head's \"+ Add action\" adds its blank first step and the picked action as one edit: closing the picker without a pick leaves the function untouched (it left an empty event and an undo step that added nothing).
- The Doctor reads each sheet's margin numbers once per run instead of building a throwaway canvas for every section that names an event.
- A line about a node named by its path (`$Body`) only raises a sentence's opening word: a line that opens on a name (`apply_impulse(...)`) keeps it as written.
- The comment rewrites of the free spot, pooled nodes and world look runtime scripts now live in their pack builders, so a rebuild keeps them.

### Changed: Editor Native lands the event alignment board's last measurements

- A write to the behaviour's own variable (`Set magnitude`, `Add 1 to tier`, a Set value on a sheet variable) reads with the behaviour's name as its object (\"Big Numbers | Set magnitude to ...\"): the pack's picker name when a pack is being read, else the category the file's own header declares. The tests beside it keep reading with System, and a sheet with no category reads as it did. A reading only; Classic Sheet reads the same object.
- A `z_index` write reads with the property as written: `$House3x3.z_index = 4` is \"House3x3 | Set z_index to 4\" (it read \"Set Z order to 4\", or \"set Z order\" under the class), through the shared grammar, with no shipped ace id or template renamed.
- Values read in the normal text colour in Editor Native (numbers and expressions had a green tint); Classic Sheet keeps its tints.
- A function's parameters are written type then bold name (\"float value\") with the type in the muted code face, under the trigger, and the resting \"+ Add parameter\" line is gone: the door is offered while the head is pointed at or selected, like the add-condition door. A local reads the same way on the canvas, its mark on a tile at its block's edge plus 10, with no cell round its type, name or value.
- The trigger arrow no longer reserves a 16 px slot on every condition line: an object icon stands at the lane's own padding (8 px into the block), a trigger's arrow leads its line and its icon stands 17 px after it, and an action's icon stands 8 px into its lane. An Else line names System with no icon.
- A group's number is drawn once in the margin (it drew twice, one over the other, as \"11\").

### Fixed: a dialog bigger than its room keeps its text full size and scrolls its body

- A dialog or window whose content is taller or wider than the editor window or screen it opens in is no longer scaled down (the Welcome dialog in a small editor drew its words at a few pixels). It is clamped to the room, its body sits in a scroll with a scroll bar, and its title bar and button row stay put and in reach; a dialog that fits is left exactly as it was, and one opened again in a bigger room gets its whole content back. The scaling path and its pins are gone.

### Changed: Editor Native draws the event alignment board's sheet (neutral lanes, bare margin, one slot per level)

- The lanes are the board's neutral greys, derived from the editor's own base colour so they follow the user's Godot theme (dark `#3a3a3a` condition line, `#323232` rest, `#363636` actions; light `#ffffff`, `#f0f0f0`, `#f7f7f7` at the default editor themes), with a 1 px lane divider, the board's canvas, and object names in the board's blue (`#8ec3f2` dark, `#124175` light) with no tile behind the icon. The starter theme is rebuilt from the same rule, and the condition cell's quieter tone below its lines is the lane drawn 0.4 (dark) or 0.48 (light) of the way back to the canvas.
- The margin is bare: numbers stand straight on the canvas in a 34 px column, with no band, rail or fill (a selected event still fills it). Blocks step 18 px per level from 52 px (top level 52, sub-events 70, 88), each level's line stands in its slot, the fold arrow is a filled triangle wholly inside its slot on the first line, and events and sub-events are exactly 1 px apart with no card border round them.
- A group is one flat bar from its own slot (fold triangle, bold name, nothing at the right but the on/off switch while it is off or pointed at), its body is marked by its slot's line instead of a bracket at the canvas edge, and its foot reads `Add event to \"Setup\"` and `+ Add to \"Setup\"...` (translated in all nine CSVs).
- An inverted condition's cross leads its words, no line is drawn between the object and words columns, a line with an object name but no icon starts the name where the lane's other names start, and an inert function body offers none of the add doors (`+ Add...` included). Classic Sheet is unchanged.
### Changed: the Editor Native readings the event-alignment board asks for

- A branch of an if/else pair closes its action lane with the same \"+ Add action\" / \"+ Add...\" line an event does, and leads with its object's icon. The function head's action lane ends with that line too (a click on it on a body with no first step puts a blank step first and fills it).
- A return reads \"Function | Return ...\" in an event and in a branch alike. A line about a node named by its path (`$House3x3`) reads under the node's name with a capitalised verb, and a name taken off a `$Path` never borrows the icon of an engine class that shares the word (`$Tree`).
- A local declared by a ternary (`var sign_text = \"-\" if value < 0 else \"\"`) reads as its own Local row (type, name, the value when no branch ran) ahead of the pair, and each arm reads \"sign_text | Set to ...\". A reading only: the file is untouched and saves back byte for byte.
- A condition-less event draws an empty condition cell in the rest tone: no \"Always\" or \"Every Tick\" word, no strip, and the add-condition door waits for the pointer.
- A group takes a number in the margin ahead of what it holds; the events after it move down. Find in project and the Project Doctor name events by the margin's number (branches and groups counted), through `EventSheetResource.margin_numbers`.
- A plain click on a branch marks that branch alone; ranges and toggles still address the whole statement.
- `_ready` on a script that is not the scene's own reads \"System | On ready\" (it read \"<script> | On created\").

### Fixed: the gap a carried event opens lines up with the blocks of its level, and opens after the whole event when carried over a branch

- In Editor Native the dashed gap an event is carried into began at the wrong edge (10 px short for a nested level, inside the number column for a top-level one); it now begins at the left edge of the blocks of the level it opens at. Proven in the real editor, in both looks and both skins, over a 1-, 2- and 3-deep event.
- Carried over a branch of a pair (`x = a if c else b`), the gap opened one level too deep and before the event's own sub-events, although the drop lands after the event the pair belongs to. It now opens at that event's level, below everything the event draws.

### Changed: Editor Native numbers the branches of a pair as events of their own

- Where a statement hoists a ternary into two rows (`return \"A\" if c else \"B\"`, `var s = \"-\" if c else \"\"`), each branch is an event of its own in the margin: the rows of the pair take the next numbers (a guard 2 with two branches reads 2, 3, 4) and the events after them move down, as does the run of actions after a pair. Folding never renumbers anything (the walk reads folded rows too), and Classic Sheet keeps one number per event.
- The editor's own \"event N\" words (Find's counter, Go to event, the repeated-run strip, the status bar's \"event N of M\", whose M counts the branches too) follow the margin. The Project Doctor and Find in project name events by the sheet's own count, which is the margin's whenever nothing branches. The \"neither of N\" note an Else row carries cites the margin's number, so 40 such notes in the shipped packs now read a larger N in this look (no other reading moved).

### Changed: a function body that opens on a branch has no head row in Editor Native, and its locals stand at the body's level

- A body event with no condition whose first action hoists a ternary into two rows (`var s = \"-\" if c else \"\"`) drew an \"Always\" head row that said nothing but its placeholder, which pushed its branches and the locals after them one level too deep. The head is no longer drawn: the branches, the locals and the run after them all stand on the level of the events round them.

### Fixed: no popup, dialog or window opens off screen or away from what opened it

Every window the editor opens now goes through one helper, `EventSheetWindowPlacement`, that works against the usable rect (the screen less the task bar) of the screen the window belongs to. A dialog is centred on the visible part of the editor window it opens over (the engine centred it on the screen, far from a small or moved editor window), shrunk when it is larger than the room it has, and kept clear of the title bar's reserve. A menu, panel or list opened at the pointer or under a field flips above and left-aligns to its anchor when there is no room below or to the right, on the monitor the anchor is on. Pointer positions follow the editor's sub-window mode, so a menu is no longer placed by screen coordinates when the editor embeds its sub-windows. The blend-mode strip opened at the top-left of the editor window instead of under its field; it now opens under the field. A sweep test fails when a new opener calls an engine popup function directly.

### Fixed: four Wave 16b decisions

- The no-em-dash rule covers the translation catalogs: every em-dash in the Russian and Simplified
  Chinese files is a plain \" - \" (or a comma in Chinese), and `translation_catalogs_test` fails the
  suite on a new one in any shipped `.csv`.
- A state rename or removal changes only the names it renames or removes. The setter, `state_changed`
  and the two helper variables are written only when the States window DECLARES a machine the sheet does
  not have yet; a hand-written `enum State` and `var state` is edited in place and nothing else in the
  file moves (pinned in `states_project_wide_test`).
- The sheet head's events count and the status bar agree: both count every event, the ones inside
  functions included, and say \"1 event\" for one. 93 sheets' head cells move (10 gain a count they never
  showed), and nothing else in the reading does.
- The Wave 16 nested-divider check is rewritten to the Editor Native rule (one 1 px line per nesting
  level at the block's left edge, the cell beginning at its own block) and still holds in Classic Sheet
  (`sub_event_strips_test`).

### Fixed: two Wave 16b leftovers

- A string default written over two source lines shows its break in the row. The value read
  `\"helloworld\"`; it now reads `\"hello\\nworld\"`, the escape a one-line spelling of the same string would
  use (`ViewportRowBuilder.string_break_as_escape`). The file is untouched and saves back byte for byte.
- The loose runtime scripts beside the pack folders (`bus_mix`, `haptics`, `sequencer`, `sequence`,
  `world_look`, `free_spot`, `pooled_nodes`, `haptic_pattern`) give every declaration its own brief,
  true `##` note instead of one note written for a group. `pack_variable_descriptions_test` now holds
  these scripts to the same rule as the pack folders (described, one line, not a paragraph, no
  neighbour named, not shouting, not cut off).

### Fixed: a string default written over two source lines opens as one variable row

- `var note := \"one` then `two\"` used to open as the variable plus a stray wordless `pass\"` row. The
  declaration lift now takes the whole literal, keeps its lines exactly as written, and is gated by
  byte-exact re-emission like every lift. Fixture `tests/fixtures/multiline_string_default.gd`.

### Fixed: the inherited bar counts in the singular

- \"1 variables / 2 functions\" now reads \"1 variable / 2 functions\". `EventSheetL10n.counted(one, many, count)`
  picks the singular row for exactly one and the plural row otherwise; all nine translation files already
  carry both rows, so each language words its own.

### Fixed: behaviour variable descriptions say what the code does

- A pack-wide fold had put notes written for a group of declarations onto the first one. Every pack
  builder was read against its code and the wrong ones rewritten: units copied from a 2D twin into a 3D
  one, a bool that claimed to hold a scene, a level stored as 0..1 described as decibels, notes that began
  \"And ...\", a note in capitals, and plain comments whose first line had been cut. `ollie_speed` stays
  exported and now says the Ollie action takes its own strength and this value is unused.
- `pack_variable_descriptions_test` now fails a note that names another declaration of the same file, is
  mostly upper case, begins with a conjunction or stops mid-sentence.

### Added: the showcase state enums describe their machine and every state

- Starfall's `State` and the quest showcase's `QuestState` carry a `##` line above the enum (what the
  machine is for) and one above each member (what the object is doing in it), written in
  `tools/build_examples.gd`. No shipped behaviour pack declares an enum machine today (the state
  machine pack keeps its states as free strings), so the pack rule in
  `pack_variable_descriptions_test` already fails any pack enum or state added without a line, and
  the same rule now also covers every enum in `demo/showcase/`, with real values pinned.

### Fixed: Wave 16b review closure

- A state renamed or removed in the States window now SAVES the declaring sheet in the same step that
  writes the other scripts naming it, so the project on disk compiles whether or not the tab is saved
  again. Undo and Redo do the same, in that order.
- Undo and Redo of such an edit never write over a file somebody changed after it (on disk, or open in
  Godot's script editor with unsaved changes): the step then writes no file, leaves the sheet unsaved,
  and says which file.
- Removing a state names, before Apply, each file whose tests of that state (`== Player.State.JUMP`, a
  `match` branch) will test the default instead, and each scene or resource that saved the state
  variable as a number, whose meaning a removal from the middle of the list shifts.
- \"Also run the parent's version\", picked into a function, is written with that function's own
  parameters (`super(amount)`), and as `return super(...)` where the parent's function answers. Where
  it may be offered is now part of the picker's one closed list of what may be hidden.
- The live state row is laid out at most four times a second while a machine flips state, instead of
  at every flip.
- The \"From <parent>\" group a kind of a class inherits starts folded to its bar, as the class board
  draws it.
- A function body of an opened file that has not been made editable no longer shows a \"+ Add action\"
  that does nothing when clicked (Editor Native draws the add line at rest, and a one-step function
  draws its step's line in its head). \"Make Function Body Editable\" brings it back.
- Renaming a state in a hand-written file wrote the state helpers (`state_changed`, `previous_state`,
  `state_entered_msec`) between `class_name` and `extends`, so the saved file no longer parsed. They
  now go under `extends` and the class description. The States window also grows to hold the list of
  what a removal reaches, so its Apply button stays inside it.
- The status bar counts the events inside functions too, so a behaviour pack's sheet no longer says
  \"0 events\".
### Changed: Editor Native lays out locals at their step, notes above the lines they are about, and adds the \"+ Add...\" doors

- A local variable (an event's own, or a `var` line in its body) is one tight row at the step of the
  code that declares it: before the event whose actions carry its value, on the level of the events
  round it, no longer drawn under the event it was lifted out of. A body that splits at a branching
  line puts each local before the slice that holds its line. It is a drawing order of rows only: the
  file still opens and saves back byte for byte, and every other look keeps its locals where they were.
- A comment row begins where the event it introduces does (its band starts at that event's block, so
  the nesting lines of the levels round it run past), and a note written about an event's actions heads
  its action lane instead of closing it.
- \"+ Add...\" sits at the far right of every event's add line and \"+ Add to 'name'...\" at the right of
  every group's foot, beside the left \"+ Add event to 'name'...\". Each selects what it belongs to and
  opens a small menu: an event offers Add action, Add condition, Add sub-event and Add comment; a
  group offers Add event (the picker, inserting into that group) and Add local variable. A group
  now closes with its foot even when it holds events. The new foot text is translated in all nine
  translation files.
- Pins: `locals_at_their_step_test`, `native_add_doors_test`.

### Fixed: the editor's own MatchRow script reads without a stray row

- `match_row.gd` wrote its default branch text as a string running over two source lines, which
  opened as the variable plus a wordless `pass\"` row and put the file over its group's wordless-row
  ceiling the day the self-reading sample picked it. The default is the same text, written on one
  line with `\\n\\t`.

### Fixed: a name cut short in a chip cell is read whole on hover

- In a cell drawn as a chip (Editor Native's condition and action cells) the hover measured the
  object column from the cell's edge instead of after its padding, so a name only a few pixels too
  wide (\"CharacterBo…\") drew its ellipsis but answered no hover. The hover now asks the same
  elide, on the same column, the row draws with.

### Fixed: the Prompts pack's two clock variables say what they hold

- `_deadline` said it held the seconds left to answer; it holds the engine time the open prompt runs
  out, and `_opened_at` no longer describes both variables in one sentence.

### Changed: Editor Native lays an event out as one panel, with its nesting as lines in the margin

- Nesting is ONE 1 px line per level down the margin, on each block's left edge and running the whole
  height of the parent block (across the air between two events too), with the fold arrow beside the
  block's first line. A nested block starts past the number column and its ancestors' lines; no fill,
  no second line, no fold column.
- A condition cell is one panel the full height of its event, however many actions it has: each
  condition line is its own lighter strip ruled underneath, and the cell carries on in a quieter tone to
  the event's foot (an event with no condition draws one empty strip and the quiet tone below it).
- A local variable declared inside an event is one tight line on the sheet itself: no band, no card, no
  gap, and no number in the margin.
- `+ Add action` stays under every event's actions at rest (the add condition line still waits for the
  hover). A test no object owns (`value > 0`) reads with System as its object, so the words of a lane
  start at one x. The action lane's name column is 140 px wide.

### Changed: states are project-wide, and every state variable is its own machine

- Renaming a state in the States window renames it in every place the project says it: this sheet's
  rows, triggers, hand-written lines and typed values, and every other script that names it through
  the declaring class (`Player.State.RUN`) or extends that class and inherits the enum. The window
  lists each sheet and file with its count before Apply writes anything, and one Undo puts the sheet
  and all of those files back. A behaviour pack's script is never rewritten.
- Removing a state is no longer refused when rows still use it: the window lists the rows and files
  that use it, and Apply moves them to the machine's default state (the same single Undo).
- Every `<x>_state` variable typed by an enum of its own (`weapon_state: WeaponState`) is a machine
  and a state row of its own, with its own window, states, default and cards. The primary `state`
  keeps its helpers and its live reading.
- The state the running game is in lights up inside its token with the time it has held, RUN · 3.2 s,
  instead of as text at the end of the row. The row holds room for the time, so nothing moves when
  the seconds change.
- Clicking a state in the row opens the States window with that state picked; a double-click on any
  word of the row opens it too.
- The States window keeps no help strip, as the board draws none.

### Changed: sub-events sit packed, with the fold arrow on the block's first line

- The fold arrow of an event stays beside the block's FIRST line in every look (the strips look used to
  move it to the block's foot). Events and sub-events in Editor Native sit 1 px apart instead of 8, so
  no empty band opens between two events. (The nesting strips this entry first filled with the parent's
  lane colour are gone: nesting is now one line per level in the margin, as the entry above says.) An
  object name the fixed column cuts short (\"CharacterBo...\") is read whole on hover. Drawing only: no
  reading, no emitted byte and no saved sheet changes.

### Added: the Class window, and the also-run action that names what it runs

- A click on the sheet head's name opens the Class window: Name, A kind of (Extends in Godot words),
  Icon and Description in one window, each field one line of the file (`class_name`, `extends`,
  `@icon`, `##`). Your classes complete first, then Godot's; Icon completes the project's pictures;
  a name another class has, a base nothing completes and a picture that is not there are said in the
  window before anything is written. Renaming lists every file and row first and goes through the
  same walk as Rename Everywhere; changing what the class is built on lists the members the old base
  gave that the new one does not, among those the sheet uses. Apply is one undo step, and a script
  opened as a preview unlocks on it like any other first edit. The name used to answer only a
  double-click; a script attached to a scene had no click on its name at all.
- A kind of one of your classes reads what it inherits right under its head: a \"From Mob\" bar (the
  triangle folds it, the parent's name opens the parent's sheet) and the parent's variables and
  functions as read-only dashed rows. Double-click a variable to start it at another value (\"Slime
  starts it at 60.0\", written in `_init`, so the parent's own value is never touched); right-click a
  function for \"Replace in Slime\" (Godot words: \"Override in Slime\"), which writes a function with the
  same parameters that already runs the parent's version first. Both survive a save and reopen.
- The head says \"a kind of Mob\" in Familiar words and \"extends Mob\" in Godot's, beside the class.
- Right-clicking a sheet's tab on a sheet that names a class offers \"New kind of <class>…\", the same
  door the FileSystem menu has.
- Your classes lead the object list as a tree of kinds, each with its own icon and its description on
  hover. After an event asks \"body is a Mob\", an action's picker opens on the objects with `body · Mob`
  first: picking it offers Mob's own members, written on `body`.
- \"Also run the parent's version\" now reads \"Also run Enemy's take_damage\" (Godot words: \"Call
  super: take_damage\"), naming the class and the function it sits in, and is offered only inside a
  function that replaces one of the parent's. Anywhere else it would write a `super()` that does not
  compile.

### Fixed: type tests read with the right article

- \"Is a kind of\" reads on the value being asked about, `body | is an Enemy`, and says \"an\" before a
  class said with a vowel (Enemy, Area2D) and \"a\" before the rest (Node2D, Unit). A row authored from
  the picker and the same line reopened from a file are one reading; the bytes written are unchanged.

### Changed: every behaviour variable has a brief description

- Every variable and constant a shipped behaviour declares now carries a one-line `##`
  description (about a thousand were bare, most of them internal state), and about a hundred
  long design notes were cut to their first sentence, the rest kept as a plain `#` comment above
  it. A variable row, the Inspector tooltip and the emitted script all read the same line.
- A variable that is not exported now writes its description above it in the generated script,
  as an exported one always did. Before, the description was kept on the row and dropped on
  emit. A tooltip attribute on a private variable is still an Inspector setting and writes nothing.
- `pack_variable_docs_test` pins both the compiler change and that no top-level variable or
  constant of a shipped pack script lacks a description.
- Descriptions are brief: about three hundred that still ran to a paragraph (a design rationale
  in place of what the variable controls) are one line now, the rationale kept as a plain `#`
  comment where it was long. `pack_variable_descriptions_test` fails on a description over 160
  characters, on an enum or enum member a pack declares without one (no shipped pack declares an
  enum today), and reads six packs back through the importer to prove the note arrives on the row.
  The state machine pack's `state` says that any name works and Go to state switches it.

### Fixed: Wave 16 review closure

- The vocabulary reference (`EVENTSHEETS-VOCABULARY.md`) lists every pack verb again. The
  generator ended a verb at any code annotation between its `##` block and its `func`, so a
  verb carrying `@warning_ignore(...)` (Fly Along, Blend To, Discover, Create Pool and about
  twenty more) vanished from the reference while the pack went on shipping it. The committed
  doc is regenerated, and `tools/vocabulary_doc.gd -- --check` (a CI gate now) fails when the
  committed doc and the generator disagree.
- The states words read in your language again. The 63 new state words (the state row's readings,
  the States window, the type cards) had been filled with the English text in all eight languages,
  so \"is IDLE\" read English where \"Is in {state}\" had been translated. They are translated now,
  the readings carry their old translations over, and Is A Kind Of and Also Run Parent Function
  are in all nine catalogs. The translation test now also fails when a language file gains cells
  that read exactly like their English key.
- Opening a .gd: the spinner, the hairline, \"Show as code\" and the ring on Code take the look's own
  link colour (Classic Sheet's too) instead of the editor's accent; the first pass reads \"reading
  the code\"; \"Show as code\" sits straight after the status words; and the ring keeps the Code
  button's own fill instead of replacing it with an empty box.
- New Event Sheet: the A kind of / Extends field warns only when nothing it offers begins with what
  is typed, so \"Ene\" on the way to Enemy is no longer red. A sheet made as a kind of one of your own
  classes is named as a class; extending a Godot class such as CharacterBody2D no longer adds a
  `class_name` to the new file.
- Typing in any field that completes (the A kind of field, parameter fields, the States window)
  no longer stops working once the completion list is open. The list was a popup window, and while
  a popup is open the keyboard goes to it, so Backspace and every letter after it were lost. It now
  floats in a window that never takes the keyboard, and closes when the field or its window loses
  focus. Escape with the list open closes the list and leaves the dialog open.
- A state row (and any enum or signal) carried over an event goes before or after it, never into it:
  GDScript has no place for a class-level declaration inside an event, and the state machine came
  apart when its enum landed there.
- Nested events: each depth bounds its name column by its own lane, and the name column gives way at
  half of that lane (it was 45 percent of the sheet's top-level lane). On an event nested past the
  150 px floor, the divider it draws is the one that can be grabbed, and its actions lane is
  measured from it.
- The States window's row counts use the editor's quieter text colour instead of a fixed alpha.
- The state row acts on the machine it stands for. Delete, Cut and Copy take the State enum and every
  line the row hides (the `state` variable, the previous state, the entered time, `state_changed`)
  under one undo, instead of removing only `var state` and leaving the rest as loose rows; Add below,
  a drag and the jump to code anchor on the enum, where the row is drawn.
- The state row's variable name opens a card naming the lines it keeps out of sight (the previous
  state, when the current state began, `state_changed`), and choosing a state for is / was / go to
  shows each state's own sentence beside its name.
- Drawing: the words with a card behind them are measured once per text instead of every frame,
  and the running game's live states are worked out once per frame received.

### Changed: sub-events lined up (Editor Native)

- Each nesting level now steps the whole block right by one 16 px strip, filled with the parent's
  colour (an event's own, or its group's) and ruled by a 1 px line down its left side. No connector
  line is drawn: the new `sub_event_strips` event-style token turns the strips on and the rail
  off, and it is on in the Editor Native starter and its rule (step 16 px, was 18) and off
  everywhere else, so every other look draws as before.
- The fold arrow of an event sits at the foot of its block in a look that draws strips.
- The sheet keeps ONE condition/action divider at every depth: a nested event's conditions lane is
  the divider minus the steps it has taken, floored at 150 px, and only an event nested past that
  floor starts its own actions further right.
- Fixed: dragging the lane divider or the name column in a sheet that never chose a look turned it
  into a Classic-like style (the lane ratio, badge column and action column all changed, and rows
  ghosted). The sheet now keeps the look it was drawn in, and the dragged number saves onto a copy
  of it.

### New: classes and kinds of a class (first slice)

- New condition **Is A Kind Of** (`body is Enemy`): the GDScript `is`, with a class field that
  completes from your own classes first, then Godot's. A Slime that extends Enemy passes for Enemy.
- New action **Also Run Parent Function** (`super(amount)`): the line that keeps a function a kind
  replaces from losing what the class above it already did.
- Right-click a script that names a class in the FileSystem: **New kind of Enemy...** (Familiar
  words) or **New script extending Enemy...** (Godot words). The New Event Sheet window opens in
  that folder with the class already in its new **A kind of** / **Extends** field; the field
  completes from your own classes first, then Godot's, warns on a name that is no class, and the
  window also warns when the file's class name is already taken. The new sheet reads \"Slime
  extends Enemy\" and writes `class_name Slime` / `extends Enemy`.
- A model of kinds (`dock/class_kinds.gd`): a project's classes as a tree of kinds, the variables
  and functions a class hands down (nearest class first), the head in Familiar or Godot words,
  starting an inherited variable at another value (written in `_init`), and replacing an inherited
  function with the parent's version run first. Every line it writes is plain GDScript that opens
  and saves byte for byte. The two new verbs author their lines; a file you open that already has
  an `is` test or a `super(...)` call keeps the words its rows have today (neither verb claims a
  hand-written line), so no opened file reads differently.

### Fixed: Opening a .gd no longer puts a banner over the sheet

- The \"Opening <file>\" strip is gone. It was a 72 px navy panel, drawn the same in every look and
  both editor themes (its detail text was close to invisible in the light theme), that pushed the
  sheet down while a .gd was read as events and let it jump back up when the read finished. A file
  that is opening now says so in three places that take no room: its tab swaps its icon for a
  small spinner in the look's accent, a 2 px hairline along the foot of the tab strip fills as the
  read advances, and the status line reads \"Opening player.gd · reading functions 6 of 14\" (reading
  the file, reading functions n of m, checking the code still matches, checking the script compiles,
  with the seconds once a pass has gone past half a second). **Show as code** sits beside it in the
  status line, and **Code** in the Events / Code / Both switch (ringed in the accent while the open
  runs) is the other way out: either stops reading the rows and shows the file as code, as the
  banner's button did. With reduced motion the spinner holds still and the hairline jumps instead
  of sliding. Every colour comes from the editor theme, so dark and light need nothing of their
  own.

### Changed: States on the sheet - one row, one spelling

- **A state machine is one row.** The enum, the `state` variable and its default now read as a single row:
  the state glyph, the type, the variable's own name, \"is one of\", the states exactly as the code writes them
  with the default marked \"(default)\", and the machine's `##` description muted and italic like any
  variable's note. The head's \"states Idle · Run · Jump - starts in Idle\" band is gone, and the declarations
  that only serve the machine (`previous_state`, `state_entered_msec`, the `state_changed` signal, the
  state variable's setter rows) stay in the code but are not drawn. A helper written differently by hand
  still shows.
- **Names as written, everywhere.** `IDLE` is never re-cased to \"Idle\": the row, the conditions and
  actions, the triggers, the trail, the Doctor and the pickers all say what the code says.
- **The state variable is the object of its rows.** \"state is RUN\", \"state was JUMP\", \"state go to RUN\",
  \"state is RUN for over 2 s\" and the triggers \"On entering X\" / \"On leaving X\" lead with the state glyph
  and the variable's name instead of borrowing the host or System. A lifted `match state:` reads as
  \"state is IDLE\" sub-events whose assignments read \"state go to RUN\". (The reading text of the four rows
  changed from \"Is in X\" / \"Was in X\" / \"Go to X\"; the ids and templates did not.)
- **Cards.** Every state name on the sheet has a faint dotted rule and a card: its name, \"the default
  state\" when it is, and its sentence. Every variable's type (int, float, bool, String, an enum, a node or
  resource class) has the same rule and a card with one sentence and the page of Godot's documentation it
  comes from.
- **Sentences are `##` lines.** An enum row carries the machine's description (the `##` above the enum) and
  one sentence per member (the `##` above each member); they lift back byte for byte and an enum without
  them is written exactly as before.
- **The States window.** Double-click the state row (or choose Declare states): what it is for, the ordered
  states each with a name, a sentence and a default radio, add, remove (a state rows still use is refused
  with their count), reorder, and rename - which follows every row, trigger and line of kept code. Exactly
  one default always: a new machine takes its first state, removing the default moves it to the next.
  The window explains itself with its own paragraph and its live code preview, as its board draws it,
  rather than the help strip at the foot that the old one-field window wore.
- Fixed with the wave's integration: four suite checks still pinned the old readings (a branch's test
  led by the System gear and spelled \"state is IDLE\" in its condition cell, a comment naming the state as
  \"Patrol\", and the States window among the dialogs with a help strip); they now pin the state variable
  as the object with its glyph, the state as written, and the window as built.

### Fixed: Undo works inside the editor

- In the editor, the sheet's Undo and Redo did nothing: Ctrl+Z after a drag-drop (or any other
  edit), the toolbar's Undo and Redo, Scene ▸ Undo and the History panel all said \"Nothing to undo\".
  The editor's undo manager keeps one history per scene and one for the editor, and the questions
  \"is there a step\" and \"take it back\" belong to those histories, not to the manager. The sheet now
  asks the history its own steps go into, so every one of those doors undoes and redoes the last
  edit. Clearing the sheet's history now clears only its own, never the open scenes'.
- The sheet's Undo and Redo (Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Shift+Z, the toolbar, the History panel) are now the
  editor's own Scene ▸ Undo and Scene ▸ Redo. Taking a step back by running the history directly
  left the editor's undo manager behind: after a Ctrl+Z in the sheet, Scene ▸ Redo found nothing to
  redo, the next Scene ▸ Undo took back one step too many, and Godot's History dock still showed
  the undone step as current. Every door now moves exactly one step, in step with the History dock.
  Like Scene ▸ Undo, it takes back the editor's newest step, so after a scene edit the first Ctrl+Z
  in the sheet takes back that scene edit; the toolbar's Undo and Redo follow scene edits too. The
  Add behaviour window's Undo takes its step back the same way.

### Fixed: Wave 15 review closure

- **The MCP server finds the editor later, and never writes behind it.** A server started before
  the EventSheets workspace opened, or one the editor dropped, served the files for the rest of its
  session, `.gd` writes included, while the editor might hold the same sheet open in a tab. It now
  looks for the editor's link again before every call, until it has one (a link file a closed
  editor left behind costs one refused connection, not one per call). While an editor is listening
  for the project but will not take the server, an edit is refused instead of written to the file;
  a read is still answered. An edit that goes through the editor's tab now passes the same gates a
  file write does first: a `.gd` mid-merge, marked to stay code, or one that does not save back
  byte for byte is refused before the tab is touched, since the person's next save writes it.
- **Open Scripts as Event Sheets keeps its word.** Its hover says a script the sheet cannot read
  still opens in the Script editor, but every `.gd` opened as a sheet, an `@tool extends
  EditorScript` included. A click (in the FileSystem or the Inspector) now leaves an editor script
  (one that extends an Editor class, such as EditorScript or EditorPlugin) and a file that does not
  save back byte for byte to the Script editor. Open as Event Sheet still opens any script.
- **The plugin icon follows a live switch of the editor theme.** The EventSheet button and a sheet
  resource's icon kept the old theme's ink until a restart; both are now drawn again in the new
  ink when the editor's theme changes.
- **Menu ▸ Manual… and Tools ▸ Manual… open the Manual where you left it**, or on its home page,
  as the Manual board draws them; F1 is the door that explains the selection. **Alt+Left and
  Alt+Right walk the Manual's trail** even while its contents tree has the keyboard, instead of
  folding and moving through the tree.
- **Closing the Manual hands the focus back to the picker.** After a section's \"?\" opened the
  Manual over the picker and the Manual was closed, the focus went to the editor's main window, so
  the picker took no hover: \"Open the Platformer guide\" needed a second click, and typing a new
  search went nowhere. The Manual now gives the focus back to the window it opened over.
- **The View menu fits a 1080p window.** It had grown to 66 items, 1392 px tall, so items near its
  foot (MCP Server among them) ran off a laptop screen. The ticks for how rows are drawn now sit
  under **View ▸ Sheet display**, and the sweeps, split views and zoom under **Collapse and
  expand**, **Split views** and **Zoom**: 40 items, 868 px. Every item keeps its place in its group
  and does what it did.
- Measured, serial, in a real editor (`tools/measure_editor_boot.gd`, three runs each): enabling
  the plugin 411 ms at 527cdff4 and 440 ms with this wave (medians, +29 ms for the settings hover
  and the FileSystem click watch); the first sheet opened 22,426 ms and 21,578 ms, so the drag
  motion and selection drawing added nothing measurable to the first open.

### Changed: Editor Native is the default look, in the boards' own colours

- A sheet with no theme of its own now wears Editor Native, moved onto the running editor's greys,
  instead of the older editor-derived look. **This changes existing projects on update:** every
  sheet that never chose a look (every `.gd` sheet, and every `.tres` sheet that never saved a
  theme) draws Editor Native once the plugin is updated, as a fresh install, the Welcome's default
  answer and View > Sheet theme > Match Editor (default) do. A sheet that chose a theme keeps
  it, and re-skinning on an editor theme change re-seats the sheet instead of writing a theme onto
  it, so opening a sheet or switching the editor's theme never marks it unsaved.
- Editor Native's palette is now the design's, measured at Godot's own Default and Light presets
  (base `#292929` / `#e6e6e6`): canvas `#282828` / `#e4e4e4`, event card `#383838` / `#ffffff` with a
  `#232323` / `#bdbdbd` edge, lanes `#2f3742` and `#2f3a31` / `#e9f0f8` and `#eaf4e6`, divider
  `#1e1e1e` / `#d6d6d6`, head bar `#3e3e3e` / `#d3d3d3`, sentence text `#d2d2d2` / `#0f0f0f`, type words
  in one grey, the light hand's object, number, string and link colours, the comment band
  `#38321f` with `#ede3c4` words / `#f7edc4` with `#2e2610` words and no edge of its own, and the
  `#3a72b0` tab edge.
  The greys no longer carry the old blue cast.
- Variable cards take their own fill and edge (`#424242` on `#2a2a2a`, `#eef7e8` on `#b7cdaa`), 28 px
  with a 4 px gap, standing on the canvas rather than on a band; an Inspector group's run sits in a
  `#333333` / `#efefef` box with a `#555555` / `#a9a9a9` edge and 4 px corners instead of the lavender
  outline; events stand 8 px apart.
- Editor Native draws the ordinary strip (Menu, the save and undo icons, Run Scene, the quick-add
  field) with the open sheets' tabs under it, as the boards do; View > Text labels still lays it out
  as words for anyone who wants that.
- New theme tokens, clear (as before) in every other bundled theme: `variable_card_fill_color`,
  `variable_card_border_color`, `variable_group_fill_color`, `variable_group_border_color` and
  `variable_group_corner_radius`.

### Added: the MCP server edits `.gd` sheets, works through the open editor, and edits single rows

- **`.gd` sheets.** `list_sheets` lists the project's `.gd` scripts beside its `.tres` sheets (the
  plugin's own code and the packs it ships are left out). Every editing tool takes a `.gd`: the file
  is opened as a sheet, edited and saved - and written only when it saved back byte for byte before
  the edit, the edited sheet compiles, and the new bytes save back byte for byte themselves.
  Otherwise nothing is written and the answer says why. A file mid-merge or marked to stay code is
  never edited.
- **The open editor.** Once the EventSheets workspace is open and the MCP switch is on, the editor
  listens on `127.0.0.1` (port and a random key in `user://eventsheets_mcp_live.json`), and the
  server your AI client launches passes every call to it. A sheet open in a tab is read as the tab
  holds it, unsaved edits included; an edit lands in the open tab as one undo step, named after the
  tool, so Ctrl+Z takes it back; nothing is saved for you. With no editor answering, or with
  `-- --no-live`, the server works on the files as before. Turning the switch off closes the link.
- **Row edits.** `read_sheet` gives every row an address (`\"3\"`, `\"3/0\"`), and `.gd` rows the file
  lines they are written on. `apply_snippet` takes `parent` and `position` to insert inside an event
  or group; `edit_row` replaces a row; `delete_row` removes one.
- **New tools.** `explain` (a file and line number to the row it opens as), `verify` (the
  command-line contract checks on the files you name), `variable_properties` (read and set a
  variable's export options), `comment` (add a comment, or reword one) and `rename` (a variable or
  function, everywhere the sheet names it). The dock's MCP switch gates every one of them.
- **Fixed: the stdio server answered nothing.** The documented launch line carried `--quiet`, which
  in Godot 4.7 silences everything written to stdout - the answers included - so a client waited
  forever. Launch with `--no-header` instead (the help page and the setup example say so, and a
  launch that still carries `--quiet` says so on stderr). Two requests written together also used
  to be read as one broken line and refused; each line is now answered, and a request id comes back
  as it was sent (`1`, not `1.0`). The server also no longer compiles the whole editor before its
  first answer - only `list_aces` builds it, when first asked.
### Fixed: every way into the Manual lands on the page it names

- **What's new opens What's new.** Menu > What's new… and the Start page's Learn > What's new asked
  for a page id the Manual did not know, so both landed on the home page with \"No documentation for
  that row\". Both now ask the Manual for the page by the id its router draws.
- **F1, Ctrl+F1 and Ctrl+P reach the sheet.** Godot's own shortcuts were answered first, so F1 on a
  selected row opened Search Help, Ctrl+F1 switched to the 2D screen and Ctrl+P opened Quick Open.
  While the keyboard is inside the sheet editor, the sheet's keys are now asked first; anywhere else
  the editor's keys are untouched. F1 answers for the cell you clicked: the condition, not the
  event's trigger.
- **The picker's status line says where a guide opened.** \"Open the Platformer guide\" said \"Opened in
  your browser\" even when the guide opened in the Manual; it now says \"in the Manual\" or \"in your
  browser\", whichever happened.
- **A Project Doctor finding lists its fixes again.** Selecting a finding built its fix rows while
  the list was still handling the click, which Godot refuses, so in the editor no fix row (and no
  Learn more) ever appeared under a finding. The rows are now built once the click is over.
- **The contents group named for another product is now \"Coming from another event-sheet editor\"**,
  as the Welcome window already says.

### New: more ways into the Manual

- **View > Manual beside the sheet** shows the Manual as a dock next to the sheet, following what you
  select. The dock existed, but nothing in the plugin opened it. With it showing, every door sends
  its page there instead of opening a window; the item is ticked while it shows and puts it away
  when chosen again.
- **The ? on a picker section heading** opens the Manual's page for that whole group of words: the
  vocabulary module guide for a built-in section (Time, Input, Math & Random ...), the behaviour's
  guide for a behaviour's section (Platformer, FPS Controller ...). The picker stays open.
- **Read the guide in Add behaviour.** The selected behaviour's name, then Read the guide, over its
  properties - for a pack in the project and for one still in the library - and on a pack's
  right-click menu. The window stays open behind the guide.
- **Learn more on a Project Doctor finding.** Selecting a finding lists, under its fixes, the Manual
  page that names its check, at the heading over that mention, or the \"What the Doctor says\"
  section of the guide about its subject. A check no page explains has no Learn more rather than a
  guess.
- **A behaviour's page offers Add to project** at its top when the behaviour waits in the plugin's
  library and the project does not hold it yet; it opens Add behaviour on that pack.
- **The Manual's home page** is two short rows of doors - Start here (Your first platformer, A
  top-down shooter, What's new, Learning paths) and Look something up (Every condition and action,
  Every behaviour, Glossary, Patterns) - and the line for people moving from another event-sheet
  editor, instead of the long list of links the index page was.
- **Pictures draw in the page.** The pictures the guides show ship in the help folder (a folder with
  a .gdignore, so a project never imports them) and are drawn where the guide places them, instead
  of a card with a \"See this picture online\" button. A picture the plugin does not carry still gets
  the card.
- **Events, Code or Both above every sheet.** The switch that behaviours and globals had now sits on
  every open sheet: the rows, the GDScript the file is, or both side by side. Selecting a row
  highlights the lines it writes and clicking a line selects its row, so the docked Manual follows
  either.

### Fixed: clicking a script opens its sheet, and the setting that does it can be found

- The Project Settings tick that sends a clicked script to the sheet is now
  `eventsheets/editor/open_scripts_as_event_sheets`, listed as \"Open Scripts as Event Sheets\" in the
  plain Project Settings view (Eventsheets > Editor) without switching on Advanced Settings. Hovering
  it says what it does instead of \"No description available\": clicking a script in the FileSystem or
  the Inspector opens it as an event sheet you can edit, instead of in the Script editor; right-click
  a script, then Open as Event Sheet, works either way. A project that ticked the old
  `auto_preview_gd_on_select` keeps its choice: it is read once, saved under the new key, and the old
  key is dropped.
- With the tick on, a single click on a script in the FileSystem now really opens it in the
  EventSheet tab (the click only ever reached the Inspector, so nothing happened), and a second click
  brings the same tab forward. Scenes and other files still open where Godot opens them.

### Changed: the plugin icon is one event, drawn in Godot's own icon ink

- `addons/eventsheet/icons/eventsheet.svg` is now one event - a frame cut in two, a trigger arrow on
  the condition side, two action bars on the other - in the single light grey Godot draws its plain
  icons in, instead of five bars in four colours. On a light editor theme the EventSheet button on the
  top bar and the icon a sheet resource wears in the FileSystem turn dark grey like their neighbours:
  the plugin redraws the svg in the editor's icon colour when it loads, and the svg is imported in the
  editor's colours (redrawn once after a switch between a dark and a light theme).
### Fixed: selecting an event draws one frame round it and everything under it

- Selecting a parent event already selected its local lines and sub-events; each of them then drew
  a frame of its own, so event 5 of BoomerLevel showed seven frames. The selection is now drawn as
  runs: one frame from the top of the parent to the bottom of its last child, one fill across every
  row inside it (local lines and both lanes included), and no frame on the rows inside. Neighbouring
  selected variable, enum and signal lines share one frame the same way, and a gap splits it. Two
  events picked side by side are still two frames, a sub-event picked on its own is framed alone,
  and a single cell keeps its own small outline.

### Changed: the pattern an event was read as leaves the row in every look

- The ⟡ chip naming a pattern (\"Shots, blasts and an arsenal\") no longer sits inside the conditions
  lane in any look; it already left it in Editor Native, and now the default look and every other
  theme follow. A row holds its object, its words and its parameters, so the lanes line up from
  event to event. The pattern is said in the help strip when the event is selected, and hovering
  the event's number opens a card beside it: \"Event 4\", what the event is, \"⟡ Reads as: ...\" and
  an Open in the Manual (F1) door. The card opens beside the number, so the pointer can still run
  down the column of numbers. Explain this reading in the row menu is unchanged. The theme token
  that switched the chip (`pattern_in_help_strip`) is kept so saved themes load, and is no longer
  offered as a choice.

### Changed: dragging carries the real row, and the sheet makes room for it

- Dragging a condition, an action, an event or several selected rows now carries the thing itself,
  drawn by the sheet's own renderer, tilted a little with a shadow; several carried things stack
  with their count. Where it would land, the lane opens a dashed gap the height of what is carried
  and the rows round it slide apart (160 ms, eased); the place it came from folds away once the
  pointer has left it, so the sheet already looks as it will after the drop. The drop target is
  read off the sheet as it is drawn, so a gap opening under the pointer never moves it.
- An event over the right half of another goes in as a sub-event (the gap opens indented); over the
  left half it goes before or after it.
- A condition over the action lane (or an action over the conditions lane) opens no gap: the ghost
  turns red-edged and says why (\"Conditions go on the left\"), as it does for any place that cannot
  take what is carried.
- Esc while dragging puts it back: every gap closes and the row slides home. With Reduced Motion on
  (View menu, or the system's own setting) the gap and the fold appear at once, without sliding.

### Fixed: a big sheet no longer freezes the editor a few seconds after it opens

- A function's hover card names the files that call it, an answer the editor counts in the
  background. When that count finished, every row of the open sheet was built again to put it in:
  on a 1,443-row sheet (the platformer pathfinding pack) the editor froze for 11-13 s a few seconds
  after the file had opened, with nobody touching it. The cards are now written again in place
  (3.5 ms for 200 functions); the rows are built again only when a band about a scene was laid out
  while the scenes were still being counted, or when a band asked
  `EventSheetProjectShareIndex.request()` and was told \"not yet\" - the readiness question a custom
  block or head band asks before it says \"counting…\", so such a band is still built again with its
  count. `request(false)` asks without that promise, for a reader that rebuilds itself.

### Fixed: building a behaviour pack is up to fifteen times faster

- Publishing a pack (the bundled pack builders and Export Addon) turns each function body into rows
  only where the file still compiles byte for byte to what it did. That check compiled the whole
  sheet twice for every function. It now compiles it once and tries the bodies in halves, so the
  bodies that all pass cost one compile together. The platformer pathfinding pack builds in 18 s
  instead of 4 min 30 s, save_system in 6 s instead of 52 s, and rebuilding every pack takes about
  4 minutes instead of 20. Every pack still comes out byte for byte as before.
### Fixed: node, scene and resource variables read as the Inspector lists them

- In the default look and Editor Native, a variable holding a node, a scene, a resource or a typed
  list now reads like any other variable: its type exactly as written, its name, its value as
  written, and its tiles. `@export var target: Node2D` reads `Node2D target = null`, the `null`
  muted because nothing is assigned yet. `@export var bullet_scene: PackedScene = preload(...)` and
  a resource of your own class (`stats: EnemyStats = preload(...)`) read `bullet_scene =
  bullet.tscn` with the eye tile and the file's chip, where they used to turn into a plain line with
  no tiles, no hover card and no variable window; double-clicking one now opens the variable window
  with Load and New beside the value, its Inspector preview showing the file by name as the Inspector
  does. `Array[Node2D] _in_range = []` keeps its `[]` instead of the
  word \"empty\". Classic Sheet and the other sentence-row themes read as before.

### Added: the behaviour library - a project holds only the behaviours it asked for

- The packs now ship a second time in `addons/eventsheet_library/`, a folder holding a `.gdignore`, so
  Godot never imports it: none of its classes reach Create Node, the picker, the class list, an import
  or an exported game, and the editor boots no slower for it. A project's own behaviours stay ordinary
  folders in `eventsheet_addons/`. A behaviour is still a folder: there means in use, deleted means
  gone. There is no on or off switch.
- **The Welcome asks \"What are you making?\"** Each answer is a kit: a tag the packs carry
  (`@ace_tags`) that at least four of them share, listing those packs by name. Each kit is titled as a
  kind of game in the editor's language (\"A 3D game\", \"Game feel\"), never shown as its bare tag; a tag
  with no title is not offered. Nothing is copied until Add and continue; Start with none is a real
  answer. The kits scroll inside their card, and the scroll gives way until the whole window - title
  bar and buttons too - fits inside the editor window and the screen.
- **Add behaviour searches the project and the library together.** A library pack's tile says
  Library, its card lists what else it needs (read from its own `@ace_requires`) and the folder it is
  copied into, and the button reads **Add to project and attach**: the folders are copied in, Godot
  imports them and the behaviour is attached, as one undo step whose Undo takes the node out and sends
  the copied folders to the system trash. A folder the project already holds is never overwritten,
  and a copy that fails part way takes back every folder it had made, so a half-copied folder is
  never left behind to be kept as \"already in the project\".
  **All / In this project** hides the library.
- **Remove from project…** on a behaviour the project holds sends its folder to the system trash and
  first says which sheets use it (\"Used by 2 sheets\", with the rows in each). Those rows stay as
  written, and they need it back to run.
- **Tidy behaviours…** is the door on the Doctor's existing note about unused packs: every behaviour
  in the project, the unused ones ticked and the used ones locked with the files that use them.
  Everything removed stays in the library.
- **The picker on an empty search** names the library packs whose actions, conditions or expressions
  match (\"Nothing in this project matches \"dash\".\" then In the library), each opening Add behaviour on
  that pack. Nothing is added from the picker itself.
- Installing: copy `addons/eventforge/`, `addons/eventsheet/` and `addons/eventsheet_library/`; the
  packs no longer need copying by hand. An existing project that copied every pack keeps them all and
  changes nothing on its own.
- For contributors: this repository is a project that holds every pack, so its tests, showcases and
  drift gates keep reading `eventsheet_addons/`. The library is derived from it by
  `tools/build_library.gd` (run by `build_sample_behaviors.gd` after every build), the pack audit
  prints `library drifted=N` and fails on drift, and `behaviour_library_test` asks the same question.
### Added: comments are made and moved where they are needed

- **Q with an action selected** puts the comment in that action list, right under the action - a `#`
  line between two statements - instead of a new row under the event.
- **Right-click on an action offers Add note** (it used to say Edit Note… and open \"Row Comment\"): the
  words after the action, saved as the comment at the end of its own line. A comment sitting among
  the actions offers none: it is words already. A condition no longer offers a note at all; to
  explain an event, put a comment row above it. A condition that still carries a note from an older
  sheet offers **Remove note**, so that note can be cleared.
- **Alt+Up / Alt+Down** move a comment that sits among an event's actions one place up or down.
- **A comment row dropped on an event's action column** joins that action list, between the actions
  it is dropped between (a blue line shows where). Ctrl while dragging copies it.
- **The Edit comment window shows named colours** - Default, Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Purple
  and Grey - each worked out from the theme's own comment colour, so a comment reads right in a dark
  theme and a light one, and each named in the editor's language. The free picker stays under More
  colours. The box reads \"Shows in the manual
  (writes ##)\". A comment's colour belongs to the sheet: it is never written to the `.gd`, so the code
  stays plain and reopening the file shows the default colour.

### Fixed: a comment is written where it sits on the sheet

- A comment row between events, or inside a group, was written at the end of the file, with a blank
  line before every line and its `##` turned into `#`. It is now written right above the event it sits
  over, at that event's indent, inside the function the event lands in: several lines stay one block,
  `##` stays `##`, a switched-off comment writes `# @off:`, and a comment inside a group sits under
  the group's tag so it reopens in the group. In an opened script, a comment under the last event of a
  handler ends that handler, and one over a variable, a code block or a function is written right
  above it. Only a comment with nothing at all below it still closes the file, and it keeps its marker
  too.
- Opening a script reads a comment written right above an `if` (or a loop) back as the comment row
  above that event, so a file saved from the sheet reopens with its comments where they were, instead
  of as an \"Every tick\" event holding the comment. A comment above a plain statement that follows another
  event of the handler, such as `# Movement` over `var direction := ...` after a blank line, is a
  comment row above that statement's event too, rather than the first line of its actions. A comment
  as the first line of a handler stays the note on its first statement. Every
  file still saves byte for byte.
- Save As .gd from a `.tres` sheet writes each comment row above the event it sits over, the way an
  opened `.gd` writes it, so the new file reopens with its comments in place instead of moving them all
  to the end on the first save. A pack publish still writes them at the end, so no pack changes.
- A file whose `_ready` reads its comments as rows is read a second time without them only when the
  whole-file check was refused at that handler: a file refused somewhere else is no longer lifted
  twice, which on a big hand-written file was a second full \"working out the events\".
- An action's note is now saved: it is written as the comment at the end of the action's own line
  (`$JumpSound.play()  # short blip`), and a line written that way reopens as the action with its
  note. A `#` inside a string is not a note, and a line whose code reads as no action stays as written.
- A comment written after an `if` in your own code is kept exactly as written; a condition takes no
  note of its own.
- The `##` description above a variable that is not exported was left in a code block above it. It
  is now the variable's description - every line of it - shown on its row and written back as its
  `##` lines. An exported variable's description keeps every line too.
- A comment inside a match case wrote `#` whatever it was and was dropped when switched off. It now
  keeps `##` for a paragraph that shows in the manual and writes `# @off:` when it is off.
- A code row that is nothing but comments showed `[b]` and `[i]` as tags; it now draws the words bold
  or italic, the way a comment row does. The file's text is unchanged.

### Fixed: the plugin ships its `.uid` files

- Every script and shader in the plugin, the packs, the showcases and the tools now ships with the
  `.uid` file Godot keeps beside it, as Godot 4.4 and later ask. A project that replaced the plugin
  folders on an update printed one \"Missing .uid file ... re-created from cache\" warning per script
  (951 of them in a project with the packs installed); it now prints none, and a fresh clone keeps
  the same ids on every machine instead of inventing its own.
- **Updating an existing project:** the first import after this update may print a one-time UID
  warning for a scene or resource of yours that named a plugin script by the id your project had
  invented for it before the shipped one arrived. Nothing breaks - Godot falls back to the path - and
  re-saving that scene clears it.
- For contributors: `tests/uid_sidecars_test.gd` holds every tracked `.gd`, `.gdshader` and
  `.gdshaderinc` to its tracked `.uid` (and the reverse) and fails on a uid spelled twice. The pack
  build and the showcase build keep every existing `.uid` exactly as it was, and the pack audit and
  the showcase check fail on a shipped script without one (`uid_sidecars missing=N`) without ever
  counting a `.uid` as drift. A new script gets its `.uid` from `--import`; commit it with the script.

### Fixed: three small readings of a user's own scripts

- In `if body is Player:` the call `body.take_damage(damage)` read \"Take damage   damage\", the value
  with no name. The event's own `is` question now settles the class the way GDScript does, so the
  row reads \"Take damage   amount = damage\" with Player's own parameter name. A negated question
  settles nothing.
- The parameters window's Expressions band read \"Set Velocity Y Y\": a verb whose name already ends
  with the field's name now says it once.
- The On changed and Shown only if tiles' hover cards quoted a placeholder shape. They now quote the
  line they add: the setter that calls your function, and the test `_validate_property` asks.

### Fixed: a variable inside a row's sentence draws once

- \"Accelerate x toward direction * speed at acceleration (per second)\" drew `acceleration` as two
  smeared copies. The bold re-draw of a row's parameters was placed by the width of the whole text
  in front of it, while the coloured pass under it draws the sentence piece by piece, and the two
  drifted apart by a pixel or more per piece. The re-draw now lands exactly on the word it covers.

### Fixed: the not-undoable note asks what a line changes, not which event it sits in

- A menu command that freed your plugin's own panel still got the \"changes the scene the editor
  has open\" note, and anything On Plugin Enabled did to the open scene by hand got none. The note
  now leaves alone what your plugin made for itself (`_panel = ... .instantiate()`, a control handed
  to `add_control_to_bottom_panel`) wherever it is changed, and a switch on or switch off that edits
  the open scene through `EditorInterface.get_edited_scene_root()` is caught like any other event.

### Fixed: a local of one function no longer hides a typo in another

- A name declared as a local or parameter anywhere in a script was accepted everywhere in it, so
  `direction = 2.0` in a function that has no `direction` grew no \"is not a variable\" note, and the
  parameters window passed it, because `_process` declared one. Both now know only the names of the
  body the line sits in: its function, or every event under the same trigger. A comment or a string
  in code kept as written (`# for example`) no longer declares a name either.

### Fixed: a made object and the Expressions panel say the type the value gives

- `var _stats := Patrol.new()` read \"any\" (and \"Variant\" in Editor Native) on the sheet. A variable
  holding a made object now reads the class that made it - `Patrol`, `Node2D`, `Stats.Block` -
  whether that is an engine class, your class_name or a class written inside the file.
- The parameters window's Expressions panel listed `GRAVITY : Variant` and `coyote_time : Variant`
  beside rows that say float. It now lists the type the variable row reads.

### Fixed: a class inside a script sets its own variables on its own line

- A step a class written inside your script takes on its own variable read under System
  (`System | Add 1 to turns` in `class Patrol`). It now reads `Patrol | Add 1 to turns`, the way
  your script's own variables read under your script's name, and a note about a name the class does
  not have names the class.

### Fixed: a line aimed at another body does not read under your script's name

- In a player script that extends CharacterBody2D, a Move and slide or Set velocity row aimed at
  another body (`enemy`) read \"Player | ...\", claiming the wrong object. A row whose target names
  another node keeps the class word again, on the row and in a folded group's object summary; the
  script's own lines still read under its name.

### Fixed: the Doctor reads your own behaviours, globals and packs again

- New behaviour, New global and Export Pack all write a folder under `eventsheet_addons/`, beside
  the packs that came with the plugin, and the Doctor had started leaving that whole folder out. A
  global like `game_state/game_state.gd` was missing from Interop, the reading ledger, Performance
  and Migration, and the guide your own pack keeps beside itself was missing from Docs. The Doctor
  now leaves out only the folders the plugin ships, from a list written beside the plugin's code by
  `tools/measure_packs.gd -- --write` and checked against the pack builders by the suite.

### Fixed: freeing your plugin's own dock is not an edit of the open scene

- Opening your own editor plugin as a sheet put an amber frame on its On Plugin Disabled event and
  \"1 problem\" in the status bar, because `_dock.queue_free()` read as a change to the scene the
  editor has open made outside the undo history. A plugin's switch on and switch off build and free
  the plugin's own UI, and a Ctrl+Z that brought the dock back would be the bug, so neither event is
  asked that question any more. A menu command in the same file that really does change the open
  scene by hand still gets the finding, in the sheet and in the Doctor.

### Fixed: windows open at the size of what they hold

- A window's READS AS and IN CODE lines wrap inside the window instead of widening it. A long
  comment opened Edit comment 2,437 px wide, a long group description opened Edit group 2,454 px
  wide, a long value opened the parameters window 7,812 px wide and the Variable window 3,694 by
  8,336 px. They now open at their usual width, and a reading line longer than four lines ends in
  \"…\" with the whole line in its hover.
- A window opened again opens at what it holds now. Edit group, the Variable window, the function
  window and the other windows that are built once kept the size of the biggest thing they had ever
  held, so a group opened after a long one opened 1,920 px wide with three quarters of it empty.
  They also no longer open a line or two taller than their content.
- The Variable window is as tall as its cards. It asked for 600 px whatever it held, so a plain
  local opened with a third of the window empty under its cards; past 520 px the cards scroll, as
  before.

### Fixed: the Doctor reports on your game, not on the packs installed beside it

- In a project with the behaviour packs copied in, the Doctor said 189 notes about a ten-script
  game. Interop measured pack scripts, the reading ledger sampled nothing but packs (\"Read the first
  12 script(s) of 189\"), Performance asked you to rewrite a pack's distance check, Migration counted
  the packs' sheets, and the Effects and Feedbacks summaries opened a pack. Those sections now read
  your own scripts only, the way the others already did: Interop now measures `player.gd` and
  `enemy.gd`, which it skipped before.
- The packs you have not used yet are one note, not one note each: \"169 pack class(es) are
  referenced by no sheet, scene or autoload ... SimpleAbilitiesBehavior, ... and 164 more\". A pack
  that one of your own `.gd` scripts names now counts as used. Before, only `.tres` sheets, scenes
  and autoloads counted, and `.gd` is the default sheet format.
- Opened in the editor, the Doctor's Docs section listed stale names and empty descriptions in the
  plugin's own pack guides (`Blend-Modes.md`, `Bound-To.md` ...) on a stock install. It now reads
  those only in the plugin's own project. In your game it reads a guide your project keeps
  elsewhere, and when there is none it says nothing.

### Fixed: your own scripts, opened as sheets, read the way the boards draw them

- Your own editor plugin, opened as a sheet, no longer offers \"Enable plugin\" while the project
  already runs it, and Enable plugin never writes over a plugin.cfg the folder already has - its
  author, version and words stay as you wrote them. Only a folder with no plugin.cfg gets one.
- The parameters window opened on a line of your own function no longer refuses it: a local the
  function declares (`direction`), the value its tick hands it (`delta`), a loop's item and a
  parameter are names a field can use, so `direction * speed` is not \"not a variable of Player\" and
  Done is not shut on a line nobody touched. A typo is still caught.
- A `@tool` script says \"Runs in the editor\" as a violet chip on its head bar, beside what it
  extends, with the `@tool` line in its hover; the second head row that said \"runs in the editor
  too\" under the bar is gone.
- A script with an inner `class` no longer says its own members are \"not a variable\": the class's
  members were read as the file's for every row built after the class, so `_start_x` and `_dir` in
  an `enemy.gd` with a `class Patrol` were each flagged under the events that set them. A local
  declared in the function (`var direction := ...`) and a function's own parameter (`body`) are no
  longer flagged either; a name the file really does not declare still is.
- A line about the script itself reads under the script's own name, with the class it extends as the
  icon in front: `spike_ring > Redraw` and `enemy > Wire On Body Entered`, where they read
  `Node2D` and `Area2D`, and an autoload's under its singleton name (`GameState`). The setter
  events of a script with no class_name used to say `Node2D` beside actions that said `spike_ring`
  for the same node. A behaviour pack still names the node it sits on by its class.
- A variable's row wears the tiles for what the script does with it, as the window already did: a
  setter (`set(value):` or `set = _set_x`) puts the on-change tile after the eye, `@onready` the
  on-ready tile, a show-if the shown-only-if tile, each with its hover card and its code line. An
  `@onready` node reads as the variable it is (`Sprite2D sprite = $Sprite2D`), and a preloaded scene
  and an inner class read as their name with their icon - no \"Object\" or \"Class\" word in front.
- A variable's value reads as the code writes it: `float speed = 220.0`, not `220`, and a
  declaration with no value (`var _start_x: float`) shows none instead of `= null`.
- A custom node's tab is its icon and its name, without a second mark in front.

### Fixed: a game you wrote yourself reads as yours, beside the installed packs

Walked in a fresh project with the plugin and its packs installed next to a hand-written game, the
way a user meets it:

- The Doctor no longer says a state \"is declared and no row can reach it\" when the object goes to it
  from a function of its own (`func _update_state()` with the `match` inside), or from a script block
  that says `state = State.RUN`.
- Rename on a function nothing in its own file calls lists the function's head line it is about to
  rewrite, instead of \"rewrites the 0 row(s) in this sheet\".
- The Inspector Designer puts every field after `@export_group(\"Feel\")` under Feel, as Godot draws
  the Inspector, not just the first one; and a field written `var coyote_time := 0.1` reads as the
  float it is, not \"Variant\". The Save studio types such variables the same way, and names an
  autoloaded script with no class name by its global's name (GameState, not game_state).
- The behaviour packs stop standing in for the game: the Sheet map no longer draws the packs' scripts
  (it drew a hundred of them and none of the game's), Find in Project lists the game's own files
  before the packs' matches, and the Doctor's spawning, collision, shapes, lighting, effects,
  streaming, save-memory and missing-input-action checks leave the packs alone, as they already
  left the plugin's own folder.
- Opening a script that is already open (FileSystem, Inspector button, double-click) brings its tab
  forward instead of opening a second tab of the same file.
- The Words page's live preview reads the `_process` word its own table sets (\"Every tick\"), where it
  said \"Every Frame\" beside a table that said otherwise.
- The Debugger's Inspect tab says why a run is silent: with Live Values on and the game running, it
  says the run comes from the saved file and asks for a save (and, on a read-only preview, for Edit
  Events first), instead of telling the reader to turn on the Live Values that was already on.
- Tools ▸ Save Studio opens cleanly in a project without the packs (the README's Quick start): it
  stopped on a script error and searched a folder called \"<null>\" for save slots.

### Fixed: your own scripts read and edit the way the function and match boards draw them

- Clicking a function of a script you just opened (which opens as a read-only preview) hands it to
  Godot's Inspector, as on any sheet; the first change made there unlocks the preview the way picking
  an action does. A scene's view and a file a merge left blocked still only show the read-only card.
- A `match` inside a function reads like any other line: its head on a line of its own leads with
  System, and the question a branch asks before it moves on (`if absf(velocity.x) > 10.0:`, `if
  is_on_floor():`) reads in the sheet's words with its object - \"System | |velocity.x| > 10\",
  \"Player | Is on floor\" - instead of the bare code. Its steps name their object too (\"System | Go to
  Run\"), and a function call used as that question no longer wears the green trigger arrow.
- The windows that write one line show that line the way the sheet draws it, icon first: the
  function window's band is the function icon and \"On function take_damage\" (no \"Function\" word and
  no \"-> nothing\"), the parameter window's band is the parameter's own line (\"amount   int = 1\"), and
  Add action, Compare and Match lead with their object's icon and name.
- A drafted function description keeps a name at the start of a clause as written: \"GameState.coins
  += amount\", never \"gameState\".
- A call to one of the script's own functions reads under the object that owns it, by the function's
  name: \"Player | take_damage with amount 2, critical false\" where it read \"ƒ | Call take_damage
  amount = 2   critical = false\". A sheet that names no object of its own still files it under
  Functions.
- A class written inside a script leads with the class icon and its name, with no \"Class\" word in
  front of it.
- A class written inside a script owns the calls its functions make to each other: \"CoinCounter |
  _walk with node root\", where they read under a nameless \"Functions\".
- A switch branch's step that sets one of the object's own variables reads under the object, as the
  same line reads anywhere else: \"PatrolBehavior | Set mode to WAITING\" under a guarded branch, where
  it read under System.
- Add an event's object page reads a pack by the word its rows read under (\"Bullet\", \"Platformer\")
  instead of its class name, and puts the project's own globals under Your Project as \"GameState
  (autoload)\" after the packs, where they sat at the top beside System with nothing to tell them
  from a pack.
- Add an event's Functions section is headed \"Functions\" alone and lists each function by its name
  as written, under the function mark the sheet draws, with no kind word after it. A hand-written
  script, which publishes none of its functions, lists them all straight under the head instead of
  folding every one of them behind \"+ Helpers\".

### Fixed: the makers read right in a project the plugin was installed into

- The Welcome window no longer offers \"Open the playable showcase scene\" in a project that has no
  showcase to open (an installed plugin brings none, so the button did nothing).
- The New menu and the Start page leave out Level Stats Screen and Skill Tree Screen in a project
  without the HUD Kit and Upgrades packs: those starters are written in the packs' words, and made
  without them every such row came out as an empty template.
- New behaviour's Category leads with \"Its own name\" and starts on it. In a project with no packs
  the list was empty and Create wrote a heading read off no item; with packs installed it picked the
  first pack made for the same kind of object, so a new Patrol was filed with Car.
- Behaviour settings… and Global settings… read a hand-written script's description when a blank
  line sits between `extends` and its `##` line, the way Godot's own script template writes it.
- Properties and actions… reads a property written `@export var enabled := true` as `bool` and
  `true`, not `Variant` and `\"true\"`.
- A variable written `var run_time := 0.0` reads as a number: `run_time += delta` no longer grows the
  amber \"run_time is any - Add to wants a number\" note on an autoload written by hand.
- The Add behaviour list no longer offers a data pack that extends Resource as something to add to
  an object: it sits on its own greyed shelf, and its hover says a sheet reads it from its file.
- Insert new object opens with a free name filled in (Player, or Player2 when the project already
  has a Player), so Insert is ready and the window no longer opens on a red line.
- Sheet Type opens with its help strip on the kind the sheet is; a custom node opened under a strip
  that called it an Event Sheet.
- Every Start page row leads with its icon, as the board draws it: a starter wears the class its
  script extends (Platformer Starter a CharacterBody2D, Keycard Door a StaticBody3D, the menus a
  Control), a recent sheet a file, a showcase a scene, a lesson the help mark. The rows were bare
  words.

### Fixed: a hand-written script opened in a user's own project reads like the sheet boards

Found by installing the plugin into a fresh project and opening scripts nobody wrote for it - a
player, an enemy with a class inside it, an editor plugin and its dock, the shipped packs.

- `if not is_on_floor():` reads as the inverted condition on the object itself - the red cross, the
  script's own name, \"Is on floor\" - instead of \"System is_on_floor()\". The same test written in a
  `match` branch reads the same words on the same object, and so does any other inverted question
  the sheet has words for.
- A verb filed under the node's class (Move and slide, Set velocity Y, Apply gravity, Accelerate x)
  and a call to one of the script's own functions lead with the script's own name and icon
  (\"Player\"), not the class (\"CharacterBody2D\") or a bare \"ƒ\".
- The \"is not a variable\" notes no longer fire on names the file really declares: a loop's item, the
  values a trigger hands its handler (an input handler's `event`) and a parameter list written out
  in full are known names too, and the variable catalog is filled again whenever the sheet being
  read changes, so no sheet read in the same pass answers for another. A variable whose type the
  file never wrote is no longer \"the wrong kind\" for Add to, and a vector or colour can be added to.
  Across the shipped packs and a handful of the plugin's own scripts this took the canvas from
  several hundred false warnings to none in most files.
- An `@onready` node reads as the variable row every member reads as - its type, its name, its
  value - instead of a row led by an \"Object\" word.
- A long pattern chip after a condition gives up its tail before it takes half the conditions lane,
  so a trigger beside it (\"On Plugin Enabled\") is no longer drawn one letter per line.
- An event more than one line tall numbered 10 or higher shows both digits in the margin. The
  number sat in the middle of the margin with a 4px inset either side, which left room for one
  digit, so a `player.gd` with eleven events numbered its last two \"1\" and \"1\".
- A function written inline - a lambda handed to a call, its body read as rows of their own - no
  longer hangs \"is not a variable\" notes under those rows for its own parameters, locals and loop
  items (`insert_after`, `function_resource` in the editor's own helper scripts).
- An opened behaviour pack reads under one name. Its On created trigger, a verb filed under its
  host's class and a line that fills one of its own tables read \"Health\", the word the rest of its
  lines and its tab already use, instead of the class the file is spelled with
  (\"SimpleHealthBehavior\", cut to \"SimpleHe...\" in the object column).
- A folded data type written inside a script names its first fields as they are written
  (\"amount: float = 0.0\") instead of their pieces joined by dashes (\"amount - : - float -> = - 0.0\").

### Fixed: the plugin and the code it writes stop filling the Output and Debugger panels

- Quitting the editor no longer prints \"Parent node is busy adding/removing children\": the bridge
  autoload is added when the plugin is switched on and removed when it is switched off, not on every
  editor start and quit.
- A project without the drawing-prefab pack starts the plugin cleanly: the prefab thumbnail maker no
  longer names the pack's class, which failed to compile and stopped the plugin's start-up half way.
  No plugin file names a class a pack declares any more, and the suite holds it.
- A project's first boot with the plugin no longer prints \"Failed loading resource\" for the workspace
  icon, and its first import no longer warns that the translators' template locale has no
  translation (the template is kept as a plain file).
- The Tools menu item, Properties bar add-on and Context menu item starters write a script that
  loads: their empty `-> void` callback is stubbed with `pass`, not `return null` (\"A void function
  cannot return a value\").
- A handler the compiler writes underscores the parameters its events never read - `func
  _process(_delta: float)`, `func _on_exit_body_entered(_body: Node)` - which is Godot's own cure for
  \"The parameter is never used\", and writes them plain again the moment an event reads one. A file
  opened as a sheet reads `_delta` back as the trigger's `delta`; one whose author left an unread
  parameter plain comes back exactly as written. Every pack and showcase is regenerated with it.
- The Save-choice block reads its boot flag and names an unread `_at_save_game`, so a sheet that
  saves a variable no longer reports an unused class variable and an unused parameter. A file the
  older block wrote still opens with its Save choices and saves back byte for byte.
- Format Time (mm:ss), As Clock Time and Mission Time Left have warning-free successors that read
  exactly the same (`int(seconds / 60.0)` in place of the integer division `int(seconds) / 60`); the
  old rows keep compiling where they are used and offer the new ones.
- The packs' own code is free of the warnings it shipped with: shadowed names, integer division,
  ternaries of two types, an int handed where an enum is expected, signals only ever emitted by
  name, and a variable declared below its use. A frozen ACE parameter that shadows a member keeps
  its name under a commented `@warning_ignore`.
- Nav Agent 3D adds its agent after the host has finished setting up its children, so the FPS arena
  no longer prints \"Parent node is busy setting up children\" and a hundred \"The agent has no parent\".
- The editor-tool starters that leave a callback empty name its parameters `_object`, `_paths` and so
  on, and the command tool starter reads its arguments; a new shared sheet starts the same way.
- The import hook passes over a script that only mentions `_on_files_imported` without loading it or
  warning about it.
- The suite loads every pack, showcase and starter script in a `-d` run and holds the warnings Godot
  reports for them to a short, explained list.

### Added: Platformer Pathfinding learns every way a platformer character gets around

- New link kinds: `double_jump` (when the movement sibling has two jumps; the second fires at the
  apex), `coyote_jump` (run off a ledge, leap in the air), and `fall_through` (one-way tile
  polygons are now jump-through platforms an agent stands on, jumps up through and drops through).
  Slope tiles read as `slope_left` / `slope_right` off their polygons.
- Path strategies (`path_strategy`, Set Path Strategy): balanced, shortest, safest,
  ground_hugging, no_falling, walk_only; Set Max Traversal Drop, path smoothing, a search cap
  (`max_search_nodes`) and Path Fail Reason. `nearest` mode now routes to the closest node it can
  reach instead of failing on a cut-off goal.
- Portals carry ids, costs and tags: Add Instant Portal, Add Gradual Portal (ladders, lifts,
  ziplines - a straight-line move with the movement sibling paused), Add Elevator Stop, Pause /
  Resume / Complete Portal inside the new On Portal Entered, remove / edit / query by id or tag.
- Hazards: Add Circle Hazard, Add Tracked Hazard (follows a node), Remove Hazard by id, a danger
  margin, Hazard At.
- Movement verbs: Start Patrol / Stop Patrol (with end and dwell triggers), Jump Toward, Find Path
  To Nearest Ledge, Find Path To Node Side, Teleport To, Set Enabled, Set Movement Limit, drift
  re-routing.
- Crowds: Crowd Arrivals (goal claims), Multi Agent Avoidance, Idle Separation, and a shared Map
  Tag so a horde builds one graph.
- Maps without a TileMapLayer: Create Obstacle Map, Set Tile State, Scan Collision Bodies
  (StaticBody2D shapes, one-way ones as jump-through), Obstacle Map JSON / Load Obstacle Map JSON,
  Clear Nav Graph; Auto Build builds from the scene's first physics TileMapLayer when a route is
  asked for with no graph.
- Inspection: graph node / link / ledge expressions, waypoint position / cell / action by index,
  path cost / end / target, Log Graph Stats and Set Debug Draw Graph (every link, coloured by
  kind).
- Every existing verb, id and call template is unchanged. The pack's code now lives as real
  GDScript in `tools/pack_builders/src/platformer_pathfinding/`, and the Path Chase showcase gains a
  patrolling Guard that shares the Chaser's graph.

### Fixed: a variable's marks are coloured tiles again, in every look

- Every look draws a variable's marks as 20 px coloured tiles, one per fact - the eye for in the
  Inspector, the slider for kept in a range, the speech bubble for has a description - and keeps
  its `##` note at the end of the row. The look a new sheet gets no longer cuts them down to one
  bare eye; a theme file that still names `variable_quiet_marks` loads and saves as before, and the
  value changes nothing.
- Each tile opens a hover card with its title, one sentence and the code that earns it
  (`@export`, `@export_range(0, 9999, 1)`, `## ...`), the same card the head bar's words open.
- A variable `@export_storage` keeps is hidden from the Inspector, so it no longer wears the eye: it
  wears a grey \"Saved, hidden\" tile instead.
- On a light editor the tiles take the design's inks.
- The Variable window's type list shows each type's editor icon beside it (`int`, `Vector2`,
  `Node2D`, ...), and the parameters window sets the object's icon on a 32 px rounded tile.

### Fixed: the windows and rows say what the boards say

- The Start page's template list draws each shelf once: Keycard Door sits under the one 3D heading
  with the other 3D starters instead of under a second \"3D\" after UI and menus.
- A property's setter event reads its variable and its parameter as written: `On brush_size set`
  with a `new_size` chip, not \"On brush size set\".
- The Parameters window's IN CODE echo never shows a template's `{uid}` mark (Blend Toward read
  `var __blend_{uid}: Tween`; it now reads `__blend_row` until the row is applied and gets its own
  token), and a several-line row keeps the indent of the lines under its `if`.
- The picker's object page shows System with the gear its rows wear, not Godot's Node ring.
- The Debugger's Objects list shows each row's count again (\"6 values\", \"(2)\", \"no report\"): the
  column it sits in had no width. Before a run, the values table says its one sentence across its
  whole width instead of a word a line down the Variable column.
- A group's head reads as its title, its description, what it holds and its switch: the
  `## @ace_group(...)` line no longer runs across it in code colours. The line moves to the counts'
  hover, under \"What this group holds.\".
- Bold words on a row (\"Set group **Boss active**\", a parameter's value) are drawn once in a
  stroked face instead of twice 0.7 px apart, so they read as weight rather than as a smear. The
  advances are the regular face's, so nothing after a bold word moves.
- A tick that runs a switch reads as one sentence on one line in every look: \"System | Every tick
  (physics), match state\", where the default look used to show a muted \"every tick (physics)\" with
  \"match state\" pushed to the far edge. Each branch's steps read as the same line reads in an
  event's action lane, the object it acts on in the column and the property as written:
  `velocity.x = 0` is \"Set velocity.x to 0\", not \"Set x to 0\" with no object.
- The side panel's Objects list says an object's count once, in its right-hand column: a line reads
  \"Tiles  $Tiles\" beside its \"2\" instead of running out of room at \"Tiles  $Tiles · 2 r...\".

### Fixed: every line leads with its icon, and the Variable window wears the row's tiles

- Rows built outside an event's own lanes lead with their object's icon too: a setter's or getter's
  trigger line (`On intensity set` under the script's icon), a switch's branches and their Else (the
  System gear), and a signal the script declares, which now names the script and wears its icon. The
  values a signal hands over are parameter lines - the parameter icon, the value's name and its type
  as written (`spring_name` `String`).
- A physics tick with no condition reads \"System | Every tick (physics)\" in every look; the default
  look no longer shows a bare lowercase \"every tick (physics)\" with no object.
- An enum's head (\"State is one of IDLE, RUN or JUMP\"), a moment's head and a pack's include bar are
  declarations, so they carry no trigger arrow.
- The Variable window carries the row's own tile beside each option that puts one on the row, in the
  row's colours and with the row tile's hover card: the eye beside Editable in the Inspector, the
  speech bubble beside Description, the slider (or the hidden eye) beside an Inspector look that
  draws one, and new tiles for On ready, Show if and the setter's code.
- A variable written under an earlier variable's `@export_group` line opens the window on the group
  and prefix it sits in, and saving it untouched writes no second group line; an empty section field
  says \"none\" instead of a made-up name.
- A local's line spells the type as the code wrote it (`left` `int`), as a parameter's does.
- The colon after a behaviour's name is translated, so French writes `Health : Take damage`.
- Soft Light, a dark sheet, takes the dark hand's trigger green and behaviour amber; every shipped
  and demo theme keeps its marks at 3:1 or better against its rows. The retired
  `behaviour_in_object_column` is no longer listed in the Theme Editor (saved themes still load).
- Resolving a line's icon reads the project's class list once per row build, and a name no theme has
  an icon for is not looked up again on every row.

### Changed: a row says what it is by its icons, never by a word in front of it

- A trigger line carries a green arrow in front of its object icon, in every look; the filled blue
  play circle in the conditions lane and the trigger icon beside the event's number are gone. An
  inverted condition carries a red cross in the same slot instead of the word `not`. A plain
  condition, a per-frame event and every action carry no mark. Each mark explains itself on hover,
  and View > Trigger Icons now switches the arrow.
- Every condition and action line leads with the icon of what it acts on: the node's own Godot class
  icon (its nearest ancestor's when the class has none, the script's `@icon` for the script's own
  object), the System gear and the input devices' glyphs in a neutral grey, on a tile that reads on
  light sheets too. A pick filter, a state's case and an Else get their icon as well, and \"System\" is
  no longer dimmed.
- A function head is a line like any other: the function icon, then `On function juice_tile` straight
  after it - no `Functions` name stands between them, the icon says it. A function's return line reads
  the same way: the function icon, then `Return left`, with no `Functions`, `Function` or `System` in
  its object column. Each parameter is its own line under it - the parameter icon, its name, its type (`index` `int`) -
  and a local variable reads as the local icon, its name, its type and its value. The `Action` /
  `Condition` / `Expression` kind word, the `Function` word, the `returns` chip, the `In Add Action`,
  `Waits`, `Shared` and `Featured` chips, `PARAM` and `Local` are gone from the row; the kind, what the
  function hands back and its flags are in the name's hover card.
- A behaviour's action keeps the node it is mounted on in the object column and starts its words with
  the behaviour's name and a colon, in the behaviour colour: `Spring: Add impulse`. Editor Native no
  longer names the behaviour in the object column (the theme's `behaviour_in_object_column` is
  retired and kept only so saved themes load).
- Editor Native gives every condition line a 16 px mark slot, so marked and unmarked lines start
  their icons at the same x in every look. New theme colours `trigger_mark_color` and
  `behaviour_name_color`; the inverted cross takes the design's red on both hands.

### Fixed: the head bar reads once, in every kind of view, and the sheet under it stays quiet

- The `#` paragraph a script closes on, when the head bar's name card says it as the file's
  description, reads there once: it is no longer drawn again as the sheet's last row. The card's Edit
  description… edits that very note. A comment row written with `[b]`-style markup no longer echoes
  its raw line beside the formatted words.
- A scene view opens on the same 30 px grey head bar a script does - the scene's name, `Scene · root
  Node2D` and how many scripts it uses, with a card naming them - and each script under it reads
  under its own head bar naming the node it sits on and the file it runs, with no folded band stack
  and no Behaviors band among the rows. In the editor, a sheet that is not saved yet opens on a bar
  named \"Untitled\".
- The behaviours card lists each kind's settings and the nodes wearing it, one line per kind, so
  nothing the Behaviors band unfolded to is lost.
- Editor Native's head bar is the neutral grey a step off its canvas, as the default look's is, and
  the name card says which scene a script is attached to in every look (and the node that runs it,
  for a script with no class_name).
- A group events can switch while the game runs keeps its on or off shape and wears a dot beside it,
  so its mark still says how it starts.
- The Functions tab counts calls in one pass over the file, and only while it is on screen: a side
  panel resting closed costs nothing on first open or on an edit.
- The Objects tab writes its headings in sentence case (\"Also in the scene\", \"Input\"); each heading's
  count and what it is for are its hover, and an object's behaviour nodes sit in one closed
  Behaviours fold under it.
- The side panel's tab titles are translated, Picker preview included, and the variables card says
  its bookkeeping sentence whole, so a language agrees its verb with the count.
- `EventSheetOpenSheetsDock` is back as a deprecated class the workspace no longer places, so a tool
  that names it still parses; the tabs above the sheet and the Start page's recent sheets replace it.
- An editor started with `--script` (a render harness, a tool) no longer counts as the project's first
  run, so it no longer switches the project to Simple Mode; the test runner fails any test that
  changes the editor's project metadata, and puts the reader's back.

### Changed: a sheet opens on one head bar, with no head card, no banner and no Behaviors band

- An opened file used to open under a title strip, a head card (\"CarouselOfJuice - Custom Node ·
  extends Node2D · @ 4 knobs\") and a full-width blue banner with Edit Events and Open in Godot Script
  Editor; then a folded head row that hid the variables, the file's closing paragraph hoisted to the
  top, and a Behaviors band among the rows. It now opens on ONE 30 px head bar, the same in every look and for every kind of file: the class icon (or the file's
  own `@icon`), the name as written, what kind of script it is (\"Custom node · extends Node2D\",
  \"Script\", \"Behaviour\", \"Global (autoload)\"), and on the right the counts - variables, events and
  behaviours - each with a dotted rule under it.
- Hovering a word on the bar (or clicking it) opens its card. The name's card says where the file
  lives, its description, that a script opened as a sheet stays plain GDScript and how many events,
  functions and code blocks it has, with Open in Script Editor and Show in FileSystem (a behaviour's
  or a global's adds its settings form; a sheet being edited adds Edit description…). The variables
  card names them and says how many the Inspector shows; the bookkeeping members the compiler writes
  (`__...`) are never counted. The behaviours card lists each kind with its count - a click opens
  that behaviour as a sheet - and offers Add behaviour….
- The rows below are the file's, in its order: the variables stay where they are declared (read with
  their Inspector facts, an `@export_group` as its own section), the signals stay where they are,
  and the paragraph a file ends on stays at its end.
- The banner is shown for one state only: a file an unfinished merge blocks. The first edit still
  unlocks a preview, and the status bar names the opened file instead of repeating the banner. The
  Sheet menu gains Open in Script Editor and Show in FileSystem, and a behaviour's Sheet | Code |
  Side by side switch moves to the toolbar.
- The look a new user gets (the one that matches the editor) paints the head bar a flat grey a step
  off the canvas.


### Fixed: a script opened as a sheet reads closer to the design boards

- Every function reads where the file writes it: the closed Helpers bar that gathered a script's
  unpublished functions (its `flash`, `distance_to`, `_bake`, the handlers it wires) is gone. Only the
  two helpers the head already says - a constructor that stores its object, a test's harness - stay
  out of the view.
- Export sections draw on a script opened as a sheet as they do on an edited one: `@export_category`
  as a band, each `@export_group` as a strip with its prefix tag, `@export_subgroup` inside its group,
  `@export_group(\"\")` as the line the group ends on. A `##` line kept above a member sits in that
  member's strip. The blank lines between two blocks cost one block gap, not a tall empty row each.
- Names read exactly as written on every surface: `bullet_speed`, `Call rebuild_preview`, and a script
  without a class_name is the object `gun_turret`. The respelling lens stays in View for whoever turns
  it on. A function hands its value back with `Function ▸ Return x`.
- `current = (current + 1) % points.size()` keeps its own words instead of reading as the weapon
  wheel's \"Switch to next weapon\".
- A behaviour's `host` reads once, as the variable the file declares.
- A group head wears one mark (on, off, or switchable) instead of a ring and a switch, and a group
  that is off is dimmed instead of struck through.
- The look a new sheet gets lists variables the way the Inspector does (type, name, value, note, icon
  tiles), as Editor Native does.
- Opening Properties on a variable the file declares no longer warns that its own name is taken.
- The help strip speaks for the selection on the tab in front of you: switching tabs no longer leaves
  the last tab's variable words in it.
- The Sheet map names each box by its file as written (`player.gd`, `level_2 (scene)`) instead of a
  respelled title (\"Player\", \"Level 2 (scene)\").
- The `#` paragraph a script ends on, under its last function, reads under that function at the foot
  of the sheet, where the file writes it, instead of above the function it closes under.


### Changed: the side panel rests as a thin rail and opens to Objects and Functions tabs

- The always-open column beside the sheet (Open Sheets, a filter, Objects / Project tabs, a USED IN
  THIS SHEET list and a Functions fold, each with its own header and splitter) is now a side panel
  that rests closed as a 30 px rail with one button. Opened, it is two tabs styled like Godot's own
  docks - a strip of flat tabs, a filter, then plain rows with an icon, a name and a count:
  - **Objects** lists what the file uses first, without a heading, then the folds for the rest of
    the scene, the Input Map actions and the globals; the sort button sits beside the filter, and
    the Project bar (when it is on) folds in at the foot of the tab.
  - **Functions** lists the sheet's functions with how many times the file calls them, then the
	functions still written as code, marked `code`; ＋ beside the filter adds one, and right-click
	still deletes a sheet function.
  - A behaviour pack adds an **Anatomy** tab, and the **Picker preview** is a tab while the picker
	is open.
- Open Sheets is gone: the tabs above the sheet are the open sheets, and recently closed sheets are
  on the Start page. View > Panels picks a tab (choosing the ticked one closes the panel) and View >
  Side Rail opens or closes it.
- Whether the panel is open, which tab it shows and how wide it was dragged are remembered per
  project in the editor's project metadata. A project that remembered the old column starts with
  the panel closed.

### Changed: no Conditions | Actions band above the sheet

- The band naming the two lanes, with its drag handles, is gone in every look. The two sides are
  told apart by their colour and their kind labels, and the lanes are resized by dragging the line
  between them on any event: the pointer shows ↔ over it, and the width is remembered on the sheet's
  style exactly as the band's drag remembered it. View > Reset Lane Split still puts the theme's own
  split back.

### Fixed: an Every X Seconds timer no longer counts while its event is off in a `.gd` sheet

- An event switched off in a `.gd` sheet (or a row of a group that is off and cannot be switched)
  stayed in the file behind a false guard, but a stateful condition's bookkeeping line
  (`__every_... += get_process_delta_time()`) was still written at the function's own indent, above
  the guard, so the timer went on counting every frame while the event was off. The line is now
  written behind the same guard, as the event with no conditions it reads back as: under the event's
  `# @off` (or its group's `# @group:` line) and an `if false:` of its own. The saved script parses,
  it reopens off, and an untouched save writes the same bytes.

### Fixed: a stateful condition switched off in a `.gd` sheet says it was left out

- A condition that keeps state of its own (Every X Seconds, Trigger Once and their kind) has no term
  it can stay in while it is off, so a `.gd` save leaves it out of the file - and it did so without
  a word. The save now adds a warning naming the condition, beside the one an unresolvable
  condition already gets.

### Fixed: an action whose code is a block, switched off in a `.gd` sheet, can be switched back on

- An action that is off is written as its own lines behind `# @off: `, and opening the file read
  those lines back one at a time; an action whose template is a block (a loop with its body under
  it) has a first line that is no action, so the whole run stayed comments whose text began with
  `@off: ` and could never be ticked back on. The run is now read a statement at a time: a block
  comes back as a GDScript row, off, that writes the same loop when it is switched on again, and a
  run a family claims comes back as that family's row. A GDScript row switched off in a `.gd` sheet
  is now kept the same way, behind `# @off: `, instead of being left out of the file.

### Fixed: a `.tres` sheet's companion writes the group header it always wrote

- The header of an off group that cannot be switched gained `enabled=false` in every compile, so a
  `.tres` sheet's regenerated companion (which still leaves such a group out) changed a line it
  never used to. The field is written only where it is read back: in a sheet that is its own `.gd`,
  and for a group that can be switched.

### Fixed: a class description edited above a comment keeps the file's own layout

- An edited class description whose `##` block was ended by a `# comment` line gained a blank line
  between the block and the comment on save; a block that is already there is now replaced in
  place, and only a new block gains the blank line that keeps it off the next member. A block that
  opens on a bare `##` no longer reads as changed when nothing was edited, so an untouched file
  spelled `##text` saves byte for byte instead of being rewritten as `## text`.

### Changed: CI names the file a red contracts walk failed on

- The suite-and-contracts step runs under `bash -e`, so a red `verify_sheets` ended the step before
  its summary, and the job failed with exit code 1 naming no file. The walk's exit code is now
  caught and answered at the end of the step, after the failing lines are printed.

### Fixed: a `##` line inside a string is no longer read as the class description

- Opening a `.gd` whose own `extends` line has no doc block, but which holds a string with an
  `extends` line and a `##` line under it (a script that writes another script), read the string's
  `##` line as the class description, and saving the untouched file then wrote that line under the
  file's own `extends`. Only the file's first `extends` line is read for the description now, the
  same line the save writes it under, so such a file opens with no description and saves byte for
  byte.

### Fixed: a comment switched off in a `.gd` sheet stays in the file

- A comment row that was switched off was left out of the compile, so in a `.gd` sheet saving
  deleted the note from the file. In a sheet that is its own `.gd` it is now written behind
  `# @off: `, and opening the file brings it back as the same comment, switched off - a note at the
  sheet's top level and a note among an event's actions alike. Turning it back on writes the file it
  was before. A `.tres` sheet still leaves a comment that is off out.

### Fixed: an action or condition switched off in a `.gd` sheet stays in the file

- A cell whose box was unticked was left out of the compile, so in a `.gd` sheet (the sheet's only
  copy) saving deleted the action or condition from the file. In a sheet that is its own `.gd` an
  action that is off is now written as its own lines behind `# @off: `, comments that never run; a
  condition that is off stays in the header as a term whose answer it cannot change -
  `(true or <it>)` among And terms, `(false and <it>)` among Or terms, and `(true or <it>)` for each
  when every condition of an Or event is off. Opening the file reads each back as the same cell,
  switched off, and an untouched save writes the same bytes. A condition that keeps state of its own
  (Every X Seconds and its kind) still has nowhere to keep it and is left out, with a warning that
  names it. A `.tres` sheet still
  leaves cells that are off out of the game.

### Fixed: an event switched off in a `.gd` sheet stays in the file

- An event whose box was unticked was left out of the compile. A `.tres` sheet still holds it, but
  a `.gd` sheet has no other copy, so saving deleted the event from the file - a handler whose only
  event was off vanished whole. In a sheet that is its own `.gd` an event that is off is now written
  behind a constant false guard under a `# @off` line (`if false and score > 10:`, `if false:` for an
  event with no conditions, `elif false:` for an else row), so it stays in the file and never runs.
  Opening the file brings it back as the same event, switched off - at the top of a handler, in a
  group, as a sub-event, as an else row or in a function's body - and turning it back on writes the
  file it was before. A `.tres` sheet still leaves an event that is off out of the game.

### Fixed: a class description edited in a `.gd` sheet reaches the file

- A `.gd` sheet's class description is read from the `##` block under `extends`, but the save wrote
  that block back exactly as the file opened, so an edit made in the Sheet Type dialog or the head
  band was dropped and gone on reopen. The save now rewrites the block when it no longer reads as the
  sheet's description, adds one under `extends` when the file had none (with a blank line after it,
  so it can never read as the next member's doc comment), and removes it when the description is
  cleared. A block left untouched keeps its own spelling byte for byte.

### Fixed: the first group added to a `.gd` sheet writes its header where it reads as one

- A `.gd` with no groups yet put the first group's `## @ace_group(...)` line straight under
  `extends`, where it read as the class's doc comment: the sheet reopened with the header as its
  class description. In a file that opens with annotations (a behaviour pack's `## @ace_` lines and
  `@icon(...)`) the header went to the very top, and a switchable group's flag `var` landed above
  `@icon`, so the saved script did not parse. The header now goes under the class's opening lines
  and its doc block, past the blank line that closes them, on a line of its own; and a header that
  does sit under `extends` (a sheet-native compile writes it there) is no longer read as the class
  description.

### Fixed: a region drawn in a function body is still a region after the file reopens

- A region drawn around rows of a function's body was written into the body (`#region Setup` ...
  `#endregion`), but opening the `.gd` again read the two lines back as comment rows inside the
  neighbouring events, so after one save the region could no longer be folded, renamed or removed.
  They now read back as the region's two fence rows, in the same place, styled ones included, and an
  untouched save still writes the same bytes. A handler's own hand-written `#region` lines keep
  reading as the comments they always were, and so does a body whose fence sits under a blank line.
  A hand-written function the file marks as a sheet function is read exactly as one the sheet wrote:
  its top-level `#region` / `#endregion` lines with no blank line above them open as region rows
  (deliberately - a region row folds, renames and saves as the same two lines), and the file still
  saves byte for byte.

### Fixed: a group that is off and cannot be switched keeps its rows in a `.gd` sheet

- A group with Active On Start off and Can Be Switched At Runtime off was left out of the compile,
  which is right for a `.tres` sheet's generated script (the `.tres` still holds the rows) but lost
  the rows of a `.gd` sheet: only the group's header survived the save, and the file reopened as an
  empty group that was on. In a `.gd` sheet the rows are now written behind a constant
  `if false:` guard, so they stay in the file and never run, and the header says
  `enabled=false`. Opening the file brings back the same off group with its rows (a group inside it
  is held off with it, a switchable one included), turning it back on writes the file it was before,
  and a file saved untouched is byte for byte the same. A `.tres` sheet still leaves the group out.

### Fixed: a region inside a group is kept when the sheet is saved

- A `#region` a group holds used to be dropped when a `.gd` sheet was saved, and written at the top
  of the class (wrapping nothing) when a `.tres` sheet compiled. A group's events are written inside
  the trigger functions, so its fences are now written there too, at the events' indent: the opener
  just before the event it wraps, the closer just after the last. Opening the file brings them back
  as the same fences in the same group, styled ones (`## @ace_region(...)`) included, and saving it
  untouched writes the same bytes. A region whose events have two triggers opens in one function and
  closes in the other; the script still parses.

### Fixed: editing a group in a `.gd` sheet reaches the file

- A `.gd` opened as a sheet kept its groups' `## @ace_group(...)` header lines exactly as it opened
  them, so every change to a group there - colour, description, folded, who runs it, which mode,
  rename, Active On Start, Can Be Switched At Runtime, a group added or deleted - was left out of the
  save and gone on reopen. Turning on Can Be Switched At Runtime was worse: the rows were guarded by
  `if __group_x_active:` with no `var __group_x_active` anywhere, so the saved script did not parse.
- Saving now rewrites the header line of each group that changed (an untouched header, including one
  spelled by hand, stays byte for byte as written), adds the header of a new group, removes the header
  of a deleted one, and keeps the switch flag in step: added when a group becomes switchable, renamed
  with its group, its starting value following Active On Start, removed when the group can no longer
  be switched. A renamed group's `# @group:` row tags follow the new name, so it reopens whole.

### Changed: a group head reads the same in every look, and a group's colour and switch say what is true

- One group head in every look, Editor Native included: one line with the fold arrow, the folder
  mark, the title, the muted description, the counts and the switch mark (◍ ◌ ◎). Editor Native's
  two-line head and its Active tick box are retired, and so is the `group_head_two_line` theme token
  (a saved theme that still names it loads as before; the line is ignored). A long description now
  shortens with an ellipsis instead of being cut mid-letter, and reads whole on hover.
- A group's bracket ends in a slim tick in the group's colour where its body ends, so the bottom of a
  group is as visible as its side. The \"+ Add event to '...'\" row at the end of a group is
  translated now.
- The colour a group shows is the colour its `.gd` keeps: the `## @ace_group(...)` header now writes
  `color=\"#D9534F\"` (the colour you picked) instead of the free-text tag, so a `.gd` sheet reopens in
  the colour it was saved in. A header that only carries the older tag (`color=\"orange\"`) still reads
  back as that tag and re-saves byte for byte.
- A group that is off at start but can be switched at runtime is in the game now, behind its flag
  starting false (`var __group_boss_active: bool = false`), so Set group active can turn it on. It
  used to be left out entirely, which left the action nothing to reach. Only a group that is off AND
  cannot be switched is left out, as before.
- The Edit group window says that before you press Apply: off and not switchable shows the warning
  \"Boss is left out of the game entirely, and Set group active cannot reach it\" in place of the code
  line it used to show for it, and off and switchable shows the flag line the compiler really writes.
  The head's switch reads \"Off at start; events can switch it on.\" for such a group.
- The group head's menu has \"Can Be Switched At Runtime\" beside \"Active On Start\", ticked at the
  group's state; choosing it is one undo step. The group's own items read Edit Group…, Active On
  Start, Can Be Switched At Runtime, Runs On, Group Color…, Turn Into Region, Ungroup; Add Local
  Variable… and the two folds follow under a separator.
- An off-at-start, switchable group keeps both halves in a `.gd` sheet: its header writes
  `enabled=false` (only for that case, so every other header is byte for byte what it was), and
  reopening the file brings the group back off and switchable. A `.gd` whose switchable groups used
  to open as plain code blocks, because nothing took their `if __group_x_active:` guard back off, now
  opens with the groups and their events as rows.

### Changed: Set group active picks the group by its name, and a rename takes the rows with it

- Set group active and Is group active pick their group from a dropdown of the sheet's groups (and
  the groups of the sheets it includes) by the name you gave them, switchable ones first, each with
  its description and what picking it does: \"Off at start, switchable\", or \"Not switchable: picking
  it turns on Can be switched at runtime\". Picking a group that is not switchable turns its switch on
  in the same undo step as the row. The value the row stores is still the guard token it always
  was, so older sheets and compiled games are unchanged; a row naming no group keeps its value as a
  choice of its own. The separate Make switchable button is gone.
- Renaming a group (the Edit group window, the name on the head, the properties bar) rewrites every
  Set group active and Is group active that names it, in this sheet as one undo step and in the
  project sheets that include it, and the rename receipt says how many rows followed. Replace All
  in the find bar renaming a group takes the rows along too, in the same undo step.
- A group's name now drives its switch's token: a rename moves both together. A group from an older
  sheet whose two name fields differ keeps its saved token until it is renamed, and the picker shows
  that token (\"Its switch is written as boss_fight.\"). The Edit group window and the properties bar
  show the name the head shows, and the properties bar's rename now changes the head too.

### Fixed: a region folds from its menu, says what a group would lose, and keeps quiet about a broken fence

- Open / Close Region on a region's menu did nothing: it shared the group's item, and the group's
  handler only folds groups. It has its own item now and folds the region exactly as its ▶ mark and
  Collapse All Regions do; folded, the row reads `#region Spawning … #endregion` with its count. On a
  fence that never closes the item is greyed, since there is nothing to fold.
- Turn Into Region used to be refused, with an English reason glued to the item, for a group with
  locals, a runtime switch or that was off, and it dropped Runs on and Runs in without a word. It is
  always offered now. A plain group turns at once (Ctrl+Z turns it back); a group carrying something
  a region cannot asks first, in the reader's language, with one sentence per thing that would go -
  the locals by name, the switch, off at start, who it runs on, which mode it runs in - and Turn Into
  Region or Cancel.
- Surround with a region wrapped everything from the first selected row to the last, so a row left
  out of the selection ended up inside. A selection that skips rows now asks: include the skipped
  rows, make one region per run, or cancel. Rows inside a group get the region inside that group,
  instead of being skipped.
- A `#region` without its `#endregion` (or the other way round) grew an amber note row inside the
  sheet. The fence row now only wears the quiet amber state every other finding uses; selecting it
  puts the sentence on the help strip with the one fix beside it - Add #endregion (written after the
  last row before the next head) or Remove #endregion - and the Doctor's region-fence check lists it
  as before.
- Every group and region item in the row menu (Edit Group…, Runs On, Turn Into Region, Rename
  Region…, Open / Close Region and the rest) goes through the translation catalog, filled in all
  eight languages. Quick questions gained a second safe answer beside OK for the surround question.

### Fixed: what the reader adds to a connect read in place is written, and a class that moves bytes stays code alone

- A connect read where it sits is an ordinary event on the canvas, but saving rebuilt it from its
  actions alone: a condition, pick, node target or local the reader added to it was dropped without
  a word. They are written now, inside the function the connect hands over (`if score < 3:` above
  its lines), exactly as a trigger's conditions guard its handler. An else has no place on a
  connect, so one set there is named in the save's warnings instead of vanishing.
- A connect whose function holds ONE line with the `)` glued to it (`pressed.connect(func() -> void:`
  then `start_game())`) was taken for an empty function and stayed code. It opens as an event like
  the longer ones.
- When putting every class of a file back did not reproduce the file, every class in it went back to
  code. The classes are now tried again one at a time, so only the one that moved a byte stays code
  and the others still read.
- The Doctor warns when a project holds two or more compute shaders (`.glsl`, which Post Kit ships
  six of) and imports on several threads, Godot's default: Godot 4.7 can crash part way through
  importing them side by side and leave every file after them unimported. The warning names the
  switch (Project Settings > Editor > Import > Use Multiple Threads), and the Post Kit guide says the
  same.

### Added: a function written inside a call reads as what it is

- A signal connected to a function written inside the connect (`start_button.pressed.connect(func()
  -> void:` and the lines under it) opens as its own event: the signal is the trigger
  (`start_button On Pressed`) and the function's lines are its actions. At the top of `_ready` it
  stands beside On created as before; anywhere else - lower in `_ready`, in any other function, inside
  a branch - it is read where it sits, nested under the lines before it, and written back exactly
  there. The typed spelling (`func() -> void:`) and the `)` glued to the last body line, which is
  how nearly every connect is written, are read now too. Saving writes the same bytes back; a connect
  that cannot come back exactly stays the line it was.
- A function handed to any other call over several lines (`sort_custom`, `filter`, `map`, a callback,
  a deferred call) is one action row: the call, read like any one-line call, with the function as one
  chip (`enemies Sort custom  func(a, b): a.position.x < b.position.x`), its first line and `…` when
  it is longer. Hovering the row lists every line as written. A sort or a total that the one-line
  reading already knows reads the same way (`Sort shop by price (lowest first)`). Display only.
- A menu answered by such a lambda (`popup.id_pressed.connect(func(id: int) -> void:` and a
  `match id:`) still reads each arm as the item it answers.
- Over the plugin's own 1,125 scripts, 424 connects now open as events, the lines still drawn as code
  fall from 1,554 to 485, and the rows that say nothing of their own from 991 to 883. Every file
  still saves back byte for byte.

### Fixed: a fresh copy of this repository imports completely the first time

- The project now imports on one thread (`editor/import/use_multiple_threads=false`). Godot 4.7 can
  crash when it imports several compute-shader `.glsl` files at once, and the Post Kit pack and its
  builder's sources hold twelve. A first `--import` of a fresh copy stopped with an access violation
  just after the translation CSVs in 6 of 17 tries, leaving every texture and icon unimported - which
  is why the first full test run after a fresh import failed all 141 `pack_icons_test`
  \"icon.svg is imported\" checks and the drawing stamp texture, and the next run passed. With one
  thread: 0 of 6. A fresh import takes about 90 s instead of about 55 s; later imports are small.
  `import_threads_test` pins the setting.

### Fixed: three small things the test runs tripped over

- With no editor around (tests, preview harnesses), the plain UndoRedo the sheet falls back to was
  handed the editor's (object, method, arguments) spelling, so every edit printed \"Method expected 1
  argument(s), but called with 3\" and kept no undo step. It is handed one bound Callable now, and the
  step is recorded without being run again, since the edit is already applied.
- The Lift Report window has the same body as every other window: Esc closes it and it grows to fit.
- `tools/run_tests_parallel.ps1` empties `.godot/test_logs/` before it starts. The red-run report
  reads every log there, so an `-Iterate` run that picked nothing kept the previous run's
  iterate.txt and named its failures under shards that had all passed.

### Fixed: two timing tests that went red for the machine's load, not the code

- `self_expressions_test` timed twenty Self-section refreshes against a literal 2000 ms but declared
  no budget, so it ran inside a shard beside seven other Godot processes and measured 2,977 and
  3,793 ms. It declares `SCALE_REFRESH_BUDGET_MS` now and runs in the serial tail. Alone, outside
  OneDrive, it takes 752-819 ms at ff3cf143 and 742-853 ms at 045246bb (the code did not get
  slower); the budget is three times the median, 2,400 ms. `shard_split_test` now fails any test
  that starts a clock without declaring a budget.
- `huge_project_budget_test` timed the Add picker's FIRST tree build, which also reads every file the
  tree shows: 2,375 ms on the first run after an import and 613 ms on the next, with the same
  first-run red at the commit before the wave. The first build is now held to its own ceiling
  (`PICKER_FIRST_OPEN_BUDGET_MS`, 7,100 ms, three times the cold median) and the open budget times
  the second build: 593-673 ms before the wave and 613-619 after, so 1,840 ms.

### Added: a class inside a script, and a value over several lines, read as rows

- A class written inside a script (`class Hit:` with variables and functions under it) reads as one
  full-width \"Class Hit\" head with its members under it, folding like a group: its variables are
  variable rows and its functions are function events, read exactly as they would be in a file of
  their own (a class inside it is a Class of its own). Hovering the head says how other code makes
  one (`Hit.new()`); double-clicking it turns the class back into the code block it is written as.
  The file still saves back byte for byte. A class that cannot come back exactly stays code on its
  own, and the rest of the file still reads; a class of fields alone keeps its Data type reading.
- A variable whose value runs over several lines is ONE variable row with the whole value on one
  line (`top_speed = (base_speed * boost + wind)`), and a local declaration written that way in a
  function is one action. Saving writes the same line breaks back until the value is edited; an
  edited value is written as typed. Lines with a comment inside them stay code.
- A list or table declared at the top of a script reads as a variable row folded to its count,
  `Array[Vector2] spawn_points = 3 entries`; a double-click (or the fold arrow) opens its entries as
  the rows under it, each still editable in place.
- A table or list inside a function with notes written among its entries folds to one row like any
  other: a note is never an entry, and the row's hover keeps the notes under the entries.
- Opening the plugin's own scripts: 65 of its 67 classes now read as rows, 2,703 of their 2,930
  lines (none did before). Declarations whose value runs over several lines that still read as code
  fell from 1,324 to 300 (5,658 lines to 2,540), and rows that say nothing of their own from 991 to
  306 (0.4% of rows to 0.1%).
- A file with classes inside it takes longer to finish reading in the background, since every
  function in its classes is now read as well (the first paint of the file is unchanged).

### Fixed: where the readings of a function inside a call, a long value and a class met

- A declaration whose value hands a function written over several lines to a call
  (`var changed: bool = _dock._perform_undoable_sheet_edit(\"Apply Cell Edit\", func() -> bool:` ...)
  was joined onto one line like a long sum, which reads the function's indented lines as one and
  would have written them back on one line after an edit. A value with a function inside it is no
  longer joined: the undo funnel reads as its one undoable step again, and any other call reads as the
  call with the function as a chip.
- A function whose first line is a connect written with a function inside it
  (`button.pressed.connect(func() -> void:` ...) fell back to one block of code: the connect was
  taken for one at the top of `_ready`. It now reads as an event inside that function, and the
  function reads as events again (`_connect_view_signals` of the split view, 51 lines, is one).
- Turning a class back into code named the compiler in a file the first sheet open builds; it now
  reaches it by path, so the first open loads nothing new.

### Fixed: shaping a field, walked end to end in the editor

- Clicking an option in \"Shape this field\", pressing Choose or Apply, or pressing ▲ in the Inspector
  Designer no longer tries to free the control being clicked (Godot refused with \"Object is locked\"
  and left it orphaned). The choice now lands right after the click.
- A script open as a sheet keeps its own path after an edit. Every edit used to hand the script's path
  to the sheet, so `load()` of the script returned the sheet, and a resource made from the script (a
  goblin.tres made from EnemyStats) could not be given it.
- The Inspector's \"Shape <Class>…\" link shows the sheet's tab when it is already open, unsaved edits
  and undo included, instead of reading the file again into a second tab. Taking the last Show when
  back after following the link now removes @tool again, as it does without the link.
- \"Groups show as: Tabs\" turns the Designer's own list into tabs too: a tab per group, only the open
  group's fields under it, and the tab follows the picked field.
- The help strip under a variable shown only while a tick box is on says so:
  \"shield_amount: shown only when has_shield is on.\" (or \"read-only when ... is off.\").
- A Look without limits (Plain number, a dropdown, flags...) on a field that stays within limits now
  says it also turns Stay within limits off on the Check tab, instead of changing that tab quietly.
- Side by side never pairs the last field of a group with the first field of the next one (or across
  a subgroup line), and the Layout tab says why it is not offered: the next field starts another
  group, or the two are not both numbers.
- With groups as tabs, a heading that holds another plugin's own controls is no longer hidden, and the
  picked group's folded heading opens through Godot's own unfold instead of a simulated click.
- A dropdown filled by a function no longer closes itself when the Inspector refreshes with nothing
  changed.

### Added: a block kept as code on purpose says why

- Quoted lines joined into one text (`block.code = \"\\n\".join(PackedStringArray([` followed by a line
  of GDScript per entry, as every pack builder writes its pack) read as one row, `text` and its first
  lines, even with notes written among them. Its hover says why it stays code and shows the lines as
  written, the notes in their places. Opening the plugin's own scripts, that took the rows saying
  nothing of their own from 1,998 to 991 (0.8% of rows to 0.4%).
- A note written among those quoted lines goes with them: it is no longer a row of its own on the
  canvas, and reads in the row's hover in its place among the lines (the hover shows the first twelve
  lines and says how many more). This holds in any file with that shape, not only the plugin's own.
- A text between triple quotes, and every line inside one, says on hover why it stays code.
- Display only: every file still opens and saves back byte for byte.

### Changed: the function Inspector shows the code each choice stands for

- Every dropdown choice of a selected function says, under its sentence, the line it writes or the
  call it asks for: `-> float` for a Returns type, `amount: int = 0` for a parameter Type,
  `await take_damage(amount, critical)` for Can wait, `static func ...` for One for the script,
  `## @ace_hidden` or `## @ace_action` for In Add Action and `## @ace_category(\"Player\")` for a
  Category. The note kept under the field is still the sentence alone.
- The two settings are labelled Waits (async) and Shared (static), the words a script uses for them.
- A parameter whose Type changes starts its default again from the new type's own value
  (`amount: int = 1` turned to String reads `= \"\"`, a Color starts as `Color.WHITE`), in the same
  undo step; a required parameter stays required.
- Text still being typed into a function's field is written to the sheet it was typed on when you
  switch tabs, never to a same-named function of the tab that comes up.
- An empty Name says why a function needs one: so callers can find it.

### Added: pick a row of a data table, and big lists in the table look

- A new Inspector look, **Pick a row of a data table**, for an int:
  `@export_custom(PROPERTY_HINT_NONE, \"eventsheet:table_row:drops:name\")`. The Inspector shows a
  dropdown of the `drops` rows by their `name` cell, with \"row 1 of drops\" under it. The file still
  stores the row's number, so `drops[drop_row]` reads it in the game, and without the plugin the
  property is Godot's plain number field. A number past the last row is never moved onto another
  row: the field shows \"row 5\" in quiet amber and says the row is gone. The line reopens on the
  same look and saves back byte for byte; a marker short of its column stays the line it was.
- The table look gains what a long list needs: a search box, a click on a column's name to sort by
  it (number columns as numbers, a second click flips it, `#` goes back to the file's order), and
  pages of eight rows (\"Rows 1-8 of 23\", ‹ ›). They only change what is shown: the list keeps its
  order in the file. A cell left empty is a quiet amber cell whose hover says which column of which
  row is missing, the bar under the grid counts the rows that have one, and a list kept in a .tres
  of its own offers **Open as a table**, which opens that file in the table view.

### Added: three new shapes a field can take in the Inspector

- Groups as tabs: `# @inspector_tabs` shows every `@export_group` of the script as a tab, so a long
  resource fits on one screen. Without the plugin the groups are Godot's ordinary headings.
- Side by side: `# @inspector_side_by_side` above a number puts it and the number declared after it
  on one line (min and max, width and height). Without the plugin they are two lines.
- Pick from a list a function gives: `# @inspector_choices attack_names` above a String makes it a
  dropdown of the words `attack_names()` returns - a list of words, or a Dictionary of word to the
  sentence that describes it. The file still stores the plain word. It needs a @tool script, like
  Checked by a function; otherwise the field is Godot's plain text field.
- Each one is a variable attribute the compiler writes as that comment line and the importer reads
  back, byte for byte, so a hand-typed file opens as the same choice.
- A sheet that is not a tool sheet but carries Show if / Lock unless is now told so when it
  compiles: Godot only asks a @tool script's `_validate_property`, so the field would never hide.

### Added: shape a field in the Inspector Designer

- Clicking a field in the Inspector Designer picks it, and the \"Shape this field\" panel beside the
  list offers six tabs - Look, Layout, Show when, Check, Help and Button. Each tab names the choice
  the field wears now and lists only the options that fit its type, every option with the sentence
  that says what it does. Showing an option shows the code it writes into the script and what the
  field is without the plugin.
- Choosing an option writes it at once and the Designer shows the field wearing it. Every choice is
  one undo step that rewrites only the lines that choice owns, and choosing the default takes them
  out again, so the file is byte for byte what it was. Show when (only when a tick box is on, or
  read-only while it is off) is written as Godot's own `_validate_property` and turns the script into
  a `@tool` script; taking the last one back removes the `@tool` it added. An option with details (a
  bar's limits, a note's sentence, a button's function) keeps its fields under the code, and Apply
  changes them.
- \"Groups show as\" above the list offers Sections and Tabs, and the panel offers the new shapes
  beside the old ones: Side by side in Layout, and Pick a row of a data table and Pick from a list
  a function gives in Look.
- Code typed by hand reads back as the same choice when it has the same shape: a `_validate_property`
  that hides or locks a field shows as its Show when. A field shaped by code in any other shape says
  \"shaped by your own code\" and is read-only in the panel, so nothing is rewritten behind your back.
- Two doors open it: a selected exported variable's help strip offers \"Shape in the Inspector…\"
  (in place of \"Change look…\", whose looks are the panel's Look tab), and Godot's Inspector shows one
  small \"Shape <Class>…\" link at the top of a resource or node whose script is a sheet. A fourth door
  on the strip waits in its \"…\" menu.

### Fixed: the function Inspector's refusals say only what is true

- Shared is refused only when the code the function compiles to reads a variable each object has
  its own copy of. A verb whose own template holds `{speed}` or `{amount}`, a parameter or local
  with a variable's name, text in quotes, a comment, a constant and a static no longer count, so a
  sheet with a variable called `speed` can make its functions shared again.
- A Default is judged only for the types the line can describe (true or false, numbers, text,
  Vector2, Vector3, Color, lists and dictionaries). `[]` for a PackedStringArray, `\"a/b\"` for a
  NodePath and `Vector2i(1, 2)` for a Vector2 are written instead of being refused as \"not null\".
- A Name with letters outside a to z (`größe`) says what is really wrong: GDScript takes it, but the
  sheet would open that function as code from then on.
- A save-state table handed to `return` (the Upgrades pack's `save_state`) keeps its skill tree
  chip again: the pattern walk reads a table's entries as the code they are.

### Fixed: the plugin's own scripts open as rows, not as code

- A doc comment with a blank `##` line between its paragraphs no longer sends the whole file back to
  code. It was read as a stray block above the function and written twice; the row builder's 563
  functions, and seven other files, now open as functions.
- Two blank lines after a multi-line `const` list or dictionary stay two before the documented
  function under them, so files laid out that way (eight in the plugin) open as rows.
- A `_init` or `_validate_property` in the middle of a file opens as its function, the way it
  already did at the end of one. A command tool's `_init` reads as Command tool ▸ On run.
- A function wired to a signal that returns a value (`button.pressed.connect(apply)` with
  `func apply() -> bool`) opens as a function instead of code.
- A published verb that names its own `## @ace_icon(...)`, loops with `## @ace_looping(item)`, or
  has no `## @ace_codegen_template(...)` line keeps those lines exactly as written, so pack sources
  such as Post Kit, Codex and Save System open with their verbs as functions.
- When one function still cannot be written back byte for byte, only that function (up to four of
  them) stays code; the rest of the file opens as rows instead of all of it falling back.
- The wordless-row share counts a function as one row whichever way it was opened, so the number no
  longer moves when the same function is read in a different place.

### Changed: a table handed to `return` or to a call opens as one row

- `return {`, `rows.append({`, `table[key] = {`, `print_rich(x, [` and every other statement that
  hands a multi-line table or list on, written one entry per line, now opens as ONE structured row
  instead of a row per line. It reads exactly as before (the statement, then `table` or `list` and
  the entries as chips), but it moves, switches off and is deleted as the one statement it is, and a
  chip edits its entry through the row menu's Add, Edit and Remove Entry. A list of one-line records
  works the same way. A table whose entries span lines (a nested value, a comment among the entries)
  keeps the lines it was, which the canvas already reads as one row.
- A variable rename, Replace All, Find References and project Find reach the text such a table
  writes, and so do they for a `var x := {` table declared the same way.
- Measured over the plugin's own `addons/` and `tools/` (1,119 files, all still byte-identical on
  save): rows with no words of their own fall from 26,591 to 5,026, and the lines still held as
  verbatim code from 124,971 to 104,494, with this and the two changes below. The wordless-row
  ceilings of the editor's own reading gate come down to match.
### Added: a selected function's Inspector describes its choices and warns before a mistake is written

- Returns, Category, each parameter's Type, and In Add Action, Waits and Shared are described
  dropdowns again: every choice says what it means as the pointer rests on it, and the chosen
  choice's sentence stays under the field as a muted line (a parameter Type reads \"int: A whole
  number...\"). Waits reads Finishes at once / Can wait, Shared reads One per object / One for the
  script, In Add Action reads Not listed / Listed. Category keeps its text box, with the sheet's
  categories one click away beside it. Every field's label says what the field is for on hover.
- A line under a field steers off an error before anything is written, in three strengths. Refused
  (red edge, nothing written): a Name that is not a valid name, is a GDScript keyword, or is already
  a function, variable or signal of this sheet, each with a free name to try (`health` - \"Try
  health_2\"); a parameter Default its Type cannot hold (`abc` for an int) or that breaks GDScript's
  defaults-last order; Waits set to Finishes at once while the body has a Wait; Shared turned on while
  the body uses a variable each object has its own copy of. A refused Name or Default keeps the typed
  text in its field and the sheet keeps its bytes. Hint (accent edge, allowed): Returns set while no
  event in the body says Return (and what callers get instead), Waits on with nothing that waits, a
  parameter left required. Confirmation (green): a free Name says the rename and how many calls
  leaving the field will change.
- The fields are drawn by an Inspector plugin that handles only the function being edited,
  registered on the first function selection, so neither the editor's start nor a sheet's first
  open loads it.
### Fixed: the plugin opens its own code cleanly

- Opening the first sheet of a session no longer prints the engine warning \"OptimizedTranslation
  does not store the message texts\". The shipped translations import compressed, and a compressed
  translation has no message texts to list; the CSV beside it is read instead, as it always was.
- `tools/verify_sheets.gd` no longer crashes when its files include the checker's own
  `addons/eventforge/verify.gd`, so the whole-project form reads every file again. The checker's
  own file is answered by the fact that it is running, like the command script already was.
- A file that ends with blank lines under its last function opens as rows: the blank lines are
  counted and written back, where before the whole file stayed code (four of the plugin's files,
  `project_find.gd` and `run_controls.gd` among them). A doc line that stays text above a function
  (`## @ace_category Combat`, without the brackets the annotation takes) keeps the gap above it as
  its own, so the function under it opens too instead of being held back as code.
- A file-scope table whose entries are written over several lines, such as a list of dictionaries
  with one key per line, opens as one Declare row with one entry per dictionary. The entry keeps its
  lines in the file, shows on one line, and is edited as code. A table with a comment among its
  entries, or several entries on one line, still shows folded as before.
- The share of rows that say nothing of their own now counts what the canvas draws. A table handed
  to a statement that stays a line per row (a nested value inside it) is drawn as one row with its
  entries as chips, and is counted as that one row; a note written among a table's entries is
  counted as words. Over all 1,120 files under `addons/` and `tools/`, on top of the other self-reading
  changes, rows with no words of their own fell from 5,418 to 1,998, lines still held as code from
  87,890 to 84,630, and functions shown as a block of code from 68 to 9. Every file still
  round-trips.

### Fixed: a statement continued with `\\` is one row

- A line ending in `\\` opened as a row whose value ended in a stray backslash, with the rest of the
  statement (`else …`, `or …`, `+ \"…\" \\`) as orphan rows below it. The whole statement is one row
  now, and it reads as the sentence it is.
- A note written inside a callback or a branch that stays code opens as a comment row, its indent
  kept so the file comes back exactly as written.

### Changed: engine callbacks written the engine docs' way open as rows

- `_notification` written as `if what == NOTIFICATION_X:` / `elif what == NOTIFICATION_Y:` opens as
  one trigger per notification, exactly as the `match what:` spelling does, and saves back as the
  chain it was. An arm may hold several rows. An arm that tests more than `what`, and the
  translation-changed arm that On Language Changed itself writes, keep the handler as it was.
- `_get_property_list` written between other functions opens as the function row it is, in place.
- A property whose accessors sit on their headers' own line (`get: return panel.tree`), or whose
  getter comes before its setter, opens as the property row with its get and set, and saves back in
  that spelling. An accessor grown past one line saves as its block.

### Fixed: six edges of the variables, functions, translation and save slices

- Group ▸ New group… is greyed for a variable declared in another file, with the same hover as End
  group here, and neither the menu nor a drop onto it groups it any more: a sheet variable of the
  same name keeps its group.
- Selecting another row, or closing the sheet, takes a function out of Godot's Inspector instead of
  leaving an empty object there. Text typed into one of its fields and not yet left is written first.
- In a translation folder whose catalogs are all hand-made, adding a language and writing the fake
  column now write nothing, report no failure, and name the catalogs they left alone. A one-file
  extract with notes off writes Godot's plain catalog shape, which the studio reads as hand-made
  from then on; that is now written down and pinned.
- Keep loading on a Manual variable stores only the old key, because Load Game's state has no
  sections; a class rename no longer stores anything on it.
- The Save studio compares numbers as numbers, so leaving an unchanged spin box (600.0 against a
  scene's 600) no longer rewrites the scene.

### Changed: the Save studio's Player settings cards are read off the rows they teach

- Each Game Settings row on a Player settings card (Declare Setting, Load All Settings, Changed
  Setting Is, Set Setting, Apply Quality...) is now read off the pack's own definition: the words
  are the row's own reading and the code is what its template writes, so a card can no longer show
  a call the pack does not make. The settings.cfg and save-slot samples are the files Godot and the
  Save System really write, and the bindings card names the file through the bindings rows' own
  constant.
- The File and format fields are built through the window kit's field form, slot files are matched
  without a hand-escaped pattern, and Look inside reads a selected node through the Save System's
  own walk instead of a copy of it. Nothing else in the window changes.
- The Player settings cards are built the first time that tab is shown, not every time the window
  opens. `tools/render_save_studio_preview.gd` shoots every tab again.

### Fixed: the Translation Studio never rewrites a catalog you wrote by hand

- With a folder of catalogs (`res://i18n`), Find text, Add a language, bringing a file back and the
  fake language now write only the catalogs the studio made itself. It knows them by the header it
  writes: `keys` first and a `notes` column, which a catalog written by hand for Godot does not
  have. A hand-made `.csv` under the folder is still read, so a line it holds is never copied into
  a second file, but it stays byte for byte as you wrote it.
- A new line whose folder's catalog is hand-made goes into a studio catalog beside it
  (`ui.extracted.csv` next to `ui.csv`), and each step's result card names every hand-made
  catalog it left alone. Folder mode always writes the `notes` column, notes on or off, since it is
  how the studio knows its own catalogs again.
- A hand-made `i18n/strings.csv` no longer makes the studio treat the project as a one-file
  target; it starts on the folder instead. A line a hand-made catalog holds is no longer reported as
  \"in no script\" when a translator's file brings it back.

### Changed: a variable from another file says where its group ends, and the strip fits a narrow dock

- A variable row standing for a declaration in another file (a global used here) keeps End group
  here greyed, and the item's hover says why: \"This variable lives in globals.gd; open that file to
  end its group.\" An \"Open globals.gd\" item beside it opens that file. A group's end line dropped on
  such a row moves nothing and says the same. Nothing here writes a group for a member another file
  declares, even when this sheet has one of the same name.
- The help strip under a selected variable keeps room for at least sixty characters of its sentence
  at a narrow dock: the sheet's counts give way first, then its doors fold into one \"…\" menu holding
  the same items. The whole sentence stays on the strip's hover, and widening the dock puts the
  counts and the doors back.

### Added: values players already saved keep loading through a rename and an update

- Renaming a variable that has a Save choice (Auto, Load only or Manually) says in the Rename window
  that values players already saved under the old name will not load, and offers Keep loading the
  old name. Ticked, the generated block reads the old key once, while the new one has nothing saved
  yet, inside the rename's own undo step:
  `score = __remember_cfg.get_value(\"Player\", \"score\", __remember_cfg.get_value(\"Player\", \"points\", score))`,
  and Load Game reads the old key the same way. Renaming the class says the same for its Auto and
  Load only variables, whose section is the class name, and offers the same box. The stored keys do
  not change; the line is plain code, reopens as the same choice and saves byte for byte.
- The Project Doctor warns when two scripts without a `class_name` save a same-named variable into
  the shared `\"vars\"` section of `user://remembered.cfg`, where they overwrite each other's value. The
  fix it names is giving the script a `class_name`, and it says what that costs: values already saved
  under `\"vars\"` stop loading for that script unless Keep loading the old name is ticked.
- A script an older version wrote with Remember Between Runs (`_ef_recall_remembered`,
  `_ef_store_remembered` and `__ef_remember_boot`) shows those variables in the Save studio as
  Remembered (older style) instead of Never, with Update to Auto. Nothing changes until it is pressed;
  then the three are rewritten as the Auto block in one undo step, with the same file and keys, so
  values the old code saved load through the new block. A section the class has since left is kept
  loading.

### Changed: a function is edited in Godot's own Inspector, and branching first steps sit beside the head

- Selecting a function's head hands it to Godot's Inspector: Name, Description, Returns, In Add
  Action, Category, Waits, Shared, and under Parameters each one's name, Type and Default, a Move
  Up button and Add Parameter. It replaces the function panel of the Properties bar. Each change is
  one undo step (the Inspector's own history entry is handed back, so one Ctrl+Z still undoes one
  change), and typed text - a rename above all, which renames every call - is written once, when
  its field is left, not on every key. The Inspector cannot describe the choices of a dropdown one
  by one, so those sentences are gone; parameters are numbered rather than named in its sections,
  because the Inspector capitalises section names and a parameter is shown exactly as written.
- A function whose first step branches (`return 1 if ready else 0`) now draws that step beside its
  head like any other first step, with the branch rows as its sub-events and no empty \"Always\" row
  between them, and a step's pattern chip comes up with its actions. 214 functions in the shipped
  packs read this way now; the 11 whose first step holds a `match` keep their own row, because a
  match can read as branches whose head belongs on a condition lane the function already uses.

### Fixed: Insert keeps Godot's whole Recent list, and four smaller edges

- Insert new object put its class first in Godot's own Create New Node Recent list and then kept
  only fifteen lines of it, so every Insert dropped the rest of what Godot had written there. It
  keeps every other line now; how long the list grows stays Godot's business.
- An Add behaviour tile clips a long name, so its hover now leads with the name as the pack wrote
  it, then the pitch. A behaviour made for a script class (a `class_name`) sits beside the object's
  own kinds when the object's script is that class, and with \"any node\" otherwise, instead of among
  the greyed kinds made for other objects; it was never greyed, only filed there.
- A `##` comment written straight under `extends` that runs into a function or variable is that
  member's doc comment, the way Godot reads it: Behaviour settings no longer shows it as the
  behaviour's Description, and the head no longer reads it as the class description, so typing a
  description never rewrites the function's doc line.
- Adding a just-made behaviour to its object reads the pack list by path, and the first-open gate
  names that list among what opening a sheet must leave uncompiled.

### Fixed: the timing pins measure the work, and sit three times over what it measures

- `tools/measure_editor_boot.gd` keeps session restore off while it times: the tabs it reopened came
  from a `user://` file every checkout shares, so the first open measured whatever the last editor
  closed on the machine had open. It prints the tabs the open left, and the budget test pins two.
- The \"who else calls this\" rebuild appended to a caller list by copying it, once per name per
  script; it appends in place now, and the test pins that a rebuild reads no script's text.
- The shader and scene readers' cold pins read their fixture files once off the clock, and the
  10,000-event smoke sets up a small sheet first, so a slow disk or the session's one-time builds
  are not in the figure.
- Every budget that flapped is re-measured on a quiet machine, in the repository and in a copy
  outside OneDrive (the two agree within a few percent once files are cached), and set at three
  times the median: the first open in a real editor 16,453 ms (budget 49,400, was 85,000), the bare
  first tab 4,104 ms, the workspace build 10,177 ms, the 2,000-line script 6,704 ms and its rebuild
  3,127 ms, the Doctor 29.7 s (budget 88.5 s; its two count pins are the regression gate), the
  callers rebuild 127 ms, the pack rebuild 1,066 ms, the 10,000-event setup 9,148 ms.

### Fixed: opening a real sheet stops compiling the Doctor, the dialogs and the Manual

- The workspace's first open in a real editor measured 16,300 to 16,348 ms against 21,023 to 21,154
  before (and 20,742 to 20,819 at the end of the first wave), three alternating quiet runs each in
  a copy of the project outside OneDrive. The gate only ever opened a blank sheet, and the reading
  of real rows reached four things a click needs: the share index asked the Project Doctor for the
  project's script list (about five seconds of compiling for a list of paths), the Interop
  section's \"stays code\" check read its file through the Doctor, a variable's default value was
  written by the variable dialog (which names the parameters dialog and the picker), and the
  read-next suggestion put the sheet through the Manual's search inside the wait.
- The script list is one small walker the Doctor walks through too; \"stays code\" reads the file;
  the default value's words and the variable-reference hint live with the variable owners; the
  suggestion is worked out a frame after the rows are on screen.
- Six more click-only dock helpers (the ask field, the function dialog, the head actions,
  go-to-definition, add rows, variable groups) were built while the canvas and the strip were
  wired, about a second between them; their signals now reach them when they fire.
- The cold-boot gate opens an ordinary showcase script after the blank sheet, and names those six
  helpers among what the first open must leave uncompiled.

### Fixed: a name stays as written in a window's table, and the last English lines are translated

- A window's table translated its cells, so in a Japanese editor the Debugger listed an object
  called Player as プレイヤー and one called targets as 対象. A table's cells are never translated
  now (its column titles are), and the sentences that sat in one say themselves in the editor's
  language: the Debugger's empty tabs and trail notes, Loose Ends' kind headings.
- The Words page's title, preset names and column heads, Compare With's Last save / Backup entries
  and the Welcome window's Preferences card read in the editor's language.

### Fixed: the Theme Editor's preview reads, and two windows keep their foot in place

- The Theme Editor's sample sheet wrapped every condition a letter a line (\"Ev / ery / tic / k\")
  in a 560 px preview; the window opens wider and gives the preview 720 px.
- A plain window measures what it holds a moment after it shows, once its labels have wrapped: the
  Debugger otherwise grew three screens tall from the first layout's one-word-a-line guess.
- Import event sheet keeps its strip and buttons at the window's foot instead of floating under the
  cards with the rest of the window blank.

### Added: a data table column has its own Default and Description, and a Texture kind

- New Data Table's column panel gives the selected column a **Default** (what a new row starts with
  in it) and a **Description** (what it holds). The Default fills each new row, in the window and
  on the Inspector's Add Row; the Description is said on the column's header in the Inspector, on
  the grid's header in the window and in the strip under it. \"New rows get\" became the Default
  field, which shows the kind's own blank until one is typed.
- A column can be a **Texture**: the cell holds a picture's `res://` path, with the picture beside
  it. In the Inspector the path is typed or the picture dragged in from the FileSystem dock; the
  game reads it with `load()`, so the rows stay plain data.
- Both notes ride the grid's declaration after its columns, as `:default=` and `:about=` with one
  value per column, and a grid written before has exactly the line it had. A script opened as a
  sheet keeps them byte for byte, and the variable window's Columns field keeps them too.
- The Inspector table's headers read each column's name as the code writes it (`max_jumps`, no
  longer Max Jumps).

### Fixed: the windows the canvas build touched speak the editor's language

- Opened in a German or Japanese editor, Sheet Type, the Welcome window and the tour, Compare With,
  Loose Ends, Manage Includes, the sheet map's count, Custom ACE Providers, Sheet Backups' restore,
  Generate, the Compare dialog's help, the shortcut and fixed-key names, the Words page's left
  column and descriptions, the region editor's fields, Split Scene Into Chunks and Export Mod
  Template read in English: their sentences were never handed to the translation tables, or were
  set straight from a table with no translate call. About 280 sentences are now translated in all
  eight languages.
- An event's hover sentence reads a value-emphasising row as plain words (Keep hp between 0 and
  100), not as `[b]hp[/b]` tags.

### Changed: Add behaviour is a shelf of tiles, your own behaviours first

- The Add behaviour window lists the packs as tiles, three to a row: each tile is the pack's
  picture, its name as the pack writes it, and the kind of object it works on. The tiles sit on one
  shelf per kind of object - your own behaviours first, then the kinds the chosen object is, then
  the ones for any node, then the rest. One made for another kind of object is greyed, with the
  reason on hover.
- A click shows the pack's properties as before; a double-click adds it straight away.
- The category buttons beside the search are gone: almost every pack names a category of its own,
  so they were one button per pack. The search now only shows and hides tiles, so a keystroke no
  longer rebuilds the list (about 8 ms instead of the whole list's 150-300 ms).
- The tile pictures come through the picker's own picture cache, so they load once per session.

### Added: Behaviour settings… and Global settings…, and every door to the makers

- The New behaviour and New global forms reopen on a file that exists, titled `Patrol · Behaviour
  settings` (or `GameState · Global settings`), every answer filled in and Save changes in place of
  Create. The answers are read from the file's own header - `## @ace_category`, `@icon`, the `##`
  description line, the type of its `host`, and for a global its row in Project Settings > Globals >
  Autoload - so a file written by hand opens the same way. Save changes writes only what changed,
  as one undo step, and saves the file; a new name first asks the rename everywhere, which lists the
  places it changes before anything is written. Made from says how it began is not kept in the file.
- Reached from the file name's click menu on the open sheet (it leads with the settings now), from
  right-clicking a behaviour in an object's Add behaviour list (Behaviour settings… or Open as sheet),
  and from right-clicking a global's card in the picker (Global settings…).
- The Add behaviour list ends with + New behaviour…: Works on starts on that object's kind and the
  new behaviour is added to the object once made.
- The Start page's Behaviours shelf leads with New behaviour…, its Project-wide shelf with New
  global…, for when no sheet is open yet.
- Fixed: every opening of the New behaviour or New global form left the previous window behind.

### Added: Insert new object remembers its class in Godot's Recent list

- A class made with Insert new object goes to the top of Godot's own Recent list, the one Create
  New Node shows, the way Create New Node puts it there: newest first, each class once, fifteen at
  most. Both windows then offer it again from Recent. Only node classes are added, since that list
  offers only nodes.

### Added: the Translation Studio's line window opens where its line is used

- The window a clicked line opens in has Open where it is used beside Done: the script that says
  the line opens in Godot's script editor at that line, or the data asset whose text it is opens in
  the Inspector. The place is read fresh on each press, so a line moved since the last Find text is
  still found, and a line nothing says any more says so on the status bar.

### Changed: the Outline, Bookmarks, History and Sheet map windows name their sheet and say what is picked

- Each of the four sheet panels is titled by its sheet (\"Outline of player.gd\", \"Bookmarks in
  player.gd\", \"History of player.gd\") and has the kit's foot: Close beside Go to Row (Go Back to This
  Step in History, Open Sheet in the Sheet map), and a strip under the list naming the picked entry,
  mark, step or box. The Outline's strip shows a region's or a note's line in code; Bookmarks and
  History say their keys there.
- History lists each step and the event it landed on in two columns. A click on a step travels to
  it and lights the rows it touched until the window closes; passing the pointer over the list no
  longer lights anything.
- A bookmark on an event with no condition yet reads as the event's words, not \"+ Add condition\".
- The four panels' buttons, hints and the Sheet map's summary go through translation.

### Fixed: reading a sheet's code no longer saves it, and the windows fit, close and say what they hold

- Opening the Manual on a verb, Find References, the Properties window, Change type, Loose Ends and
  the Insert new object preview read the code a sheet compiles to through the compiler's save path:
  on a script-backed sheet that wrote the open sheet's unsaved edits into its `.gd`, and a sheet with
  no file left a stray `res://event_sheet_generated.gd` declaring its `class_name` in the project.
  They read it through `SheetCompiler.echo()` now, which compiles and writes nothing.
- Every plain window (Bookmarks, Replay Recorder, Outline, History, the Start page, the studios, the
  Debugger and the rest) closes on Esc, through its own close, and grows to fit what it holds once
  shown: Bookmarks and the Replay Recorder opened cutting off their foot.
- Optimise this sheet scrolls its findings inside the window: a dozen findings opened it taller than
  the editor, with Apply below the screen. Add global variable opens at the height of its form.
- Loose Ends reads the whole script first: on a sheet whose file compiles it no longer lists rows
  that use a variable a code block declares as \"doesn't compile\", nor prints a parse error for each.
- Manage Includes' Contents preview is read-only and hidden until an include is picked; it showed an
  empty canvas offering Add your first event and New from template to no sheet.
- The Translation Studio's table in a project with no catalog yet reads \"No lines yet\" instead of
  \"No lines in  yet\".

### Fixed: an opened script keeps what it says when it is read, retyped or previewed

- A declaration whose value runs over several lines (`var waves = [` then its items, then `]`) no
  longer opens as a variable whose value is `[` with the rest stranded in code blocks after it,
  where moving or editing that row wrote a file that did not parse. It stays one block of code, or
  opens as a Declare row the way `var waves := [` already did.
- Change Type Everywhere on a variable written with no type (`var target = null`) or an inferred one
  (`var slot := 0`) writes the new type into the declaration. It used to leave the line as it was,
  so the file reopened as the old type.
- OK on a variable's window, changing nothing, leaves its line as the file wrote it. It used to
  rewrite it in the window's own words, and some of them changed what the line meant:
  `@export var tint = Color.WHITE` became `@export var tint: Variant = \"Color.WHITE\"` (a String),
  `var target = null` became `var target = \"\"`, `const SAVE_FILE := \"...\"` became a `var`, a
  trailing `# comment` after the value went, `\"exp\", \"hide_slider\"` lost `\"hide_slider\"`, and
  `@export_range(0, 100)`, `1e3`, `.5` and `Vector2(1, 2)` gained a step, digits or decimals.
  Write the type in code starts unticked on a line written without one.
- \"Use the file on disk\" only loads the file once the unsaved version is in Sheet Backups, as the
  question promises; when that backup cannot be written, the edits stay and the status says why.
- `tests/fixtures/existing_project_large/` holds five more hand-written scripts in the shapes a grown
  project collects, and the round-trip gate opens and saves each byte for byte.

### Fixed: Preview In Language reads a folder of catalogs, and the save words name what is there

- View > Preview In Language offered only pseudo in a project with no `strings.csv`, whose catalog
  is the `res://i18n` folder: it now reads every catalog under the folder, as the Translation
  Studio's coverage already did.
- The Save Studio's look-inside tab and the Quest pack's Save Quests / Load Quests no longer point
  at Add Save Support or a Remember Between Runs option, both gone; the Inspector Designer
  says its names read as the file declares them, which is what it shows.
- Save As Preset…'s file-name poll stops once its dialog is closed.
- The translation catalogs drop the last row still telling translators to use Remember Between
  Runs: Remember Value As already says to give the variable a Save choice.

### Fixed: the first open of the workspace stops compiling the picker, the Doctor and the Manual

- Wiring the canvas and its menus reached six dock helpers that are built on first use (the ACE,
  add-rows, row-edit, variables, group-prompt and split helpers) the moment the editor was built,
  and the ACE helper names the picker, the parameters dialog, the Doctor, the public API and the
  Manual. Those signals now reach their helper when they fire, so the first open of the workspace
  is back to leaving all of them uncompiled.

### Changed: the Theme Editor names each token as the code does, and folds

- A token row reads as the style file names it (`condition_lane_ratio`, no longer Condition Lane
  Ratio), in the code font, and is never translated.
- Every section folds under its title. Quick Style, Reading Comfort, the editor-wide switches and
  Sheet & rows start open; the cell, reading-mark, bar and Manual sections start folded.
- The description that was a hover tooltip now reads in the strip at the window's foot: THIS FIELD
  says what the token under the pointer does, IN FILE the line it writes into a saved preset
  (`condition_lane_ratio = 0.45`), updated as it is edited.
- Save As Preset…, Close and Apply To Current Sheet sit in the window's own button row, beside a
  note saying Apply writes the look into the open sheet only. A preset that saves, or fails to, says
  so in that note as well as on the status bar.
- Save As Preset… opens the editor's own save dialog, with a line at its foot naming the file a
  Save would replace.
- A float token with no range of its own steps 0.01 instead of 0.5 (every shipped number token
  already declares its own range and step).
- Fixed: with no theme on the sheet, the Theme Editor seeded the cell styles after moving the
  colours onto the editor's, so its cell colours were not the ones the sheet paints.

### Changed: the Inspector Designer says what each row's buttons do

- A strip at the window's foot names the variable under the pointer: THIS FIELD says what ✎ does
  and, for a variable that can move, which variable ▲ puts it above (one undo step); IN CODE is
  the line it is in the file (`@export var jump_strength: float = 480.0`).
- Beside Close, a note says the sheet variables at the top keep the file's order, which is why
  they carry no ▲.
- The Inspector preview, here and in the variable window, names a variable exactly as the code
  writes it (`max_speed`, no longer Max Speed).

### Changed: Translation Studio reads as four steps, with your languages on top

- Tools > Translation Studio opens as \"Your game in other languages\": one page instead of three tabs.
  A strip at the top holds a card per language with its code, how much is done, a bar and its state
  (Source, Done, Nearly done, In progress, Not started); hovering a card gives the exact counts and
  whether players can pick it yet (its translation file is turned on under Localization).
- Four numbered steps run the same work as before: Find text, Add a language (new: a searchable list
  of every language Godot knows, adding an empty column), Bring in a spreadsheet, Check, Try a fake
  language, and Turn on in the game. Each button writes a result card under itself saying what just
  happened, in figures and one line per outcome.
- The translation table sits under the steps, with its own catalog picker in place of the old file
  field: a project with an i18n/strings.csv works on it, and one without gets one catalog per
  folder under i18n/. Check switches the table to Missing only, and Try a fake language to Fake
  language. Clicking a line opens it in its own window: the English, where players see it, who
  says it (the line's tr() context, else the script that says it), the translator's note, and every
  language marked Done, Missing or Too long against the room the English leaves plus 40%, with
  Previous and Next walking the lines the table shows. A long list of keys in a result card names
  the first five.
- The sweep's folder, the notes toggle and the fake column's code and stretch are no longer fields:
  the sweep reads the whole project, always writes where each line is used, and the fake column is
  always qps at 40% longer. Add a language adds its column to every catalog of a folder.

### Added: translations in pieces, and a table that stays fast at a million lines

- Translation Studio can write ONE CATALOG PER PROJECT FOLDER: in a project with no
  `i18n/strings.csv` a key said in `res://dialogue/chapter_03/` lands in `i18n/dialogue/chapter_03.csv`, one said in
  `res://ui/` in `i18n/ui.csv`, and the top of the project in `i18n/strings.csv`. A project that
  already has its `i18n/strings.csv` keeps it exactly as before, and a key some catalog already holds
  is never moved out of it. Coverage, orphans, importing a translator's file and the pseudo column
  all work over the whole folder; an import leaves a catalog nothing landed in byte for byte alone.
- Register Translation Catalogs now finds the `.translation` files in every folder under the one you
  name, so each catalog Godot imports is added under Project Settings > Localization.
- The Notes tab is a translation table: pick a catalog, find text or a key, and switch between All
  lines, Missing only (empty cells read \"missing\" in amber, and the status line counts what each
  language still lacks) and Fake language (each line beside its pseudo text, a test column players
  never see). Only the rows on screen are drawn and a row is only read when it scrolls into view, so a 200,000-line catalog opens in about a fifth of a second.

### Changed: the third code pay-back

- The dock drops its one-line forwarders: the menus, shortcuts, context menus and tests call the
  helper that does the work (the sheet file helper, the row edits, the variables manager, the code
  panel, the provider glue) instead of a dock method that only passed the call on. Nothing a user
  sees or does changes.
- The sheet canvas drops its forty-six one-line forwarders the same way: the row builder, the
  folding, the drag, the input handlers and the row metrics are called directly.
- Nine finding families shared one copy each of the same \"findings pinned to this event\" filter; the
  row builder holds it once (`ViewportRowBuilder.findings_at_event`).
- The window kit gains `table` (a titled results Tree with a least height and narrow columns) and
  `add_results` (a results card plus the strip naming the picked result). Find in Project, Find
  References, Find Repeated Rows, the Debugger, Live Values, Outline, History, Bookmarks, Loose Ends,
  the Doctor's inbox, the find results bar and the Lift Report build their lists through them.
- Seven hand-built accept dialogs open through the kit's form window: Change type everywhere, the
  rename receipt, What changed since you saved, Migrate rows, Adopt pattern, Keep as code and Re-mint.

### Added: the Save studio's file, slots, look-inside and player-settings tabs

- File and format: the Save System node's own settings - a described dropdown over its six formats
  (the chosen one's sentence stays under it), the file name whose ending follows the format, folder,
  encryption key, backups and autosave - with a live sample of the file the format writes. They are
  written onto the scene the SaveSystem global names, so its Inspector shows the same values; a
  global that is a bare script says so and offers Keep them in a scene.
- Save slots: each slot file with when it was saved and its size, Look inside and Delete per slot,
  Open the save folder, and Copy a slot out… (converted through the pack's own backends when the
  copy's ending names another format).
- For your players: the Save System pack gains Open Save Folder and Can Open Save Folder (false on
  the web and on phones). Switching the option off hides the action from Add Action through the
  reader's own hide list, which now also applies to authored rows.
- Look inside a save: a slot, the selected node or a bundled pack, in plain words, with the file as
  Godot writes it one click away.
- Player settings: the three files a game keeps (save slots, settings.cfg through the Game Settings
  pack, bindings.cfg through the built-in bindings rows) and six uses as Events | Code | Both cards.
- These four tabs replace the old Format Preview and Save Slots tabs beside What gets saved.

### Changed: one Save choice per variable replaces Add Save Support and Remember Between Runs

- The Save Studio opens on a new **What gets saved** tab: pick an open sheet as the Object, and give
  each of its variables a **Save** choice - Never, Auto, Load only, Save only or Manually. Every
  option says what it does on hover, the closed dropdown keeps the chosen one's sentence, and one
  line under the list tells the whole story (\"Player: health, coins come back by themselves;
  spawn_point only at your Save Game / Load Game.\"). A **Where it is kept** table says which step
  writes which file. Right-click a variable and choose **Save Choice...** to open it.
- The choice is stored on the variable and compiles to plain Godot at the end of the script: Auto
  loads when the node is ready and saves when it leaves the tree or the game quits, Load only loads
  by itself and is written back only at Save Game, Save only is written and never read back, and
  every saved variable is in a generated `save_state()` / `load_state()` pair with the node joining
  the `persist` group, so the Save System's Save Game and Load Game carry it. The values stay in
  `user://remembered.cfg` under the class name, where Remember Between Runs kept them, and a
  variable marked Remember Between Runs reads as Auto. Such a variable now also joins Save Game's
  snapshot, since Auto is both homes.
- An opened script reads that block back as the choices it came from and saves byte for byte; a
  hand-written `save_state()` / `load_state()` is never claimed. A sheet that has its own pair keeps
  it and gets the block without the pair, so a remembered variable still loads and saves by itself
  beside it; Manual, which is only the pair, then saves nothing, and the compile says so.
- Removed: the **Add Save Support** tab (scan a script, tick variables, paste the generated pair) and
  the **Remember Between Runs** menu toggle - the Save choice does both. `EventSheets.save_state_code`,
  `persistable_fields` and `add_save_support` are unchanged and share the compiler's generator.

### Changed: the help, keys, words and asking windows say what they do on the window

- Keyboard Shortcuts: a key put on a fixed key (Ctrl+F, Ctrl+P…) or on another changeable key says
  so in an amber note under its row, not only in a tooltip. The rows are built once and repainted, so
  the button listening for a key is never rebuilt under it. Reset all to defaults sits beside Done,
  and a help strip names the key being changed and where custom keys are kept.
- Words: each vocabulary choice describes itself on hover and keeps the chosen one's sentence under
  the dropdown; Reset to defaults sits beside Close; the help strip follows every word's two fields.
- Ask: the mode line wears the ok edge while Ask is on and amber while it is off, Add these events is
  the window's blue main button, and the strip says which setting the sentence is sent to.
- Generate Events from a Description: Generate is greyed while it cannot run, and the window itself
  says why (Ask off, no key, a request still out) and what went wrong with the last request, instead
  of only the dock's status line; the strip says which setting the description is sent to.
- The Manual window drops its Read this online button: Open online under the guide tree does the same.
- A described dropdown no longer prints a missing-metadata error each time an item is added.
### Changed: the hand-built confirm dialogs open through the window kit

- The window kit's form window takes `hide_on_ok` (OK closes it, for a form OK cannot refuse) and a
  least size. Twenty-five dialogs that built the same confirm-dialog shell by hand now open through
  it: Message, Send message, Modes, States, Compare, Add global variable, Edit comment, Scope Actions
  To Node, the custom block form, Add child, Add an include, Optimise, Paste Retargeted, the three
  Moment forms, Edit group, the sheet head's text line, Publish New Version, Connect a signal, New
  Event Sheet, New Function, Edit Parameter, Save as snippet and Edit code block. Nothing they show or
  do changes.

### Fixed: a warning badge reads in a light editor

- A small badge beside a title (the line window's Missing, for one) lifted its words lighter than its
  tint, so in a light editor amber words sat on an amber wash at a contrast of about 2. On a light
  panel the words are now pressed darker than the tint (about 4.9); a dark editor is unchanged.

### Fixed: a sheet with no theme reads in a light editor

- A sheet that keeps the editor-matched default look, in a light editor theme, kept the dark
  palette's pale cell words (pale blue on a pale lane) and a black number gutter. The words now take
  the editor's own text colour, with the native look's light-hand colours for object labels, values
  and quoted text, and the gutter takes the editor's row grey. A dark editor is unchanged.

### Fixed: Compare stops printing a missing-meta error

- The Compare window's operator list asked for its code note in a way Godot answers with an engine
  error whenever the note is not there; it now asks whether the note exists first.

### Fixed: every other plain window reads in a light theme too

- Nineteen more windows (the Replay Recorder, Compare With, the conflict view, Loose Ends, the Project
  Doctor, the start page, the tour, the include manager, Import sheet, the Lift Workbench and its
  drafts, the publish preview, Sheet Backups, the dropped-node preview, Custom ACE Providers, the
  project view, New shared sheet, Why didn't this fire and the feedback card) now sit on the
  editor's own base colour instead of showing the engine's grey margins in a light editor theme. The
  six that painted a hand-built panel of their own take the same one body, so the fix is 62 lines
  shorter than what it replaces.

### Fixed: quick questions stop wiring Enter twice, and the debugger windows read in a light theme

- Seven quick questions (Row Comment, Save View, Duplicate Events For, the two Add an Inspector button
  questions, Move into a group, Group by prefix and Rename class) wired Enter in their field a second
  time, so Godot printed \"already connected\" every time one opened. The quick question alone wires it.
- The Debugger and Live Values windows paint the editor's base colour behind their cards, as every
  other kit window does, instead of showing the engine's grey in a light editor theme.
- A double-click on one of the Project Doctor's fix rows applies the fix once, not once per click.
- Curate script shows its lines in a wider card that scrolls past twelve lines, instead of a narrow
  card that grew the window off the screen.
- The Row Comment question and the Live Values edit messages are translated.

### Fixed: kit windows paint their own background

- The Outline, Sheet map, Bookmarks, History and the making windows built on the window kit painted
  nothing behind their cards, so in a light editor theme the margins showed a mid-grey hole around
  them. They now sit on the editor's own base colour, light or dark.

### Fixed: the Inspector preview shows the variable's own value

- The Inspector preview a variable's hover and the Inspector Designer draw showed a fixed sample in
  place of the value: every slider at the same spot on a 0 to 1 track, every progress bar at 65 of
  100, every set of flags as Fire, Ice and Poison. A slider, progress bar or min-max range now sits at
  the variable's value inside its own bounds (a `max_health` of 100 out of 0 to 200 reads half way),
  flags and number choices show the variable's own names ticked or chosen by its value, a colour
  shows its colour, a toggle row its chosen word, and a path, password or expression its own text.

### Changed: eight more quick questions take the one small-prompt shape

- Go to event, Extract to function, the conditional breakpoint, a Timeline step, a list or dictionary
  entry, Run with arguments, Curate script and the unknown feature tag question were each a one-off
  dialog. They now ask the way every quick question does: the title is the question (\"Go to which
  event?\", \"Break only when?\"), one sentence says what happens, the note says whether Ctrl+Z undoes
  it, and the blue button is the safe answer.
- Enter in the field a quick question asks for presses its blue button, in every quick question.
- The code block window is titled Edit code block.

### Changed: the making windows share one form shell and one described dropdown

- New data table, Insert new object, New behaviour, New global, Sheet Type and the class's
  Properties are built from the window kit's form window and its described field (a dropdown
  with the chosen choice's sentence kept in view under it) instead of each hand-building its own,
  and every file picker they open is the kit's one picker. Nothing they ask or write changed.
- Insert new object says a file it could not write on its own window, which stays open on what
  was typed, the way the other making windows already did, instead of closing and saying it on
  the dock's status line.
- Open, Save As, Export Generated GDScript, Save Sheet as Text, Include Sheet, Extract to an
  included sheet, Save as Test Sheet and the data table's CSV import and export open the same
  one-shot file picker. Dropping an object marked secret, locked or water asks its offer as the
  same quick question every other one is, with the same three answers it had. Row Comment, Save
  View and Duplicate Events For are that quick question with one field, and Enter in the field
  answers it.

### Changed: a behaviour names its host once

- A behaviour opened as a sheet showed its host twice: on the `host` variable row, and again as a
  separate Host binding bar for the generated `_enter_tree` that sets it. Only the variable row is
  left, where the file declares it; the title's hover still says what the behaviour works on. The
  `_enter_tree` stays in the file and is written back exactly as it was, and one that fails to
  compile still shows, so its error has a row.

### Fixed: enabling the plugin compiles less

- The Inspector drawers' widgets and the translation layer behind them were compiled at every editor
  start, because the Inspector plugin Godot builds at boot named them; translating the drawers' badges
  had made that cost larger. The widgets now load the first time a drawer or a decor line is drawn,
  which takes about 2,500 lines of GDScript (six files) off the enable path. Enabling the plugin
  measured 703 to 1,289 ms before and 495 to 655 ms after on the same busy machine.

### Added: the sheet panels say more and read in both themes

- **Outline**: a Filter box narrows a long outline to the entries that hold the typed words, keeping
  the folder each match lives in. Groups and the \"To do\" folder wear the FileSystem dock's folder
  icon instead of a \"▸\" mark; a region and a function keep their \"#\" and \"ƒ\".
- **Sheet map**: a key under the picture names each kind of box and line in the colour it is drawn
  in, and Reset Layout puts every dragged box back in its column and forgets where it was dragged.
- **Bookmarks**: a numbered entry leads with its event number and only an unnumbered one (a comment,
  a group) wears the star; the foot says bookmarks last for this session only.
- **History**: the first line reads \"Opened player.gd\" (the sheet's own file) instead of \"Before
  these edits\".
- Fixed: the History steps, the Outline kinds and the Bookmarks sheet header no longer keep their
  dark-theme colours on a light editor theme, where the History text all but vanished.

### Changed: the four checking windows say a finding as an edge and a sentence

- **Check the project** (Tools > Project Doctor) keeps its worst-first inbox and its new-since-last-look
  dots; every line now leads with an edge in its severity's colour. The selected finding's fixes open
  as rows under it, and a click on one applies it through undo and re-runs the check. That is the only
  way a fix is applied now: the \"Fix selected\" button, which was greyed for every built-in fix, and the
  chip bar below the list that did the same job are gone. The blue button names the file it opens.
- **Run every test sheet** (Tools > Run Tests) gains Run Again and Copy Report, and keeps its window
  open between runs. A failing claim is a red edge plus its reason; a passing one is its name and PASS.
- **Why didn't event 12 fire?** asks the question by the row's number, and the condition that said
  no wears a red edge instead of an arrow in a table. Jump to This Row stands on the right.
- **Find what is unfinished in player.gd** (Loose Ends) gains Refresh: the list was read once on open
  and went stale after any edit.
- The Doctor, Why and Loose Ends windows draw the editor theme's panel behind their content, so they
  read in a light editor theme instead of showing dark text on the engine's grey.

### Changed: the Debugger says where the run is on every tab, and Live Values reads at a glance

- A band over the Debugger's five tabs names the sheet, whether a run is reporting, whether Live
  Values and Event Trace are on, and - only while the game is paused at a row - which row. The
  window is titled after the sheet (\"Debug player.gd while it runs\").
- Inspect lists \"This sheet's variables\" first, with how many values the run reported for each
  object beside it, and dims an object the run has said nothing about. A strip under the values
  names the one under the cursor and, after an edit, shows exactly what was sent into the running
  game (the sheet itself is never changed).
- A watch that cannot be read says \"cannot read\" in the warning colour and why on its own line
  (\"velocity is not a streamed value.\"), instead of an engine error packed into the value cell. The
  Debugger's Watch tab and the Live Values window draw the one table over the one list.
- Profile draws each event's share of the run as a thin bar under its name.
- Live Values puts how the game got to its mode on a line of its own, and a value that cannot be
  edited (a behavior's, a list's items) reads muted instead of looking editable.
- Fixed: double-clicking a Profile or Breakpoints row to go to it no longer marks the game as paused
  there, and the paused row is forgotten when the run ends.

### Changed: the find windows say where each hit is and what their main button does

- **Command Palette** (Ctrl+P): the four prefix modes are tabs - Commands, `#` Sheets, `@` Symbols,
  `?` Docs - plus an Error line tab that waits for a pasted `res://file.gd:42`. Clicking a tab types
  its prefix. The foot has Cancel and Run, and a strip names the keys and the modes. Every mode shares
  one fuzzy order (prefix, then substring, then subsequence).
- **Find in Project**: Find and Replace share one card, and results gain an Event column (the event's
  margin number, or the line of a closed script) with a count under them. Picking a result names it
  in the strip, with the matched text as it reads in code. That Replace in Project saves closed sheets
  at once is said beside the button.
- **Find References**: uses group under the sheet they sit in, with a count per sheet and in all. Go
  to Row lands on the picked use, and the window is titled after the symbol.
- **Find Repeated Rows**: why a run cannot become one function (it uses an event-local or a For Each
  item) is said beside the greyed-out Make a Function button when the run is picked, not in the
  status bar after pressing it. The strip names the events the run sits in.

### Changed: renaming a variable shows everything it touches first, and the refactor windows say more

- A variable's Rename - from its menu or its name cell in the variables table - now opens the same
  window a function's Rename does: the declaration and every row that says the name, as they read now
  beside how they would read, the other files that call it by that name, and the sheets that include
  this one and are saved with it. Cancel changes nothing, and a name the sheet would refuse says why
  at the foot of the window. After the button, the small \"Renamed\" receipt lists the places it landed.
- Change Type Everywhere puts the rows it will not guess at under their own heading, \"Left for you to
  check\", instead of in the same list as the rows it rewrites, and its foot keeps the chosen type's
  sentence and the declaration it would write in view. Its words now follow the editor language.
- What Changed Since Save is a window with the editor's own buttons: Go to Row (or a double-click)
  shows the picked row, Close closes, and the foot says whether the picked line is a changed row or a
  saved line a save removes.
- The file-changed-on-disk question lists, before you choose, whether this tab has unsaved edits and
  when the file changed, and offers What Changed… to read the difference over the question itself.

### Fixed: a global made as a scene is found wherever the scene lives

- An autoload that points at a scene whose root node carries the script - the way many existing
  projects set one up, the scene in another folder or under another name - is now a global: it
  leads the picker as its own object and the Properties and actions window ticks it. Before, only a
  scene sitting beside the script under the same name counted.
- The Properties and actions window titles a file that has no class name and no autoload name by its
  file name exactly as written (`game_state.gd`), not a made-up `GameState`. Turning such a file on as
  a global still offers the name Godot's own Autoload tab would.

### Fixed: the Add behaviour list opens without a stall on a big project

- Opening the list and typing in its search read every pack's script twice and walked the whole of
  Project Settings twice per pack on each rebuild. It now reads each pack once per opening and the
  Autoload list once per rebuild.

### Fixed: no translation key is listed twice

- \"Destroy\" and \"%d more like it in this file.\" each sat twice in all nine translation files (two
  harvests met in a merge), and in Spanish and Italian the two rows had drifted to different words.
  The earlier row of each is gone - the later one is the one the editor always read - and a gate
  now fails when any catalog lists a key twice.

### Fixed: a range that leaves its step to Godot opens as a range

- `@export_range(1, 3)` - two numbers, the step left to Godot - stayed a code line on a variable,
  so its Inspector look could not be changed from the sheet. It now opens as the same range the
  three-number form does and saves back exactly as written, and a modifier added later writes the
  step in front of it (Godot's own default, 1).
- A whole number typed in the Ghost Row with a range (\"max_jumps is a whole number from 1 to 3, in
  the Inspector\") writes `@export_range(1, 3)` rather than `@export_range(1, 3, 1)`.
- Such a range's Inspector look shows Godot's default step, 1, instead of an empty step that read as
  a slider moving by nothing.

### Fixed: a selected variable's help strip fits beside its doors

- The status-bar strip under a selected variable repeated the row's own type, name and value and
  then the Inspector facts before saying anything new, so at ordinary editor widths the sentence was
  cut off before the doors. It now says the one thing the row cannot - why the variable is outside
  the group above it, else its slider (\"A slider from 1 to 3 in the Inspector.\"), else where the
  Inspector shows it - and hovering it says the whole, type, name and value first. The strip also
  takes a larger share of the bar beside the status message.

### Added: make a script class like inserting an object, and change it from its Properties

- Sheet ▸ New Class… (and the Project bar's New class…) opens Insert new object: tiles grouped by
  kind (2D, 3D, User interface, Data and project) or the class tree the way Godot's Create New Node
  lists it, with Godot's own Favorites and Recent from that dialog. Search reaches every class a
  script can extend. The chosen kind's sentence stays on screen, a taken or unwritable name is said
  beside the field and holds Insert back, and the window shows the head the file will start with
  before Insert writes `<name>.gd` in the FileSystem dock's folder and opens it.
- Selecting a line of the sheet's class head shows Object type in the Properties bar: Name
  (Rename… lists every file first), Type (a described dropdown; a type that lacks members the events
  use names each of them before it changes anything), Description, Icon (a preview of the icon
  Godot will show, Pick… or Use default), Runs in the editor and File. Each writes only its own head
  line, then the object's instance variables follow as for a selected object.
### Changed: making your own behaviour and your own global is a form, not a commented script

- **Sheet > New behaviour…** replaces New Behaviour Addon…. It asks the name, what the behaviour works
  on (Any node, Node2D, CharacterBody2D and the rest, each described, the chosen one's sentence kept in
  view), the shelf it sits on in the Add behaviour list (the shelves the installed packs use, each
  named by the packs on it), its icon (the Works-on class's own editor icon unless you choose another),
  a description, and what to start from: Empty, a copy of a shipped pack made for the same kind of
  object, a script you wrote or the selected node's script (its exported variables become the
  behaviour's properties; the script itself is not changed). Create writes the behaviour the way every
  pack is written - a folder under eventsheet_addons/, `host` typed as what it works on - and opens it
  as a sheet. The teaching skeleton and the two recipes are gone.
- **Sheet > New global…** asks the name, Script or Scene (the one choice Godot asks of an autoload),
  the picker heading, the icon, a description and what to start from: Empty, a copy of a pack meant to
  run as an autoload, a script you wrote, or an autoload you already have (opened as a sheet, nothing
  moved). Create writes the file - and the scene, for a Scene - and turns it on as its row in Project
  Settings > Globals > Autoload, one undo step.
- **Sheet > Properties and actions…** lists what the open behaviour or global offers: its Properties
  (or Global values) as a Name / Type / Default / What it means table with the lines they are in code,
  then its Actions, Conditions, Triggers and Expressions, each kind a coloured word and a \"returns\" chip
  where it gives a value back; clicking a name shows its row. Each list's Add opens the window that
  already writes that kind of member. A global's tick turns it on or off in Godot's own Autoload list.
- The Add behaviour list puts your own behaviours first under Your behaviours, greys a pack made for
  another kind of object (\"Made for a Node2D - Enemy is a Node.\") or one that is a global, with the
  reason on hover, and adding one is a step Ctrl+Z takes back out of the scene.
- A global is its own object at the top of every picker, straight after System, whether it is a
  global made here (its annotated actions) or an autoload the project already had (reflected, as Your
  Project reflects a class, which no longer lists autoloads). A global made as a scene emits the same
  `GameState.add_score(1)` calls as one made as a script.
- Size: the old scaffold and its dialog were 531 lines; the generator, the two forms and the new window
  are 890. The generator shrank by half (a new behaviour is a sheet compiled to GDScript, not text
  written out by hand); the growth is the global form and the Properties and actions window, which
  had no counterpart before. Both reuse the window kit and the Anatomy census rather than new widgets.

### Fixed: Sheet > Publish New Version… opens its window again

- The menu entry called a dock function that no longer existed, so choosing it did nothing but
  print a script error. It opens the version window again.
- A gate now reads every call the menus and the dock's helpers make into the dock, and fails when
  one names a function, variable or signal the dock (or the helper it goes through) does not have.

### Fixed: the Inspector Designer and the Inspector's badges speak the editor's language

- The Inspector Designer window (title, intro, empty hint, button tooltips, \"Dragged in the
  viewport:\") and the Inspector drawers' own words - the Required and validate badges, the action
  button's tooltip, the slider, corner, link and table tooltips - read in the editor's language
  instead of English in every one. The validate badge with no function named now reads \"validated
  by a sheet function while you edit\" rather than \"a sheet function()\".
- The Designer's intro and chip text take the window kit's text colours, so they read in a light
  editor theme.
- ▲ in the Designer moves a variable above the next one UP in the Inspector. It used to swap with
  the variable just above it in the file even when that one is not shown in the Inspector, which
  changed nothing anybody could see.
### Changed: Sheet Type, Manage Includes and Import event sheet say what they will write

- Sheet Type's \"Ships as\" line is the band across the top, with a \"?\" where the field that is wrong
  goes; the problem is said under the fields, the field is marked, and the foot says which field to
  fix while OK waits. The chosen kind's description stays in view under Kind, and the help strip
  shows the `extends` line the sheet will compile to.
- Manage Includes shows what each include brings beside its name (\"3 function(s)\", \"file not
  found\"), and the help strip says what the selected one is and every function and variable it
  ADDS. Move up and Move down are named. Add… opens a picker of the project's sheet files in which
  a file already included, one that would include this sheet back, or one that is not a sheet is
  marked the moment it is chosen, and Include waits. Includes stay `.tres` sheets: the compiler
  merges sheet resources, so a `.gd` script cannot be an include.
- Import event sheet names the sheet coming across, the file it becomes and how many rows mapped
  in a band across the top; the object grid has column heads (Object, Becomes, Node); the report
  sits beside the preview; the long explanations moved into the help strip; and when Save as…
  cannot compile the sheet, the reason is said in the foot beside the button.

### Changed: the Start page, the Welcome, the tour and New Event Sheet say what a choice does first

- The Start page's first column opens with \"Open an existing script…\", so a project that already
  has scripts starts there, then offers every starter the New-Sheet menu has - the 3D controllers,
  the menus and the autoloads included - under shelves (2D, 3D, UI and menus, Behaviours,
  Project-wide, Game shapes, Data, Editor tools, Showcases), with chips that narrow it to one
  shelf. Each row shows its kind beside its name. A click selects and the help strip says what the
  row is and what opening it does; Open, Enter or a double-click opens it.
- The Welcome's \"how do you usually make games?\" answer is chosen first and applied by Apply; the
  help strip says what it would set (words, theme, Project bar, GDScript panel, tour). Before, a
  click applied it at once, and \"I'm new\" closed the window before Preferences could be read. What
  the plugin is reads as the band across the top, and the four Get Started doors sit two by two.
- The tour's foot reads Back on the left, then the progress as dots and a count, Skip tour and Next.
- New Event Sheet names the exact file Create writes, and a name already taken in the folder says
  so beside the field and holds Create, instead of quietly writing a suffixed file. Each starter in
  Start from carries its one-line description, and the help strip shows the line the sheet starts
  with.
### Changed: New Custom Resource is now New Data Table, filled in like a spreadsheet

- Sheet > New Data Table… opens one small spreadsheet instead of three questions: name what one
  row is (`LootDrop` - the Name, `LootDropTable`, is derived from it), then fill a grid. Click a
  column's header to select it and set its name and kind beside the grid - Text, Number (whole),
  Number, True/false, Choice (with its words), Colour or Translation key, each saying what it holds
  as the pointer rests on it; double-click a header to rename its column; drag a column's right edge
  to resize it and double-click the edge to fit it. + Column (the last header) and + Row add, Delete
  removes the selected row, and a cell is typed into, ticked or picked from a dropdown by its kind.
- Import from CSV… reads a CSV's header line as the columns (a column of numbers comes in as a
  number column) and its lines as the rows; Export to CSV… writes the rows back out.
- Insert writes two files into the folder open in the FileSystem dock - the Resource script, opened
  as a sheet, and a .tres holding the rows typed - and refuses, on the window, a row with no name, a
  column name GDScript cannot use, a class name already taken, or a file already there.
- \"Warn in the Inspector when a row is missing a value\" makes the script a tool script and gives
  it a check that warns until the table has rows and names the first row with an empty text cell;
  the tick it replaces asked only for rows, and its validator never ran in the editor because the
  script was not a tool script.
- Remove column, in the panel beside the grid, takes a column out of every row.

### Changed: the Modes and States windows name the rows a removed name leaves behind, as you type

- Taking a mode or a state off the list now says so under the field while you type - \"Cutscene left
  the list - 2 row(s) still name it.\", \"Hurt left the list - 3 row(s) still say State.HURT.\" - before
  OK is pressed. The On entering event the Modes window wrote for that mode goes with it on OK, so it
  is not counted. The windows are titled \"Edit the game's modes\" and \"Declare this object's states\",
  and OK reads Declare Modes / Declare States.
- The Modes window's words are translated, and its Physics and Mouse answers each say what they do
  when the pointer rests on them.

### Fixed: an empty node or resource slot previews the line it saves

- The variable window's IN CODE line for a node or resource slot left empty read
  `@export var falloff: Curve = \"\"`, which does not parse, while Save wrote the slot with no value.
  The preview now shows `@export var falloff: Curve`, the line Save writes.

### Fixed: two hand-written declarations open as variables instead of code lines

- `@export_subgroup(\"\")`, which ends only the subgroup before the next property, now opens as a fact
  of the variable under it (the way `@export_group(\"\")` already did) and saves back as written; the
  Properties table writes it too.
- `@onready var label = $Label`, written with no type, now opens as an `@onready` variable that reads
  as Variant - the node lookup implies no type - and saves back without one.

### Changed: a behaviour or a global opens as its own file, in its file's order

- A behaviour (a pack's node script, or a sheet made as one), a global (an autoload) and any other
  file of a pack open under the slim title row an edited sheet has, with a quiet kind word -
  `Behaviour` or `Global (autoload)` - where any other script says Script, and every row reads where
  the file has it. The head bar that folded the file's first lines away, the Settings / Verbs / How
  it works / Internal state bands, the Triggers bar and the Helpers bar are gone for these files;
  lines the sheet cannot read stay code blocks, as before.
- Their facts live in the hover of the file's name - what it works on or the name it loads as, the
  category it is listed under, how many properties or global values it has and how many actions,
  conditions, triggers and expressions it offers - and a click on the name offers Open in the script
  editor and Show in FileSystem. A double-click still renames the class. A global's Autoload entry
  is said there too, instead of as a row.
- The title strip of such a file carries a Sheet | Code | Side by side switch: the rows, the file as
  code, or both next to each other.

### Changed: a function is drawn like an event on every sheet, and edited in the Properties bar

- A function reads `Action  Function  On take_damage` on every sheet, written or only read: its
  kind as a coloured word (Action green, Condition blue, Expression purple, the theme's role colours,
  never a glyph), then its name exactly as the code writes it, a `returns float` chip when it hands
  something back (none for void), its settings as small tags (In Add Action, Waits, Shared,
  Featured) and each parameter on a line of its own - `PARAM int amount = 1` - whose hover says what
  it is for and its value when a call leaves it out.
- Its own actions - the first step of its body, when that step asks nothing of its own - sit in the
  action lane beside the head, the way any event puts \"do this\" beside \"when this happens\", instead
  of a separate \"Always\" row under it. They stay live on a sheet you are writing: Add action on the
  head fills that step, and clicking or editing one of them edits the step's own action.
- Function heads carry an event number like any event, counted where the sheet draws them, and the
  rest of their body is numbered after them; Go to event, Find and the Doctor count the same way.
- The description is not drawn on the row. Hovering the name shows it (with how Add Action reads
  the verb, who else calls it, and how to edit it); a double-click on the name opens a small window
  beside the row that edits it as one undo step. The italic description line, the \"+ describe this
  function\" placeholder, the reading-only \"ƒ\" block with its input chips and kind word, and the
  role wash are gone.
- The native theme's default ACE kind colours changed: Action was amber and is now green, Condition
  was teal and is now blue, and Expression is a plainer purple, in a dark and in a light editor, so
  a kind reads the same colour wherever it is named - on the rows, in the picker preview, the
  Anatomy panel and the function window's pill. A theme you saved yourself keeps its own colours.
- Clicking a function's head selects it and the Properties bar shows Name (renaming renames every
  call), Description, Returns, In Add Action, Category, Waits and Shared - every dropdown describing
  each choice - and its parameters, retyped and given defaults in place, reordered by dragging the
  handle, added with + Add. Each change is one undo step and nothing is written when nothing
  changed. A read-only preview keeps the popup that only reads. (It is a panel of the Properties
  bar, not Godot's own Inspector.)
- The New / Edit Function window is on the window kit: a band showing the row it will draw, the
  Function card (with the code name beside Name), one described Kind dropdown in place of the three
  cards, the help strip with the `func` line as IN CODE, the live name in the title, and the reason
  a name cannot be used in amber. Every word of it reads in the editor's language.

### Changed: the parameters window is laid out the familiar way

- The window is titled \"Parameters for Player: Move toward\", and its head names the object and the
  action with the action's description under them (and the class-reference link for a native
  node's action). The live sentence band at the top is gone: the sheet shows the row once Done is
  pressed, and the strip under the rows still gives the GDScript line it will write.
- One row per parameter, name left and box right. The row the strip is speaking about is lit, and a
  problem is said in one sentence under its own row as well as beside Done. The window now opens
  already saying what is wrong with a value it was handed, rather than only once you type in it.
- The Expressions panel sits beside the form: search it, double-click an entry, and it is inserted
  at the caret of the box that last had focus; its band names the verb, the field and what the
  field holds now. It replaces the floating dictionary and the button beside every expression box.
- A parameter that names a node (a node picked from the scene, an \"On node\" target, or one typed as
  a node class) carries \"click to choose\", which opens the node search; the pick replaces what the
  box said. The magnifier beside every expression box is gone.
- A position written as a literal - a lone vector parameter, or one declared as a Vector2/Vector3 -
  splits into X and Y (and Z) rows, one expression box each.
- Every dropdown is a described one: each choice carries its line as Godot's own item tooltip (or the
  code it writes, when its words differ from it), and the closed dropdown says the chosen one's.
  The muted caption beside a dropdown is gone. An angle shows its unit beside the box.
- Back, Done and Cancel, with Back leading; Apply & Add Another stays in the picker loop.
- Text and tones on the window follow the editor theme, so a light editor reads as well as a dark one.

### Changed: the picking windows say what the pick answers

- **Add Action / Condition** names the object in its title (\"Add an action to Player\") and shows
  the event the pick lands on in a band above the search (\"Player Is on floor - the action you
  pick lands here\"). Its description panel is now the help strip every other window wears: the
  verb's name, kind and category, what it does and how often this sheet uses it, the line it
  ships as, and its guide link. A verb found by another engine's word (\"WaitForSeconds\") says so
  on an ALSO CALLED line instead of on the row's tail. With nothing highlighted, or on the object
  cards, the strip says what the window is for - the hint line above the list is gone. A Help (F1)
  button, and F1 itself, open the highlighted verb's Manual page; the Recent pane loses its star.
- **The Expressions dictionary** names the field in its title (\"Insert an expression into Speed\"),
  bands the verb, field and what the field holds now (kept in step as results chain in), gains a
  Close button beside Insert, and says what the list is and what the highlighted entry inserts in
  its strip. (It has since become the parameters window's Expressions panel, above, which keeps
  the band.)
- **Pick Node** names the field in its title (\"Pick a node for Target\") and bands it the same way;
  its strip says what the highlighted node is and the reference it goes in as; Used in sheet rows
  now show their class and icon; a Cancel button sits beside Use Node.
- All three read in the editor's language and in the light theme: their group colours are
  darkened on a light panel, and the missing-reference red follows the error tone.

### Fixed: the Inspector preview's summary reads in the editor's language

- The plain-words summary under the Inspector preview (and `EventSheets.describe_inspector`) was
  one English sentence glued from phrase fragments, so it could not be translated. It is now one
  short whole sentence per fact - \"A whole number. It runs from 0 to 100. Shown as a progress bar.
  It is grouped under Combat.\" - and every sentence reads in the editor's language. A button
  named by its method alone is named as written rather than capitalised.

### Changed: the Doctor's Migration section reads the remembered pack vocabulary

- The Migration section loaded and reflected every pack script to learn the installed vocabulary,
  about 6.5 seconds of each audit, although the registry already keeps that answer in `.godot/`
  between sessions. It now reads the same memory, so only a pack that changed is loaded again: on
  this project the section went from 9.7 to 2.3 seconds and the whole audit from 47 to 40.

### Changed: twenty-two scripts the first open compiled and never called wait for their moment

- The first sheet open compiled the sheet export, the token index a `{uid}` mint draws against, the
  class vocabulary filter, the pack annotation writer and reading check, the Loose Ends walk, the
  command palette, the GDScript lint, the project search and Find References, the tileset and
  damage type facts, the picker recipes and quick-add scorer, the snippet codec, number scrubbing,
  the runtime error wording, the verb suggestion, rescue tips, workspaces, the inline format bar
  and the text listing - and, run with every function counted, called none of them. Each is now
  read by path when its own moment comes (a paste, a search, an error from the running game, a
  pack being curated), and the error strip's button label is spelled beside a test that holds it
  equal to its source.
- Workspace first-open closure: 324 files / 159,464 lines -> 303 / 154,300. Timed in a busy
  machine the change sits inside the noise (three alternating pairs, medians: editor load 17.6 s
  -> 17.0 s, build 22.4 s -> 20.9 s); the closure figure is the one to hold it to.
### Added: a variable row's own gestures - its doors, its submenus, its groups and its buttons

- **The help strip speaks for a selected variable.** Under the sheet it reads the row as code - `int
  health = 10 · in the Inspector · not in a group` - adds why a prefix group shuts it out or the range
  its slider keeps, and offers the doors that fit: Move into a group…, Change look…, Edit range… and
  Properties…. Nothing is added inside the row.
- **Right-click > Inspector look and Group.** Inspector look lists the plain editor and every look the
  type can wear, ticked at the one it wears; a look with details (min, max, choices) ends in \"…\" and
  opens the variable window on them. Group lists the sheet's groups (Move into), New group… (the old
  Group Under a Heading), Group by prefix… and End group here.
- **Group by prefix…** proposes the start the selected names share (`bullet_count`, `bullet_speed`
  -> Bullet / bullet) and writes the one line `@export_group(\"Bullet\", \"bullet\")` above the first.
  Only a whole word counts: `speed` and `spread` propose nothing rather than a group \"Sp\".
- **End group here** writes `@export_group(\"\")` above the variable, and the end line it draws can be
  dragged onto another variable to move where the group ends.
- **Add > Inspector button…** asks for the label, an editor icon (checked against the editor's own
  icons), the function it runs - one of the sheet's or a new one - and its group, showing the code as
  it is typed. It writes Godot's own spelling, `@export_tool_button(\"Bake\", \"Bake\") var bake_button =
  _bake`, which opens back as the same row. The canvas menu's Add Inspector Button… asks the same.
- **The Ghost Row reads a variable sentence.** `max_jumps is a whole number from 1 to 3, in the
  Inspector` declares `@export_range(1, 3, 1) var max_jumps: int = 1`; \"saved but hidden\" and \"in
  group Bullet\" are understood too.
- **The variable window searches its types and fills its slots.** Search types… beside the Type
  dropdown finds a type by its name, its words or its sentence, and any node or resource class by
  name. A node slot among the events gets Pick (the editor's node picker; the value becomes `$Muzzle`
  looked up when the node is ready), a resource slot Load (`preload(\"res://…\")`) and New (`Curve.new()`).

### Changed: Match is written as branches, and each branch fills like any event

- **The Match window is laid out like the parameters window.** A Value (this sheet's variables
  offered as you type, with the type and description of the one picked), one row per branch -
  \"Match 1  IDLE\" - and Otherwise (Else, or nothing). One match per state adds a branch for every
  value of the enum the Value holds; a value matched twice is refused before Done. A branch reads
  back by its own name (IDLE) and is written with its enum's (State.IDLE). The window no longer
  edits branch bodies as code. It has Done and Cancel but no Back: it opens from the row menu or a
  double-click, never from the picker, so there is no earlier step to go back to.
- **On the sheet a switch on plain values reads as branches.** The event says \"match state\"
  beside its trigger, and every branch is a sub-event stating its own test in the sheet's comparison
  grammar - \"state is IDLE\" (a member of one of this sheet's enums by its own name, any other value
  as written), several values as an OR block, Else - with its own Add action: the
  picked action lands in that branch, replacing the `pass` that held an empty one's place. An empty
  branch shows its Add action rather than \"pass\". This replaces the \"decides by\" caption, the Else
  chips the later branches wore, and the \"Is in Patrol\" / \"State: PATROL\" arm readings for such
  matches.
- **Add Match To Actions… adds nothing until Done**, then the match and its branches in one undo
  step (it used to add a placeholder match first, so a Cancel left one behind). Re-editing keeps
  every branch's actions under its new value, an untouched Done is no edit, and removing a branch
  that holds actions says how many Done will drop.
- The Edit Enum and Edit Signal windows are on the window kit: a band showing the line they write,
  a card per part, the help strip with its IN CODE line, and a red edge on the field a problem is
  about, with OK held until it is fixed. Both, and the Match window, read in the editor's language.

### Changed: the small writing windows say what they write before OK

- **Connect Signal** shows the event that will land in a band, notes the signals the sheet already
  handles (\"in door.gd\", and a warning that Connect adds a second event), shows the handler line it
  writes, and has a Cancel button.
- **Paste Special** splits Objects and Variables into two cards, each field with its note under it,
  and says what the paste will change in one line.
- **Insert Snippet** is one window: the library (searchable, names exactly as saved, how many blanks
  each asks for) and the chosen snippet's blanks to fill, with Insert held while one is empty. The
  separate blanks window is gone.
- **Save Selection as Snippet** is on the kit, with Cancel; a name the library already has is said
  in amber before saving - the first is kept and this one is written as name-2 - instead of being
  refused after the click.
- **Edit Parameter** shows the function's row with this input marked and the `func` line it writes;
  a default ahead of an input without one is refused in the window (it used to close and refuse in
  the status bar), and the \"Delete this parameter\" tick read on Apply is now a red Delete button.
- **Edit Comment** shows the `#` or `##` line in the band and the strip, and the swatch says \"theme
  default\" beside itself instead of an \"Alpha 0\" hint. An untouched Save is still no edit.
- **Compare** shows the row it writes in a band at the top (its title names the object); the strip
  keeps the IN CODE line.
- **Add behavior**: the search and the shelves share one row, a pack's knobs are typed Inspector
  fields (a number you can scrub, a tick) under their own names with the pack's doc line in the help
  strip, the strip says where the pack will land, and a search that hides the chosen pack moves the
  choice to the first match. Every window here reads in the editor's language.

### Fixed: the variable window speaks the editor's language

- The variable window, its look list and its type list were English in every language: the card
  titles, field names, ticks, placeholders, refusals, the \"Keep in step\" choices, every type's
  words and sentence, every Inspector look's name, hover and detail fields, and each field's help
  paragraph. All of it now reads in the editor's language, and so do the help strip's READS AS and
  IN CODE labels and the preview card's caption. The look and type tables stay English in the code
  as the keys their translations are filed under, and a test holds every word they show to a row
  in all eight languages. The values a look stores (a file picker's reach) stay the same whatever
  language picked them.
- **A typo in a `var hp := 100` value is refused.** The words of an inferred declaration are
  written as they stand, so the window now checks they still give the same type - `abc` or `5.0`
  on an int is refused with the reason, before it reaches the file. Ticking Write the type in code
  lifts the check, since the type is then written out.
- A variable opened with a subgroup prefix or a group end, then saved with its subgroup cleared or
  a group given, no longer keeps those keys: they meant nothing any more, and the file was already
  right without them.

### Changed: the Show if lift loads only for a file that needs it

- The lift that reads a generated `_validate_property` back onto its variables is loaded by path,
  and only for a file carrying the generated function's doc line, so the first open of every other
  file no longer compiles it.

### Fixed: two flock members on one spot always part

- Flock and Flock 3D send a member that sits exactly on a neighbour away along an angle of its own.
  That angle was its instance id's remainder by 360, so two members whose ids differed by a multiple
  of 360 took the same angle and stayed stuck together - about one pair in 360, and which pair
  depended on how many objects the game had made before them. The angle is now a hash of the id
  spread over the whole turn.

### Fixed: dialog text is readable in a light editor theme

- The help strip (\"live, as you pick\": the heading, the description, READS AS and IN CODE), the
  Inspector preview card's group name, tables, result cards and the function, variable, parameter,
  mode, state and wizard dialogs drew their text in fixed light greys made for the dark theme, on
  panels that follow the editor theme - so in a light theme it all but vanished. Text on a dialog
  panel now takes the editor theme's own Label colour (`EventSheetPopupUI.text_color()`), with
  quieter `secondary_text_color()` / `muted_text_color()` voices moved toward the panel fill, and
  the ok / warning / error tones are darkened on a light panel (`readable_on_panel()`). A test sweeps
  the dialog files so a dark-only text colour cannot come back.

### Changed: the variable window is four cards, every look a described choice

- **Variable** asks for the row in the order it reads: scope, name, type, initial value,
  description, flags. **The type list names every GDScript type** as the code writes it, grouped
  (Basic, Text, Vectors and maths, Colour, Nodes and resources, Collections, Packed arrays,
  Special), with the plain words beside it (\"int · Whole number\") and a sentence on hover. The
  sheet's own enums are types of their own. A node or resource slot takes any class in a **Class**
  field beside it (Sprite2D, EnemyStats), and a type the list does not name is kept, never lost.
  The Number / Text / Boolean aliases and their \"Whole numbers only\" tick are gone - int and float
  are listed as what they are. Change Type Everywhere offers the same list.
- **The initial value is edited the way the Inspector would**: a tick for a bool, a swatch for a
  colour, one coloured field per axis for a vector, `&\"` / `^\"` before a StringName or NodePath,
  an enum's members or the sheet's functions offered as you type, and \"Edit items…\" for a list.
  A value written as code (`Kind.ROUND`, `&\"jump\"`, `_bake`) reaches the file as code on both of
  the sheet's stores; an empty node slot starts at `null`.
- **Write the type in code** is a tick on every row placed among the events: ticked the line names
  its type, unticked it is written as `var damage = 3`.
- **In the Inspector** carries the \"Editable in the Inspector\" tick on its heading and one
  **Inspector look** dropdown, which lists only the looks the type can wear - slider, angle in
  degrees, exponential slider, slider with no handle, dropdown of numbers, checkbox flags, layers
  grid (which layers is a detail), easing curve (either flag or both), file and folder pickers with
  their reach, big text box, hint inside the box, dropdown, dropdown with free typing, password
  dots, expression field, solid colour, linked axes, node path filtered, Inspector button (label
  and editor icon, checked against the editor's own icons), every drawer, your own hint
  (`@export_custom(hint, \"text\", usage)`) and saved but hidden. Every option says on hover what the
  Inspector will show and the annotation it writes, the chosen one's annotation sits muted beside
  the dropdown, and its details sit under it.
- **Where it sits in the Inspector** has the Category, Group and Subgroup, each completing from the
  sections the sheet already has, and **Only names starting** for a prefix group: ticking it offers
  the start the group's names share, and a name that would fall outside the group says so.
- **When it changes** folds away on changed (with Wait for ready), show if, lock unless, link
  with, clamp, read-only, the decor fields and the setter and getter bodies, and says how the
  Inspector is refreshed. Simple Mode hides it.
- **The live column** shows the row the window writes, the whole block of code it compiles to -
  category, group, description and declaration - and the Inspector preview card, which now draws
  the category band and an Inspector button.
- The Look Gallery window is gone: the described dropdown and the preview card do its job.

### Fixed: what the variable window sends is what a row keeps

- **Editing a variable the file opened no longer drops its look.** A row placed among the events
  kept only its sections through a save; a range, file picker, flags, layers grid, enum values and
  the rest were thrown away. Every attribute the window sends is kept now, and anything it has no
  field for (a group's end line, which side of the sections its `##` sat) rides through untouched.
- **Editing a sheet-level variable keeps its attributes, choices and Inspector tick**; the window
  used to open it with none of them and save it back without them.
- **A Big text box compiles from a row** (`@export_multiline`), and one a file wrote reopens on that
  look.
- **The help strip and the preview card read in the light editor theme**: their text takes the
  theme's own colour instead of a fixed pale one.

### Added: every Inspector export option opens as the variable it belongs to

- **A group with a name prefix** - `@export_group(\"Bullet\", \"bullet\")` - and a subgroup with one
  open as grouped variables instead of a block of code above them, and save back unchanged. The
  prefix is the variable's `group_prefix` / `subgroup_prefix` attribute.
- **The end of a group** - `@export_group(\"\")` - is a fact of the variable after it
  (`group_end`), and the sheet's settings folder now closes there instead of running on.
- **An untyped variable** (`@export var damage = 3`, `var _cooldown = 0.0`, `const GRAVITY = 900`)
  opens as a variable row whose type is the one its value implies (int, float, ...), and saves
  back with no type written, exactly as it was. The new `untyped` field says so; clearing it writes
  the type in the code.
- **`@export_file_path`** opens as a file picker that keeps the path as typed (`plain_path` on the
  file spec), and **a free-form `@export_custom(hint, \"text\", usage)`** opens as the variable's own
  `custom_hint` {hint, text, usage} instead of a line of code. Both compile from the same fields.
- The hand-written-project round trip gained `turret.gd`, a script using every one of these next to
  the category, NodePath, range-extra and easing-flag spellings that already lifted.

### Changed: export groups read on the sheet the way the Inspector shows them

- **`@export_category` is a heavy centred band**, and **`@export_subgroup` is its own tinted,
  foldable strip inside its group**, holding its own variables. A group strip names itself in bold,
  and its outline now wraps every variable in the group, not just the first.
- **`@export_group(\"\")` draws as the line the group ends on** (\"Gun ends here\"), inside the strip, so
  where a group stops is never a guess.
- **Prefix groups** (`@export_group(\"Bullet\", \"bullet\")`) show their prefix (`bullet*`) beside the
  name, and each member's matching start is tinted. The first exported name that does not start with
  the prefix is outside the group, as the Inspector has it - and selecting it says why in the help
  strip under the sheet (\"Not in Bullet: that group only takes names starting bullet.\"), never inside
  the row. A section line the importer leaves as a script line still reads this way, and a
  double-click on it still edits the line.
- **Hovering a group's name** says what kind of section it is, what it holds and the line that
  declares it. The hover is on the name only.
- **A type the code does not write** (`var hp := 100`) reads in grey italics, its hover says Godot
  takes it from the value, and **Properties offers \"Write the type in code (: int)\"**, which turns the
  line into `var hp: int = 100` in one undo step. Saving such a variable from Properties untouched now
  keeps its value exactly as written (`5.0` stays `5.0`), and keeps the category it opens.
- **The badge tiles' hovers end with the code that earns them** - `@export`, `@export_range(0, 600)`,
  the `##` line.
### Added: a tool sheet's variables, written both ways

- **Show if and Lock unless work on a `.gd` sheet.** A sheet variable (the rows a `.gd` sheet keeps
  its variables in) with a Show if or a Lock unless now compiles exactly as on a `.tres` sheet: one
  generated `_validate_property` straight after the last variable, and the variable it reads gets a
  setter that calls `notify_property_list_changed()`, so the field appears or locks the moment the
  box is ticked instead of after the node is selected again. Before, the dialog's fields were
  dropped on a sheet variable without a word.
- **On changed, and Wait for ready.** A sheet variable's On changed calls its function from the
  setter. The new `wait_for_ready` option puts that call behind `if is_node_ready():`, because a
  tool script's setters also run while the scene loads, before the children the function reaches
  exist. It works on dictionary variables too.
- **A sheet-written file reopens as the options that wrote it.** The generated `_validate_property`
  goes back onto the variables as their Show if / Lock unless, and the setters that only refresh the
  Inspector go with it; the file still saves byte for byte. A hand-edited function stays the
  function, and a file that writes its own `_validate_property` keeps it: the generated one stands
  aside (with a warning naming the variables it leaves to it) instead of writing a second.
- The generated function now follows every variable, tree rows included. On a `.tres` sheet with
  dictionary variables it used to sit straight after those, before the tree variables, so a saved
  file moves it once.
- **Add An Inspector Button For It...** on a function's row asks what the button should say (the
  function's name, read as words, to start with) and shows the `@export_tool_button` line it adds.
  Ctrl+Z removes it again.
- **Saved but hidden survives an edit.** Editing a sheet variable that is `@export_storage`, or an
  Inspector button row, through the Variable dialog no longer turns it back into a plain export.
- A group show-if comment (`# @inspector_show_if`) in a file with no `_validate_property` of its
  own now stays the comment it is, rather than becoming an option whose save would write a function
  the file never had.

### Changed: the first sheet opens with less to compile

- **Six scripts the first open never uses are compiled when they are first asked for instead.**
  The Doctor's Files section (asked for one sentence when a scene-trust note is selected), the
  translation scanner and the grid file codec (a pseudo column, a project walk), the Inspector mock
  a hover over an exported variable shows, the Project bar (off unless the project asks for it) and
  the Hierarchy pane. The workspace's first open compiles 14 fewer files and 7,255 fewer lines
  (337 files and 165,891 lines before, 323 and 158,636 after). Measured interleaved against the previous build on the same machine (three
  runs each, medians): loading the workspace editor 12.3 s -> 11.5 s, building it 15.1 s -> 14.3 s;
  those six scripts cost about 1.4 s to compile once something does ask for them.
- **The compiler stays on the first open, on purpose.** Opening a `.gd` as a sheet re-emits every
  lifted row and the whole sheet to prove the bytes come back unchanged, and that proof is the
  compiler, so loading it later would only move the same wait into the open's background job.

### Changed: quick questions take one plain shape

- **A quick question now reads the same everywhere.** Its title is the question, one plain sentence
  says what happens, a short list shows what it touches, and a muted last line says whether Ctrl+Z
  undoes it. The safe answer is the blue button Enter presses; Esc and the close box always cancel;
  a risky answer (delete, discard, use the other version) is its own red-outlined button on the
  left, so neither Enter nor Esc can take it.
- **Keep this event?** - taking the last condition off a top-level event asks in those words, with
  Keep it as the blue answer and Delete the event on the left.
- **A file changed outside the editor** - on a tab with no unsaved edits the question offers Reload
  as the safe answer; with unsaved edits, Keep mine is the safe answer and Use the file on disk is
  the red one. Taking the file on disk first keeps the version in the editor, unsaved edits and all,
  as a backup in Sheet Backups, and the prompt says so (Ctrl+Z still cannot bring the edits back).
  Refocusing the editor while the question is open no longer asks it a second time.
- **Rename (a variable) asks Rename health?** and keeps Rename greyed, saying why, until the name
  typed is one the sheet accepts. Afterwards a short receipt - **Renamed health to hit_points** -
  lists the places it changed: this sheet with its count, each sheet that includes it (saved), and
  any hand-written script that still calls the old name (not changed - check it).
- **Rename class asks Rename Player to Hero?** as you type, lists every file it will change with
  its count before it changes any, and refuses a name another class already has, saying so. The
  question shows at once and fills that list a moment later, so a big project does not stall the
  click. Its note says what Ctrl+Z reaches: this sheet only - other open tabs change and stay
  unsaved, and closed files are saved, each with a backup in Sheet Backups.
- **The question's badge and red button read in a light editor theme too**, and its sentence wraps
  at the display's scale, so a 2x screen no longer wraps it at half the width.
- **Keep old events working?** (the providers panel's forwarding stand-in) shows the exact lines
  it would add to the script as you type the old name, and the button stays greyed until there is
  one to add.
- **Save before closing?**, **Clear every bookmark?**, **Make a variable local to one event?** and
  **Rename the translation key too?** take the same shape: Save and Keep Bookmarks stay the Enter answers, Discard and Clear all are
  the red buttons on the left.

### Added: the window kit's dropdown, result card and state note

- **A dropdown says what each choice means.** Every choice carries its sentence as Godot's own item
  tooltip, so the open list explains a choice as the pointer rests on it, and the closed dropdown's
  tooltip is the chosen one's sentence. The variable dialog's Scope and Type, the retype dialog's
  type, the function dialog's \"What kind of value?\", and the Save studio's Format and export
  dropdowns all read this way (the last three gained their sentences with it, in all nine
  languages).
- **A result card says what just happened**: a titled panel with a short flow of figures and one
  line per outcome, led by a green, amber or red mark and closed by a chip naming the file it
  touched. The grid CSV and language-variants windows answer in it instead of a bare label, and the
  language-variants warning about a preloading script now reads as its own amber line.
- **A state note** is one sentence with a coloured edge under the field it is about, hidden when
  there is nothing to say. The variable dialog's name clash and the New Behaviour Addon dialog's
  name problem use it in place of hand-tinted labels.

### Tests: a real-editor boot budget, and scripts from a project the plugin has never touched

- **`tools/measure_editor_boot.gd` times the two waits a person meets inside a real editor**:
  enabling the plugin (`EditorInterface.set_plugin_enabled`, on an editor that booted without it)
  and opening the first sheet through the plugin's own door until its lift lands. Measured three
  times at this commit: a visible editor enables in 443-465 ms (median 447) and opens the first
  sheet in 23.5-26.6 s (median 26.5); headless, 446-497 ms (median 460) and 27.3-29.3 s (median
  27.6). `editor_boot_budget_test` runs it as a subprocess in the serial tail and fails past
  1,500 ms or 85 s - about three times the medians, so it catches a regression in kind without
  flapping on a shared runner.
- **`existing_project_round_trip_test`** opens five hand-written scripts of the kind an existing
  project already holds (a player with `@export` categories and groups, an autoload with an inner
  class, a `Resource` with `class_name`, a `@tool` script, an enum state machine in a `match`) and
  asks each the standing contracts `tools/verify_sheets.gd` asks. All five re-emit byte for byte
  today; a file that ever does not is listed as expected to degrade, with its reason, instead of
  being quietly weakened.
- **A tighter WARN line beside each budget**: about 1.5 times the medians (690 ms and 41.4 s). Past
  one the test prints a loud `[boot] WARN` line and still passes. The parallel launcher repeats
  every `[boot]` line under its verdict, and CI writes them into the tail job's summary and keeps
  the tail log with the run, so the numbers can be compared from run to run.

### Removed: the Addon manager - a pack is a folder, as everywhere else in Godot

- **Tools ▸ Addon manager… is gone**, and with it the pack Update… and Restore… windows and the
  pack import, update-check and hashing record they used. A pack is a folder of ordinary behaviour
  scripts under `eventsheet_addons/`: you see it in the FileSystem dock, add one from the Asset
  Library or by unpacking a .zip there, and remove one by deleting its folder - version control
  undoes that like any other delete. Taking a newer version is replacing the folder's files and
  reading the diff version control shows. Nothing else changes for the rows: a verb a new version
  retires still forwards to its successor and compiles to the same line, and the Doctor's Migration
  section still names every row whose verb is no longer installed.
- **There is no switch to turn a pack off any more.** The scanner no longer reads the
  `eventsheets/addons/disabled_packs` project setting, so every pack on disk is in the picker. **If
  your project had packs switched off, they come back.** To keep one out, delete its folder. The
  Project Doctor notes a project that still carries the old setting, once, as a quiet note saying
  it is ignored now and can be deleted. The \"Switch it back on\" quick fix went with the switch.
- **For scripts that called the catalog:** `EventSheetPackCatalog.disabled_packs()`,
  `is_enabled()`, `set_enabled()` and `is_disabled_path()` keep their shapes and are deprecated
  no-ops (empty, true, nothing, false). `publish_pack` and every `EventSheets` method are unchanged.
- **Follow-up:** the Tools menu no longer raises an index error in the Output each time it is built
  (the manager's old tooltip outlived its item), and the README and the guide index describe taking
  a pack's new version as replacing its folder instead of the removed update window.

### Fixed: what the review of the window fixes found

- **Replace in Project and Rename class:** both rewrote the behaviour packs under `eventsheet_addons/`
  as plain text when a word matched, string literals and comments included, although nobody had
  opened them and the next pack rebuild takes the change back. They now read past pack scripts; Find
  in Project still lists matches in them.
- **Sheet Type:** a new Name together with other changes made two undo steps, so one Ctrl+Z after OK
  took back the type settings and left the rename in place, and the line naming the other files the
  rename wrote was replaced by \"Sheet type updated.\" OK is now one undo step, the status keeps the
  file list, and the dialog says how far the rename reaches before OK, as Rename class does.
- **Rename class:** on a `.tres` sheet, renaming the class and then renaming it back was refused as
  \"already the name of another class\", because the compiled script still declared the old name.
- **Debugger, Breakpoints tab:** unticking a breakpoint, ticking it again and then pressing Ctrl+Z
  took it out of the list instead of leaving it there unticked. A breakpoint touched on one sheet no
  longer keeps a row listed on another.
- **ACE picker:** the reactive-trigger tip read \"Reactive alternative:On Timeout\" with no space.
- **States and Modes:** a refused OK in States, and the Modes notice about an On entering event that
  was kept, closed the dialog and spoke on the status bar behind it. Both now say it under the form
  with the dialog still open; in Modes the next OK on the same answers closes it.
- **Keyboard Shortcuts:** the Fixed keys list left out Ctrl+Y (Redo) and Backspace (delete), which the
  clash check already guarded.
- **History:** in split view, hovering an edit lit its rows in the left pane even when you were
  working in the right one. It now lights the pane you are working in.

### Fixed: the sheet and project windows

- **New Event Sheet:** when the name you typed was taken, the sheet was written as
  `event_sheet-2.gd`, a file name no class name can match, and nothing said so. The suffix now joins
  with an underscore (`event_sheet_2.gd`) and the Output panel says which file was written instead.
- **Sheet Type:** OK applied the sheet type even while the Ships as line showed a problem, so a broken
  class name or an unknown class went straight into the sheet. OK now stays disabled until the line
  reads clean.
- **Sheet Type:** changing Name on a class that already had one only relabelled the class, leaving
  every row that used the old name pointing at a class that no longer existed. It now renames the
  uses too, the same way Rename class on the sheet's head does.
- **Start page:** the Systems starter had a pitch but never showed, because the starter list it
  reads did not offer it. Entity System is now offered there and in Create New > Event Sheet.
- **Start page:** a showcase card opened whichever scene came last by name in its folder, so
  Platformer Shooter opened its target scene. It now opens the scene named after the showcase.
- **Tour:** the add-an-event, add-a-condition and add-an-action steps only looked at top-level
  events, so an event made inside a group never ticked. Events in groups and sub-events count now.
- **Import event sheet:** when Save as could not compile the sheet, the error was dropped and the
  window just stayed open. The reason is now shown on the window's report.
- **New Custom Resource:** the Resource name hint said `LootTable` while the wizard actually makes
  `LootDropTable`; its problems were written to the status bar hidden behind the window; and a class
  name already in use was accepted. The hint now shows the real name, problems show on the window,
  and a taken class name is refused.
- **New Behaviour Addon:** a name that clashed was only reported when you pressed Create. It is now
  reported while you type.
- **Addon manager:** Import from .zip promised a reading check it never ran, and overwrote files
  already in the project without keeping them. It now backs up every file it replaces into the backup
  ring first and reports how each imported pack reads. Check for updates and Import from URL no
  longer claim to check or download anything online; they say what they actually do.
- **Edit modes:** removing a mode left its On entering event behind, naming a mode the game no
  longer has. The event now goes in the same undo step; one you added your own rows to is kept and
  named on the status bar instead.
- **States:** OK rewrote the state list without looking at the rows, so removing or renaming a state
  that a Go to, Is in or On entering row still named left that row pointing at nothing. Such a change
  is now refused, and the status bar names the states the rows still use.

### Fixed: the finding and navigation windows

- **Find References:** double-clicking a result now selects the row the use sits on, opening its
  sheet first when it is not the one on screen. It used to open the sheet (and only when it was a
  saved file) and leave you at the top.
- **Outline:** in split view, picking an entry now lands in the pane you are working in, the same
  pane the outline was read from. To-do notes carry the editor's own info icon instead of an emoji.
- **Bookmarks:** Clear All now asks first, because bookmarks are not part of undo. OK and Esc keep
  them; only the separate Clear All button removes them. The list and its jumps follow the pane you
  are working in during split view, and entries carry an editor icon instead of an emoji.
- **History:** the list now opens with a \"Before these edits\" line, so a click can undo back past
  the first edit. Rows light up on the canvas while the pointer is over an entry, instead of when
  you click one.
- **Sheet map:** the summary counts scenes and globals as what they are instead of calling every box
  a sheet. Lines now join the edges of the two boxes that face each other, so a line to a box on the
  left no longer runs back through both boxes. Every line shows its label (\"includes\", \"calls Game\",
  \"signals On died\"), and clicking a scene box opens that scene in Godot's scene editor.
- **File Changed On Disk:** the reload question only warns that unsaved edits will be lost when the
  tab has unsaved edits.
- **Change Type Everywhere:** the OK button is off when the variable is not declared in the sheet,
  instead of doing nothing.
- **Unsaved Changes:** Save on a sheet that was never saved opens Save As, and the tab now closes
  once that file is written. Cancelling the Save As keeps the tab open.

### Fixed: debugging, checks and small prompts

- **Debugger, Breakpoints tab:** unticking a breakpoint deleted it, outside Undo, although the hint
  said it stays in place but inactive. Now it stays in the list unticked (condition kept), ticking
  turns it back on, and both are one Undo step.
- **Run Tests:** a test that asserted nothing was remembered on its sheet's health card as a clean
  run while the report said FAIL; it now counts as a failure. Choosing Run Tests again while a run
  is still going shows the window instead of starting a second run on top of the first.
- **Loose Ends:** after any edit, clicking an entry jumped nowhere, because the list still pointed
  at the sheet as it was before the edit. It now finds the row in the sheet as it is now.
- **Project Doctor:** \"Fix selected\" only worked for fixes an extension check attached. It now
  applies a finding's one built-in fix too, and when it cannot (no fix, or several to pick from)
  it is greyed with the reason in its tooltip.
- **Resolve Conflict:** the heading counted conflicted regions as \"to resolve\" instead of the
  events that differ, and Save overwrote the file with no backup. It now counts the differing
  events, and Save puts the file as it was into Sheet Backups first.
- **Keep the old name working:** Add Shim could be pressed with no old name typed. It is greyed
  until a name is entered.
- **Edit Script Block:** the confirm button read OK; it now says Save, as everything about it does.

### Fixed: project-wide find, replace and rename see `.gd` sheets too

- **Find in Project** searched only `.tres` sheets, so a project of `.gd` sheets (the default kind)
  found nothing. It now searches every sheet in both formats, and opening a result takes you to the
  event or line it was found on instead of the top of the sheet.
- **Replace in Project** now replaces in closed `.gd` sheets as well, backing each file up first and
  naming it on the status line.
- **Rename class** counted uses across the whole project but rewrote only the open sheet and the
  `.tres` sheets that include it, so a `.gd` that extends or creates the class kept the old name. It
  now rewrites every sheet that names the class, in both formats (open tabs are left unsaved for you
  to save), and the count it shows is the one it applies.
- **Rename class** now refuses a name another class, engine class or built-in type already has.
- **Ctrl+P `#`** and **What Changed…** now list `.gd` sheets alongside `.tres` sheets.
- **Rename Everywhere** (a variable's rename window) now has a Cancel button.
- **Rename a function:** after a rename that also saved sheets which include this one, the status
  line no longer says one Ctrl+Z takes all of it back - it says Ctrl+Z covers this sheet's rows and
  names the files it saved.

### Fixed: the studio and settings windows

- **Theme Editor:** each number token's box now uses that token's own range and step, so a lane
  ratio of 0.45 can be set instead of snapping to steps of 0.5.
- **Theme Editor:** a preset that cannot be saved now says so on the status bar.
- **Translation Studio:** pressing Report Coverage again replaces the report instead of adding a
  second copy under it.
- **Keyboard Shortcuts:** clicking a second binding while another one is listening now listens on
  the one you clicked, and a key that lands on one of the fixed keys (Ctrl+F, F9, Delete...) is
  flagged as a clash.
- **Words:** the table of words scrolls, so the window no longer grows past the bottom of the screen.
- **Generate Events from a Description:** it sends nothing while Ask is off, a second click while a
  request is out sends nothing, and a request that cannot start is reported and cleaned up.
- **Ask:** Discard drops the proposal and keeps the sentence you typed.
- **Add Save Support:** a typed array such as `Array[String]` now loads back from a save instead of
  failing the assignment.
- **Save Studio:** the Save Slots list shows save files only, not the editor's settings files.

### Fixed: the dialogs that write events keep your typing and say what is wrong

- A function parameter called `move speed` (a space, a keyword, or a name another parameter of the
  same function already has) is refused in the Edit Parameter dialog, which stays open with the
  reason under the form. It used to be stored as typed and break the generated `func` line.
- The New Function dialog says what is wrong with the name while you type it, and OK no longer
  closes the dialog and pops it back up on a refused name.
- The New Function preview names the picker shelf the function will really land on, instead of
  \"(this behaviour)\" on every sheet.
- Pressing OK on Edit Comment without changing anything no longer adds an undo step or marks the
  sheet as changed.
- Add behavior labels each setting with the property's own name, as the pack wrote it, and picking
  Platformer no longer highlights Platformer Pathfinding as well.
- Edit Enum and Edit Signal keep the dialog open with the error in it when a name is refused,
  instead of closing and reporting it in the status bar.
- Snippets are listed by the name they were saved under, not re-capitalised, and saving a selection
  under a name the library already has is refused with a message instead of quietly writing
  `name-2`.
- Paste Special keeps the form open when the paste is refused, so the retargets you typed survive.
- Compare keeps OK disabled while \"To\" (or the other end of a range, or the tolerance) is empty.
- The condition and action picker shows the editor's Favorites icon (or the word Pin) instead of
  emoji, and the node picker shows each node's class icon.
- An instance variable placed among the events keeps its \"Editable in the Inspector\" tick when you
  press OK in the variable dialog. It was dropped on every edit.
- A Show if or Lock unless condition now updates the Inspector the moment the variable it reads
  changes: that variable's generated setter calls `notify_property_list_changed()`. Before, the
  field only hid or showed after the node was selected again.

### Changed: the first open no longer compiles the Doctor, the pickers, the dialogs, the API or the Manual

- The last doors from the workspace's first open into the Project Doctor, the Add picker, the
  parameters and variable dialogs, the public API and the Manual are now by-path loads at the call
  that needs them, through `interaction/reading_doors.gd`. That covers the Doctor sections, the
  share index and the local tokens (the Doctor), the completions and quick add (the picker's fuzzy
  match and the dialog's scene lists), the variable tables and owners (the variable dialog's
  default text), the diagnostics, the game catalog, the Command Palette, the picker recipes and the
  Manual's own reference files (the API), and the member descriptions, the pattern marks and the
  after-save learning track (the Manual).
- The session's simple verbs (`EventSheets.register_simple_ace`) are read by the vocabulary from
  `dock_registry.gd`, which holds the API's own list, instead of through the API.
- Three things still BUILT a click-only dialog while the workspace was built in the real editor:
  the Properties bar's field factory made a parameters dialog up front, a registry refresh and the
  rail census read the picker field (which builds the picker), and handing the workspace the Live
  Values debugger built the parameters dialog to wire its expression picker. Each now waits for the
  dialog: the factory builds it on the first field, the picker is handed the registry and asked
  whether it is open only once it exists, and the debugger is kept and wired when the dialog is built.
- What the first open compiles, as `tools/measure_editor_closure.gd` reads it: 420 files and 205,738
  lines before, 338 files and 165,630 lines after (558 files and 249,073 lines before this cut began).
  Headless `editor_build_ms`, median of three, interleaved with the tree before the cut: 18,174 ms
  there, 12,170 ms here. `_ensure_editor()` in a real editor: 20,516 ms there, 14,600 ms here.
- New `editor_closure_budget_test` walks the same graph and fails when the first open's set grows
  more than three percent past the figure pinned in the test (338 files and 165,938 lines, measured
  again after a rebase), printing the files that hold the most of it in.
  `plugin_boot_lazy_test` now holds the workspace editor and the reading core to never naming the
  compiler, the importer, the lifter, the Doctor, the pickers, the dialogs, the API or the Manual,
  and the workspace build budget in `huge_project_budget_test` is lowered from 21 s to 18.3 s - a
  sanity ceiling with room for a busy runner, since the closure test is what holds regrowth.
- The picker's idle warm no longer compiles the picker and the API on its own a couple of seconds
  after the workspace opens: its row-icon slices wait until both are already loaded, and building
  the picker on the first click asks for them again. Escape and a stray click on the workspace no
  longer build the picker to ask whether it is open.
- The cold boot probe now opens a blank sheet in the workspace it built and runs the whole idle warm,
  then lists which of the picker, the parameters and variable dialogs, the Doctor, the API and the
  Manual's scripts that compiled; `huge_project_budget_test` pins every one of them uncompiled. This
  sees a helper the workspace reaches by path at runtime, which the static closure walk cannot.
- The workspace tab's class icon and the toolbar's find magnifier use the same editor-icon lookup as
  before the cut again (no mirrored stand-in when the theme lacks an icon), and the \"welcomed\" flag
  the Welcome window writes and the workspace reads is spelled in one place.
- Every row reading is unchanged: `tools/reading_dump.gd` gives byte-identical output on this tree
  and on the tree before the cut began.

### Fixed: two prompts no longer lose work on Esc or Enter

- **Every Tick:** taking the last condition off an event asks whether to keep it as an every-tick
  event or delete it. Delete was the Cancel button, so Esc or the close box deleted the event. Now
  Delete The Event is its own button and every other way out of the question keeps the event.
- **Unsaved Changes:** Discard was the OK button, so Enter closed the tab and threw the edits away.
  Save is now the OK button, Discard is a separate button you click on purpose, and Cancel and Esc
  still just abort.

### Added: the first open of the workspace is measured, and what it compiles is listed

- The cold boot probe now also times building the workspace editor in a fresh process of its own:
  `editor_load_ms` (loading `event_sheet_editor.gd`, which compiles every class it names) and
  `editor_build_ms` (that plus `.new()`). Measured at about 15.6 s and 16.8 s headless on this
  project, and the huge-project budget test holds it under 21 s while that number is cut down.
- `tools/measure_editor_closure.gd` reads the sources without compiling them and prints what that
  first open compiles (558 files, about 249,000 lines today), the fifteen names whose removal would
  drop the most of it, and the largest files in it. The output is the same on every run.

### Changed: the sheet's reading code no longer names the dialogs, the compiler or the API it borrows from

- The row builder, the sentence grammar, the reading rows and the row marks asked the picker dialog
  for theme icons. Those icon lookups (the category icon table, class icons, the role dots, the
  `res://` icon cache) now live in `ace_icons.gd` (`EventSheetACEIcons`), which names only the
  vocabulary it reads. The picker's old static calls still work and forward there.
- The three compiler spellings the readers needed (the shared-helper prefix, the Distance Travelled
  member, how a function's return type is written) live in `compiler_spellings.gd`. The compiler reads
  them from there too, so there is still one copy of each.
- The compiler and the lifter, and on the canvas the parameter and variable dialogs, the Manual's
  reference and the public API, are loaded by path where they are actually called, through
  `interaction/reading_doors.gd`. Before, naming them compiled all of them as soon as the reading
  code loaded.
- The Doctor sections register and list scripts through the Doctor itself instead of the public API.
  The multiplayer and spawn facts read the scene through `EventSheetSceneReplication`, which the API's
  `synced_properties` and `spawners_of` now forward to.
- Every row reading is unchanged: `tools/reading_dump.gd` gives byte-identical output before and after.
  `reading_core_lazy_test` reads the compile-time graph, fails if any of these names comes back, and
  checks that each leaf and each by-path door gives the same answers the classes did.
- This half of the cut does not shrink the first open on its own yet. The dock still names every one
  of these classes, and other doors stay open (for example `variable_owners.gd` to both dialogs,
  `scene_sheet.gd` to the importer, `ace_registry.gd` to the API), so each class is still compiled
  through some other path.

### Changed: the first open compiles the workspace, not every window a click could open

- The workspace's dialogs, windows, studios and click-only helpers (the picker, the parameters and
  variable dialogs, the Manual, the Doctor panel, the Welcome window and tour, the starters, the
  wizards, Save Studio, Translation Studio, Find all references and the row and ACE edit operations,
  67 delegates in all) are built the first time something asks for them instead of when the workspace
  is built. Each is a loosely typed field whose getter loads its file by path, builds it, wires it
  and connects what the old start-up wiring connected, so every dialog opens, every signal fires and
  undo works exactly as before.
- The dock and the helpers that draw the first paint reach the compiler, the importer, the Doctor,
  the public API and the pickers by path at the moment they are needed, instead of naming them. A
  cache those files keep is only dropped when the file has been loaded this session.
- The workspace announces itself, registers its own palette commands and row items, and fires the
  sheet lifecycle listeners through `addons/eventsheet/api/dock_registry.gd`, a file that names no
  class. `EventSheets` keeps the same methods and reads the same lists, and no longer names the dock,
  which breaks the cycle that tied every file naming the API to the whole workspace.
- A freed workspace takes its own palette commands and row items off those lists. Left behind, they
  were closures over a freed node, and one still listed when the engine shut down took the process
  down on the way out.
- What the first open compiles, as `tools/measure_editor_closure.gd` reads it: 558 files and 249,073
  lines before, 417 files and 205,473 lines after. The cold boot probe's headless `editor_load_ms`
  went from about 18.3 s to about 13.4 s, and `editor_build_ms` from about 19.6 s to about 15.5 s
  (median of three each, same machine, back to back). Those figures are this change alone, measured
  on the tree before the reading change above landed; with both in, the first open measures 420
  files and 205,738 lines. `workspace_first_open_lazy_test` pins the
  click-only files out of that set, the heavy names out of the first-paint files and the shared lists
  between the workspace and the API.

### Changed: the first sheet tab of a session no longer re-reads every pack

- The verbs every pack script publishes are remembered in `.godot/eventsheets_pack_vocabulary.bin`
  between editor sessions, so the first sheet tab answers the picker's pack vocabulary without
  compiling or reflecting the packs again. On this project's 181 pack scripts the vocabulary step of
  the first open went from about 6.7 s to about 3.1 s (median of three, same machine, interleaved).
- A remembered entry is used only while the script's bytes (content hash included), every script it
  builds on or names, its autoload, the plugin and engine versions and the reflecting code are what
  they were; anything else and that one script is reflected again. A script whose surface is computed
  by code (`_get_property_list`, `_eventforge_register`) is always reflected fresh. Deleting the file
  is always safe. The first session after an update pays about one second extra to write it.
- `pack_vocabulary_cache_test` compares the remembered answer with a fresh reflection for every pack,
  dump line for dump line and field for field.
- A pack written before the helper class it names (so it did not compile and published nothing) is
  reflected again the moment that class exists: every capitalised name a pack spells that was not a
  class when its entry was written is part of the entry, and the entry stops holding once that name
  resolves. Before, the empty answer was served until the pack itself was touched.
- Later refreshes in the same session hash a remembered pack again only while its file's modified
  second is not yet over, instead of hashing every pack on every refresh; a same-length edit is still
  caught either way.
- `tools/build_help_bundle.gd -- --force` bakes past the figure-collapse refusal (printing the refusal
  it overrode), for a guide edit that really removes most figures; a broken toolchain still refuses.
- The categories left without a picker section icon are one list, `ACEPickerDialog.UNMAPPED_ICON_CATEGORIES`,
  read by both `tools/check_section_icons.gd` and `pack_icons_test`.

### Fixed: the help bundle never bakes a manual with no figures in it

- `EventSheets.round_trip_failure(source)` is the byte gate's reason: `\"\"` when `round_trips(source)`
  is true, otherwise `import failed`, `compile failed: <errors>` or `output differs`. `round_trips`
  keeps its shape and now answers through it. A guide figure that cannot be drawn because the
  importer or compiler faulted says so, instead of reading as a body whose re-emission drifted.
- `tools/build_help_bundle.gd` asks a known-good body first and stops with exit 2 when it does not
  round-trip, and refuses to write anything (exit 2, naming the first underlying error) when the fresh
  figure verdicts have none drawable or fewer than half the drawable count already committed. A bake
  once rewrote every figure as \"would not reproduce\" while the live check disagreed.

### Fixed: four picker sections that asked for an icon the editor does not have

- The Input, Input Event, JSON and Material sections of the picker had headers with no icon,
  because the icon each one asked for (InputEvent, JSON, Material) does not exist in the editor
  theme. They now show the keyboard, text-file and standard-material icons.
- `tools/check_section_icons.gd`, run in a real editor, asks the editor theme for every builtin
  section's icon and names any that is missing (`section icons: N checked, M missing`). The test
  suite now records the icon name each section was checked against, so renaming one is caught.

### Fixed: a sheet with modes and an On created row opens as rows again

- A sheet whose mode rows (or any signal row) came before its On created row compiles its handler
  above `_ready`, and opening that script by path used to fall back WHOLE to code blocks: the
  handler stayed a block, the On created row dropped the line that connects it anyway, and the file
  no longer reproduced. The On created row now keeps a connect line whose handler sits above it, as
  one of its own statements, so the file opens as its rows and still saves back byte for byte.

### Changed: Editor Native reads an enum value as written and ends the sheet on its last row

- An enum variable's value reads as its choice spelled the way the file spells it: `GateState.CLOSED`
  on a `GateState` variable reads `CLOSED`, matching the enum's own row, never \"Closed\". A derived
  property row's hover leads a second property's words with that property's identifier as written
  too. No identifier is recapitalised anywhere.
- Under a theme that folds the workspace bands away, a sheet, group or verb that already holds events
  no longer ends on a \"+ Add event…\" row - its events offer Add on hover. An empty one keeps the row,
  its only way in. A status bar that says what is selected keeps its middle quiet on a load instead
  of repeating the sheet's name the tab already shows.
- **Event Is Any Press** and **Anything Is Pressed** each point at the other in their description
  (the press edge inside an input event, the held state anywhere), and a search for \"Any Key\" offers
  both.

### Fixed: a selected event inside a group keeps one filled margin in Editor Native

- Selecting an event that sits in a group used to split its filled margin in two: the number blue,
  then the group's indent and its rails dark, then the trigger icon blue again. Under a theme that
  marks a selection by its gutter and frame (Editor Native, dark and light) the fill now runs unbroken
  from the number across the indent to the trigger icon at any depth, with no rail drawn between
  the number and the rest. Classic Sheet and every theme that washes its selection paints exactly as
  before.

### Fixed: a typed variable with no starting value opens as a variable, not a script block

- `@export var spark_prefab: PackedScene`, `var target: Node2D`, `var hp: int` - the usual way to
  write a slot the Inspector or `_ready` fills - used to open as a raw script block in the variable
  list, because the only spelling a variable row could save back carried ` = ...`. Each now opens as
  the variable row it is, in every theme, with its type, its `@export` hint, its group and its doc
  comment, and saves back byte for byte: the row remembers the file gave no value and writes none,
  for as long as its starting value stays empty. Give it a value and that value is written.
- Gated like every other lift: a line that does not come back exactly keeps its block. Two spellings
  stay code on purpose - a trailing `# comment` with no `=` before it (the comment would be read as
  part of the type) and an `@onready` with nothing to wait for (every reader of an @onready row reads
  the node it names).

### Added: the Flock pack and Flock 3D - birds, fish and swarms that steer by their neighbours

- **Flock** (`FlockBehavior`, on a Node2D) and **Flock 3D** (`Flock3DBehavior`, on a Node3D): attach
  one under every member and the group flies as one, with separation from the members too close,
  alignment with the way the neighbours fly and cohesion toward their middle, inside a neighbour
  radius. An **avoid group** is fled on top - a predator, the player, a hazard. Weights, speeds, the
  steering force and both radii are Inspector values, and each has a Set row: Set Flock Weights, Set
  Flock Speeds, Set Max Force, Set Flock Radii, Set Avoid Group, Set Flock Group (leaves the old
  group and joins the new in one row) and Set Flocking Enabled.
- Compiles to plain typed GDScript: the host joins the flock group, a neighbour carrying the same
  behaviour reports its flock velocity (a CharacterBody or RigidBody in the group reports its own),
  and a CharacterBody host flies with `move_and_slide`. The 3D host looks along its flight.
- Found under the names the technique goes by elsewhere: Boids, Flocking, and Separation Alignment
  Cohesion. Each pack has a guide; for hundreds of members the guide points to one script driving a
  MultiMeshInstance.

### Added: pause requests, so one reason unpausing does not unpause the rest

- **Ask To Pause**, **Stop Asking To Pause** and **Pause Requested By**, beside Pause The Game, Set
  Game Paused and Is Game Paused. Pausing used to be one switch, so closing the inventory unpaused a
  game the dialogue box still had paused. These keep a set of who is asking - `_pause_requests[&\"inventory\"] = true`
  then `get_tree().paused = true`, and `_pause_requests.erase(&\"inventory\")` then
  `get_tree().paused = not _pause_requests.is_empty()` - and the game runs again only when nobody is
  left asking. Read \"Ask the game to pause for \"inventory\"\" and \"A pause is requested by \"dialogue\"\",
  a question about the request rather than about a node frozen by the pause.
- **One set for the whole game, no autoload.** The compiler declares
  `static var _pause_requests: Dictionary` beside the class only when a row reads it, and its starting
  value is the one Dictionary kept on the Engine singleton, so a pause menu sheet and an inventory
  sheet count into the same set.
- **The two lines open again as the request.** Each pair is its own statement run, so the importer
  reads it back as Ask To Pause or Stop Asking To Pause - never as a Set Paused that would lose who
  asked - and the file saves back byte for byte. A lone `get_tree().paused = true` keeps the row it
  always had.
- **The game's modes go through the requests.** Edit modes now writes Ask To Pause for \"mode\" on
  entering a mode that stops the game and Stop Asking To Pause for \"mode\" on entering one that runs
  it, instead of Set Game Paused, so an inventory that asked to pause stays paused when the game
  passes through a running mode. A sheet holding the old Set Game Paused policy reads back the same
  and is rewritten as the request the next time the dialog writes it.
- Each answers to the names a counted pause goes by elsewhere (Time.timeScale, Pause Counter,
  Request Pause, Release Pause) and is keyed in all nine translation files.

### Changed: Editor Native says an event's pattern in the help strip, not the conditions lane

- **The conditions lane holds conditions only.** A theme can take the ⟡ chip that names the pattern
  an event was read as (\"Making an object and putting it somewhere\") out of the conditions lane
  (`pattern_in_help_strip`, on in Editor Native). Selecting the event says it in the help strip
  instead, muted like the newer-spelling hint, with an Open in the Manual door - the page the chip's
  click opened. A finding on the same event still outranks it, and then the strip's hover carries
  the pattern under the finding's sentence. Every other theme states the token off and keeps the chip
  exactly where it was. Drawing only: the default-theme reading is byte-identical.

### Changed: Editor Native's two lanes read blue and green on any editor

- **The conditions lane is blue and the actions lane green, whatever grey the editor is.** The two
  lanes used to move with the editor's greys like the cards do, which kept the reference editor's few
  levels of tint and read as plain grey on a neutral editor. Each lane is now tinted from the card it
  sits on: the card's brightness a step lighter in a dark editor (a step darker in a light one), at a
  blue or green hue with a fixed amount of colour, so a neutral, a tinted and a light editor all get
  the same visible blue and green. The sentence text keeps at least 4.5:1 contrast on both, and a
  hovered cell is its own lane lifted a step, so a hovered action stays green. Only Editor Native's
  colours move; every other theme draws exactly as before.

### Changed: Editor Native stops showing the compiler's \"__\" names inside events

- **A spawned copy is named by its scene.** A theme can stop showing the compiler's two-underscore
  bookkeeping inside events (`fold_compiler_helpers`, on in Editor Native): a `__` local holding a
  fresh copy of a scene (`var __spawn_shot = load(\"res://shot.tscn\").instantiate()`) reads by the
  scene file in the object column (\"shot.tscn | Set position to ...\"), in its sub-events too, the
  line that puts it in the tree reads \"System | Spawn shot.tscn\", and the local's own \"Local value\"
  row folds into that one. A row that only keeps a hidden `__` member up to date - the clock an Every
  X Seconds row adds the frame time to and then wraps - folds away like its variable row did, unless
  it is all the event does. Every other theme states the token off and reads exactly as before.
  Reading only: compiled output, the byte round trip and the default-theme reading are unchanged.
- **A For Each's own `__` name reads as what it walks.** Under the same token, a loop the compiler
  wrote over a group (`for __shot in get_tree().get_nodes_in_group(\"shots\")`) reads \"For each shots\"
  with \"shots\" in its object column, and every row under it says \"shots\" where the file says
  `__shot` (\"shots still exists\", \"shots | Destroy\"). Several groups walked as one list read joined
  (\"shots/targets\"); a loop over a plain list reads by that list's name. A theme derived from the
  editor (Editor Native's own rule) reads the same as the starter. A loop name the author wrote
  (no `__`) always reads exactly as written, and Classic Sheet keeps the `__` names.

### Added: new rows for the tree, for starting over, and for distance

- **Tell Children**, beside Call Method On Group (with value): calls a method on a node and on every
  node beneath it in one line, `$Door.propagate_call(\"open\")`, with the values as the list
  propagate_call takes. Reads \"Tell Door and everything under it to open\"; the line typed by hand
  opens as the row and saves back byte for byte. The way back up stays a signal.
- **First Parent Of Type**, the upward mirror of First Child Of Type: the closest node above one
  that is of a type (an engine class, or a class_name up a script's chain), null when there is none.
  The short walk up the tree is written into the file once, the first time a row asks for it.
  Reads \"the first Room above Chest\".
- **Next Sibling** and **Previous Sibling**: the node either side of one under the same parent,
  wrapping past the ends, as plain index arithmetic. Read \"the sibling after Slot\".
- **Reset Variable** and **Reset Variables**, beside Set value: put one variable, or every variable
  the sheet declares, back to the value its declaration starts it on. The compiler writes the
  declared value, so the starting value is typed once; exported variables are left out of \"every\",
  and a variable the sheet does not declare writes nothing and says so in the compile warnings. The
  line is a plain assignment, so it reopens as Set value (like Set boolean, the Reset rows stay out of
  reverse lifting), and the parameter dialog says when a Set value line is the one Reset wrote.
- **Distance Travelled**: how far a CharacterBody or RigidBody has really moved, a `_travelled` total
  added up in `_physics_process` from `get_real_velocity()` / `linear_velocity`, kept beside the class
  only when a row reads it. Reads \"how far Player has travelled\"; a host with no velocity is told so.
- Each answers to the names its job goes by in other editors (BroadcastMessage, GetComponentInParent,
  Pick parent, Reset global variable, Odometer, ...) and is keyed in all nine translation files.

### Added: siblings beside the rows they extend

- **Event Is Any Press**, beside Key Pressed (event): true once per real press of any key, mouse
  button or gamepad button inside an input event - `(event.is_pressed() and not event.is_echo())`,
  so a held key's repeats and a moving mouse never count. Press-anything screens, cutscene skips.
- **For Each Cell In Rectangle**, the looping twin of Cells In Rectangle the way For Each Cell In
  Radius is the twin of Cells In Radius: the same block walk, each cell arriving as `cell`. The
  loop reads as its row (\"For each cell in the Vector2i(6, 4) block at Vector2i(2, 2)\"), not as
  the walk it compiles to.
- **Pick First Where** and **Pick Last Where**, beside Pick Where: the first or last instance the test
  holds for, in the list's own order. They take the end of the filtered list with `pop_front()` /
  `pop_back()`, so a list where nothing passes picks null rather than raising an error.
- **Remove Where**, the action sibling of Filter with the same element and test fields: takes every
  match out of the array in place with `assign()`, so a typed array stays typed.
- **Has Been True For**, FPS Below For's \"has it lasted?\" helper for any test: true once the test has
  held without a break for the seconds you name - standing still for two seconds, a button held for
  one. Filed in Run Context beside Was Recently True, its mirror.
- **Rescale (kept inside)** and **Rescale Into Property**, beside Rescale: the answer clamped to the
  range it was rescaled into (lowest end first, so a range that runs downward is kept inside too),
  and the same rescale written straight onto a node's property - the exhaust's amount from the speed.
  Rescale itself writes exactly the line it always wrote.
- **Ceil (whole)**, beside Ceil: `ceili()`, the whole number a page count or a row count is.
- **Debug Overlay: Draw Rect and Draw Ray (3D)**, with the same expiry as Draw Ray: a world-space
  rectangle outlined for N seconds, and a ray in a 3D world projected through the active camera.
- **Move children, as a Ghost Row recipe.** Typing \"move children of $Crate to $Level\" (or
  `SetParent`) offers For Each Child with Add Child (existing node) inside it, filled in - the two
  shipped rows, which compile to `for child in $Crate.get_children(): child.reparent($Level)`. With an
  event selected the rows go under it. No new row: the two that ship already say it.
- Each sibling answers to the name its job goes by elsewhere (`Input.anyKeyDown`, \"On any key
  pressed\", \"For each XY element\", `FindLast`, `RemoveAll`, `InverseLerp`, `CeilToInt`, \"Draw Debug
  Rect\"), and an expression field reads `Mathf.CeilToInt`, `FloorToInt` and `RoundToInt` as `ceili`,
  `floori` and `roundi`. The rows that lift (every condition and action here but Has Been True For,
  whose helper is written beside the class as FPS Below For's is) read back as themselves and
  re-emit byte for byte.

### Added: the names a reader brings from another engine find what already ships

- **Search answers to API calls and action titles.** The picker and Quick add already answered to the
  names another event-sheet editor uses; they now also answer to the call a C# developer types and
  the action title a visual-scripting add-on lists, for the rows that already do that job here.
  `WaitForSeconds` finds Wait, \"Random Wait\" finds Wait and Random (the random wait is Wait with
  Random in its seconds slot), `Random.Range` finds Random and Random Integer, \"Get Random Child\" and
  \"Pick random instance\" find Pick A Random One, `string.Format`, \"Format String\" and \"Build String\"
  find Text From Pattern, `magnitude` and \"Get Speed\" find Vector Length (and Get Velocity),
  `Debug.Log` finds Print Log, \"Show a value on screen\" finds the Debug Overlay's Watch Value,
  `Debug.DrawRay` and \"Draw Debug Ray\" find Draw Ray, and `GetRange` finds Slice. The found row says the name back as it was typed, in
  quotes after its own name, and a test pins that every name reaches a row that ships.
- **An expression field reads the C# spelling.** The translator that already turned `&` and
  `random(10)` into GDScript now reads `Mathf.Lerp`, `Mathf.InverseLerp`, `Mathf.Clamp`,
  `Mathf.Abs`, `Mathf.Min`, `Mathf.Max`, `Mathf.Sqrt`, `Vector2.Distance` / `Vector3.Distance`,
  `Time.deltaTime`, the `2f` float suffix and an interpolated `$\"HP: {hp}\"` (written as
  `\"HP: %s\" % [hp]`). `Random.Range` becomes `randi_range(a, b - 1)` with two whole numbers and
  `randf_range(a, b)` with a decimal (its arguments may hold calls); with two names, where only their
  types would say which, the field refuses it rather than guess, and so it does for a formatted slot
  like `{hp:0.0}` and for a `Mathf` call it has no twin for. The help strip says the GDScript under a
  quiet note and OK writes it, exactly as for the other editor's spelling. Compiled output is
  unchanged.
- **Text From Pattern fills its values from the pattern.** Typing `\"{player} scored {score}!\"` into
  the pattern writes `{\"player\": player, \"score\": 100}` into the values field: a value already
  given to a name is kept, a new slot names the variable of the same name, and a key the pattern no
  longer asks for is dropped. A values field holding a variable or a call is left alone. Read off the
  row's template, so any row that writes `{pattern}.format({values})` gets the same help.

### Changed: the Editor Native look

- **The Editor Native theme starter, and the tokens the native look is drawn with.** One more theme
  starter beside Classic Sheet in View > Sheet theme: tinted condition and action lanes, cards with a
  one-pixel edge, 24 px rows, a 92 px object column, a note-card comment and a filled gutter on the
  selected event. Its greys follow the running editor - dark in a dark editor, light in a light one -
  and its colours swap to their light partners on a light editor, while any token you change stays
  exactly as you set it (`follow_editor_greys` on the theme resource; every other theme leaves it off
  and is painted as saved). Thirty-seven new theme tokens: nine type colours, four word colours, the
  Include line (border, fill, tag, name, note), the inner-class block (border, fill, head, tag, name,
  note), the comment border, the gutter icon, the selected-gutter fill, the object hairline, the link
  colour and four badge pairs (Inspector, range, tooltip, saved). The ones that re-colour something
  the sheet already draws - the comment edge, the selected gutter, the Include line and the number,
  text and keyword colours inside a sentence - are clear by default, and clear means \"as before\", so
  a theme you saved yourself keeps its look; every bundled theme now states them in its own colours
  (see the last entry). Drawing only: compiled output is unchanged.
- **Text labels: the toolbar as words, and an accent edge on the open tab.** View > Text labels lays
  the resting strip out as words - Sheet (the same Menu), Save, the Undo and Redo arrows, the play
  button, Add Event as the one filled button, Add Condition and Add Action, the Quick add field at
  the right edge as the strip's Find, and a new Sentences menu holding the variable-row dial
  (sentence, both, code - the dial View > Variable rows turns) and Words. Nothing joins or leaves the
  strip, so every key, id and handler is unchanged. Until you choose, the sheet's theme decides: the
  Editor Native starter turns it on (`toolbar_text_labels` on the theme resource) and every other
  theme keeps the icon-led strip; your choice, once made, is remembered per project and wins. One
  new bar token, the open tab's edge (`active_tab_edge_color`), draws a two-pixel accent line along
  the top of the open sheet's tab; it is clear in every theme but Editor Native, so their tabs look
  exactly as the editor draws them.
- **Trigger icons, the object hairline, word colours and the divider grip.** Each event now shows how
  it is started as a small icon right beside its number - a play mark for On ready, a turning arrow
  for Every tick, a falling ball for Every physics tick, a keyboard for input, a signal mark for a
  signal and a notification mark for an engine notification - in the slot beside the gutter that
  every event row already reserved, so no row, column or reading moves; hovering one says what it
  means, and View > Trigger
  Icons turns them off (remembered per project, and in saved views). System rows wear a drawn gear
  and the Keyboard, Mouse, Gamepad and Touch rows a drawn device glyph in place of their category
  icon, in the object colour. The line between the object column and the sentence is drawn in the
  theme's object hairline colour and stands short of the row's edges, falling back to the old faint
  label ink where that colour would vanish against its lane. Where a theme states the variable word
  colour (Editor Native does), the sheet's own variable names inside a condition or action sentence
  wear it - in every spelling the sheet shows them in, and only inside the row's parameters - and the
  number, text and keyword colours now also reach the values of a row read from hand-written code.
  The hovered event's lane divider shows a three-dot grip where the divider is wide enough to hold
  it. Drawing only: compiled output and every reading are unchanged.
- **Variable rows draw their values the way the Inspector does, and wear badges.** The type word is
  drawn in a monospaced face in its type's colour (number, text, true/false, vector, colour, object,
  enum, collection or class, from the theme's type colours, kept readable on the row). A true/false
  value gets a tick box in front of it - filled with a tick for true, an empty frame for false - a
  vector shows X and Y (and Z) fields with axis-coloured letters, the way the Inspector draws them,
  and a scene, resource or node path sits in a framed chip with a resource mark; a colour keeps its
  swatch. The variable's `##` comment, already the row's last note, leans into italics. At the row's
  right, small icon tiles say how the Inspector treats the variable: an eye where it is shown in the
  Inspector (the old sliders mark beside the name, restyled and moved there), a slider where
  `@export_range` keeps it between limits, a speech bubble where a `##` comment gives it a tooltip,
  and a disk where the sheet's own file saves it with the Save System or remembers it - read off the
  same save keys the Doctor's save check reads. Hovering a tile says what it means. View > Value
  Glyphs and View > Variable Badges turn each half off (remembered per project, and in saved views);
  with both off a variable row looks exactly as before. Every mark is painted on the canvas, no word
  of any row changes, and compiled output is unchanged.
- **One class head, the Include bar, the included variables and the class block.** The head opens on
  ONE class head row: the class icon and name, the word Script, and `· extends` with the base class
  and its change… control - the extends band's own words and gesture, drawn on the head row instead
  of on a row of their own (every other head band is still one row per line of the file). A shared
  sheet (`## @ace_shared_sheet`) wears a drawn share mark on a small tile and says which scripts of
  the project include it (\"Included by Portcullis, Drawbridge\"), read from the project's scripts -
  each file once per version, so a later look only checks dates, and dropped on the editor's
  filesystem ping - and, on a dock too narrow for both, cut short with an ellipsis rather than run
  under the head's code echo; the scene the script is attached to, when exactly one scene runs it, sits
  at the head's right edge (\"Scene gate.tscn\") on an editable sheet (a read-only preview already says
  it on the bar it opens on). Each Include line reads as a bar - the Include word and the name in the
  theme's Include colours with no fill behind any word, what kind of include it is, and the line it
  writes (`var _enemy_common`, `extends EnemyCommon`) at the right edge in the script editor's
  colours; a theme that states the Include edge (Editor Native does) draws it as a rounded bar. Under
  each Include bar, the included sheet's own variables are listed read-only in a dashed outline,
  \"int health = 100  from EnemyCommon\"; a click opens the shared sheet, and View > Included
  Variables turns them off (remembered per project, and in saved views). An inner data class draws as
  one framed block in the class colours, and while it is open it ends in \"+ Add field\" (the Add
  Field dialog its right-click menu has always had) and a short note on when to use a class, with
  the variables of this sheet that hold it (\"Used by settings (a variable), waves (a list)\"), read
  off the open sheet so an edit shows before it is saved; a read-only preview shows neither. The word
  Script's hover says a node runs the script only when the script extends a node. Compiled output is unchanged; the reading of every opened sheet
  changes only in that the class head row carries the extends words and no extends row follows it.
- **Add links on hover, the selected gutter, the two-line group head and the status bar counts.**
  \"Add condition\" and \"Add action\" now appear as link words, in the theme's link colour, under the
  lanes of the event under the pointer, of the selected event (a keyboard selection has no pointer)
  and under a lane that holds nothing yet - every other event keeps them hidden; a click opens the
  picker on that lane of that event, and the link underlines while the pointer is on it. View > Add
  Links On Hover turns it off and brings back the old pills in those same places (remembered per
  project, and in saved views). Either way they are the same two add lines in the same places, so no
  row moves. A selected event's filled gutter (the selected-gutter token, which
  Editor Native states) now reaches across the trigger icon's slot, with the number and the icon in
  white. A theme can lay group heads out in two lines (`group_head_two_line`, on in Editor Native):
  the name over its description in italics, the event count, and the on/off switch drawn as a tick
  box and the word Active; every other theme keeps the one-line head. The status bar now says how
  many rows of the sheet are drawn amber (\"No problems\", \"2 problems\") and how many events the sheet
  holds, beside the address and the zoom - a count only, the findings themselves still live in the
  amber row, the help strip and the Doctor. Drawing only: compiled output and every reading are
  unchanged.
- **Every bundled theme wears the new look in its own colours.** Classic Sheet, Mockup Slate,
  Catppuccin Mocha, Dracula, Gruvbox Dark, Monokai, Nord, Solarized Light, Soft Light, High Contrast
  and the Designer Template now state every native-look token from their own palette instead of
  leaving them clear or on the generic dark design: numbers in the palette's green, text in its
  orange, true/false in its blue, vectors and the Include line in its teal or aqua, colours in its
  pink, enums and the comment edge in its yellow, lists and the inner-class block in its purple,
  links and the open tab's edge in its accent. Each word and glyph colour reads at 4.5:1 or better on
  the surface it is drawn on (both lanes, a variable row, the Include bar, the class block, its badge
  tile; 7:1 in High Contrast), and Solarized Light's are light-theme values. The rows at the head of a
  sheet (its class line, variables, Include lines and the rows under them) are now filled with the
  theme's own row colours and written in its own inks rather than the plugin's fixed dark pair, so a
  light theme is light there too. The six palette presets are rebuilt by `tools/build_theme_presets.gd`,
  which now carries these values. Drawing only: compiled output and every reading are unchanged.
- **Editor Native variable rows read the way the Inspector lists a property.** A theme can draw its
  variable rows Inspector-style (`inspector_variable_rows`, on in Editor Native): the GDScript type
  in mono in its family's colour (`float`, `int`, `String`, `Vector2`, `Color`, `Array[Node2D]`, an
  enum's or a class's own name - never \"number\" or \"list of objects\"), the name exactly as the code
  writes it (`open_speed` reads \"open_speed\", never a capitalised \"Open Speed\", in this theme as in
  every other), the value (a colour as its swatch then its `#RRGGBB` hex, the swatch still opening
  the picker), then the `##` description as the row's one italic note - an exported variable's lifted
  tooltip, or the `##` line kept above a plain `var`, which the view then stops listing twice - and
  the attributes only as the icon tiles at the right. The outlined kind mark, the scope word and the
  declaration echo are gone from the row; the scope and the quieter notes (limits, what a static
  shares, a frozen constant) move to the name's hover, and View > Variable rows > Code still shows
  the line. A second token (`hide_compiler_variables`, on in Editor Native) leaves the compiler's own
  bookkeeping members - names starting with two underscores, such as the clock an Every X Seconds row
  keeps - out of the variable rows; they still compile and save. Locals inside an event keep their
  sentence row, and every other theme states both tokens off and draws exactly as before. Drawing
  only: compiled output and the default-theme reading are unchanged.
- **The Editor Native head is one class head card.** A theme can head the sheet the way the
  Inspector heads a script (`inspector_class_head`, on in Editor Native): one card in the head's grey
  with a one-pixel edge - the class icon, the name in bold, the word Script, \"· extends\" and the base
  in the faint colour, the share mark and an italic \"Included by ...\" on a shared sheet, and the
  attached scene at the right. The \"change…\" word and the `class_name` echo are gone from it: a click
  on Script, \"· extends\" or the base opens the Sheet Type dialog, as their hover says. No head line
  echoes its code, and the rows for the scene's facts (\"spawns shot.tscn\", lit by, collisions and the
  rest) leave the canvas for the scene's hover - a click on the scene shows it in the FileSystem dock -
  while a fact that is a problem keeps its row. The \"+ add: icon · @tool · description · states\" row
  moves to the canvas's \"+ Add…\" menu as Add to Class Head…. The Include bars keep the line they
  write, as the design draws them. Every other theme states the token off and draws exactly as before.
  Drawing only: compiled output and the default-theme reading are unchanged.
- **Editor Native events are drawn at the design's size.** A one-condition, one-action event is one
  24 px line, not two and a half. The cause was three things at once: every event reserved a line for
  \"Add condition\" and \"Add action\" even at rest, the text followed the editor's 14-16 px font, and
  the cells kept a 3 px pad. Five theme tokens, off by default, on in Editor Native and stated off in
  every other shipped theme: `add_lines_on_hover` gives an event its add lines only while it is
  hovered or selected (an empty lane keeps its line, because there it is the only invitation);
  `row_font_size` draws the sheet's text at 12 px, never larger than the editor's own font;
  `trigger_icon_in_gutter` puts the trigger icon beside the number on the event's first line, makes
  the number and the icon one margin column inside the card, and leaves the coloured trigger badge out
  of the conditions lane, which now starts with its object column; `behaviour_in_object_column` reads
  a behaviour's row as \"Platformer | Jump\" instead of the script's object cut short in the column with
  \"Platformer\" repeated at the head of the sentence; `lane_chips_yield` keeps the chip after a
  condition (the pattern a spawning event is) to two fifths of the lane, cut short with an ellipsis,
  so \"Every tick (physics)\" no longer breaks across three lines. Editor Native also states a 1 px cell
  pad, no badge column and a 114 px object column (the design's 92 px name after its icon), and its
  action lane is the card moved toward green rather than olive, so the two lanes read blue and green
  beside each other on a grey editor. Drawing only: compiled output and the default-theme reading are
  unchanged, and Classic Sheet renders pixel for pixel as before.
- **The Editor Native chrome: tabs, strip, status bar, group heads, notes and selection.** Four theme
  switches, off by default, on in Editor Native and stated off in every other shipped theme, plus one
  bar colour. `tabs_above_toolbar` stacks the open-sheet tabs over the strip, as the design does. The
  word-led strip rests without the play button (it stays on the full strip, and Godot's own play bar
  keeps F5 and F6), and its field reads \"Find\" behind the editor's magnifier at 180 px.
  `status_bar_selection_line` says what is selected the way the sheet reads it - \"Event 3 selected ·
  Every tick (physics), \"ui_accept\" is down\" - at the status bar's left edge, with No problems in a new
  ok colour (`status_ok_color`, the design's green in Editor Native, clear everywhere else), the event
  count and the zoom at its right; the file line moves to that text's hover. `comment_notes` draws a
  comment as the design's note: the yellow card with a drag grip and a speech bubble before its words
  and no code echo beside it, and a comment inside an event's action lane as the same card set in the
  lane, without its \"#\". `selection_as_outline` marks a selected event by its filled gutter and a
  two-pixel frame in the gutter's hue, with nothing washed over its lanes, so its words read as they
  did; a selected cell keeps its own highlight. The two-line group head (`group_head_two_line`) now
  drops the folder mark and the `## @ace_group(...)` echo and draws as the design's card - a one-pixel
  edge, a small filled fold arrow - with the count and the Active tick at its right. Drawing only:
  compiled output and the default-theme reading are unchanged, and Classic Sheet renders pixel for
  pixel as before.
- **An italic note keeps its last letter in the editor.** A variable's `##` note and a two-line group
  head's description lean into italics, and the span each is laid out in is measured upright. In the
  running editor the slanted words can measure a pixel or two wider than that, so the note lost its
  last letter (\"in units per secon\", \"the space ke\"). The note now draws in its span's room plus
  whatever the slant adds. Drawing only, and only where Editor Native draws italics.
- **Editor Native follows the design closer: variable cards, enum choices, the strip and the head.**
  A new token (`variable_row_cards`, on in Editor Native, stated off in every other shipped theme)
  draws each Inspector-style variable row as its own 28 px card - the event card's fill, its one-pixel
  edge and corner radius - with a 6 px gap to the next, in place of the flat variable wash; the
  words do not move. An enum variable reads its choice alone in the Inspector's capitals (\"State =
  Closed\", not \"GateState.CLOSED\"); typing into it still edits the file's spelling. The two-line
  group head is the count then the Active tick, with no switch ring between them (its fact moves to
  the tick's hover; the group's Edit dialog still switches it). A physics tick with no condition of
  its own reads \"System | Every tick (physics)\" like its neighbours, not a bare lowercase note. The
  type word keeps its family's colour, a deliberate departure from the design's single grey, so a
  `Color` and a `float` tell apart at a glance. The double-underscore rule of
  `hide_compiler_variables` is the prefix itself: the compiler and the packs mint too many such names
  to list, so a variable an author names `__cache` is left out of the rows too (it still compiles).
  With View > Text labels on, under any theme, the word-led strip now rests with a rule between Sheet
  and Save, the magnifier at the Find field's left, and without the play button or the chevron (both
  stay on the full strip, which View > Full toolbar opens). And a selected row about System, Keyboard,
  Mouse, Gamepad, Touch or Input no longer reports \"No node named System in the open scene\": those are
  never nodes, so the Scene dock is left alone, and a row the open scene does not hold is followed
  quietly rather than flagged in red. Drawing only: compiled output and the default-theme reading are
  unchanged.
- **Editor Native puts the sheet straight under the toolbar.** A new theme switch
  (`fold_workspace_bands`, on in Editor Native, stated off in every other shipped theme) folds away
  the five bands that stood between the strip and the sheet, so the order is the design's: tabs, the
  toolbar, the canvas's own padding (8 px above, 10 px at each side), then the class head. Nothing
  they offered goes: the title strip's name and unsaved state are the tab's (its ●, and \"Unsaved
  (in-memory)\" on its hover); the identity band's line and its published counts (\"@ 1 knob\") ride
  the class head name's hover; the Conditions | Actions header's two grabbers move into the sheet,
  where pressing the divider inside any event drags the lane split and pressing a fixed object
  column's hairline drags its width; Add event is the toolbar's Add Event and + Add… the right-click
  on empty space it always was; and the side rail rests tucked with no edge strip left behind until
  you bring it out with the new View > Side Rail (its chevron and its grabber tuck it again, and the
  choice is remembered per project). Drawing and layout only: compiled output and the default-theme
  reading are unchanged, and Classic Sheet renders pixel for pixel as before.

### Added: the first game you already know, as a showcase and a ten-step tour

- **Top-Down Shooter.** A player that faces the mouse and fires where it points, monsters that walk
  in from the edges and take three hits, an explosion and a score - the game most event-sheet users
  built first - as a new showcase in four small sheets. The monster is a family, so the main sheet's
  picking row reads \"For each Shooter Monster where health ≤ 0\" and compiles to the loop a person
  writes by hand. Checked by a runtime smoke on the real scene: one bullet takes one point of
  health, a monster at zero explodes, and the score reads 1. The Start page lists it.
- **Build a top-down shooter (10 steps).** A new Welcome window button starts a tour on the same
  floating window as the built-in one: ten steps that rebuild the main sheet on a practice sheet,
  each ticking itself off by compiling the sheet and finding the line its rows write, so a row made
  in the picker, by Quick add, by the Ghost Row or typed as GDScript all count. The last step opens
  the finished game. A new guide, the Top-Down Shooter Tutorial, shows the same ten steps with the
  GDScript beside each row.

### Added: picking, offered in Godot's shape

- **\"Enemy health <= 0\" on another object's sheet offers the For each.** In another event-sheet
  editor a condition on an object type picks every instance that passes it. A Godot script belongs
  to one node, so the same words typed on the Player's sheet had nothing to pick from. When the
  object named is a family (a script marked `## @ace_family(Enemy)`), the Ghost Row now leads with
  `⟳ For each Enemy where health ≤ 0`: Enter writes one event with a For each over the family and the
  test as its filter, compiling to the loop a person writes by hand - `for enemy in
  get_tree().get_nodes_in_group(\"family_enemy\")` with the test inside. Pressed with C on a selected
  event, the loop joins that event instead. The other editor's `=`, `≤`, `≥` and `≠` read as
  GDScript's, the sheet's own class is never offered, and words that are not a comparison are not
  guessed at.
- **A family loop reads as the family's row.** A loop over a family's group - including the
  `get_nodes_in_group(\"family_enemy\")` call a hand-written file spells it with - reads `For each
  Enemy` with Enemy in the object column, where it used to read the engine call; a family pick with a
  test names the family (`Enemy`) rather than its group (`Family Enemy`).

### Added: the Include you look for, where you look for it, as one line

- **Include sheet… on the canvas menu.** Including a shared sheet was two menus deep on the toolbar.
  It is now on the menu the canvas's \"+ Add…\" corner link and a right-click on empty space open,
  which is where a reader from another event-sheet editor reaches for \"Include event sheet\".
- **An included shared sheet reads as one Include line at the head.** Each shared sheet a script
  includes gets one flat line in the head, with no pills: `Include DamageRules · shared sheet, as
  its base - extends DamageRules`, or `Include PauseHandling · shared sheet, as a helper - var
  _pause_handling, called from _process`. The line leads with the word the other editor uses and
  ends in the exact Godot shape it stands for, so both readers find their own word on the same
  line; clicking the name opens the shared sheet. A helper's forwarding call inside an event now
  reads `Include PauseHandling ▸ run its Every Frame`, the way a base's `super` call already did,
  and the file name on that action is a word rather than a pill. The Include bar of an ordinary base
  script lost its pills and its purple wash too.

### Added: an expression field reads the spelling another event-sheet editor uses

- **`Player.X + random(10) & \" px\"` is read, not refused.** The first expression a reader from
  another event-sheet editor writes used to go red, because it is not GDScript. An expression field
  now reads it: an object's `X`, `Y`, `Angle`, `Opacity`, `Text` or `Visible` for a node that is
  really in the open scene, `Self.X` for the object the sheet is on, `&` joining text, and the
  functions the project importer already knew (`random`, `choose`, `dt`, `len`, `left`, `mid`,
  `tokenat`, `zeropad`, `uppercase` and the rest). The help strip says the GDScript it will write
  under \"reads as\", nothing is coloured and nothing blocks OK, and OK writes that GDScript - the
  typed spelling is not kept, so the file on disk stays plain code and the round trip is untouched.
  Text that is already GDScript is never rewritten, an object the scene has not got is left as
  typed, `&` between two numbers stays bitwise AND unless the field takes text, and a line still
  being typed (`a &`) stays refused rather than committing half a thought.

### Added: the Welcome window asks how you usually make games

- **One question instead of three hidden settings.** The Welcome window opens on \"How do you usually
  make games?\" with three answers. \"I come from another event-sheet editor\" switches on that
  editor's words, the Classic sheet look and the Project bar, and opens the migration guide in the
  Manual rather than a browser tab. \"I write GDScript\" switches on Godot's words and the GDScript
  panel beside every sheet. \"I'm new to event sheets\" keeps the familiar words and starts the tour.
  A line under the answers says exactly what the pressed one set and where to change it, every
  setting stays reversible where it always lived, and the answer is remembered per project. The old
  footer link to the guide is gone, because the second answer is that link and more.

### Added: the picker answers to the names another event-sheet editor uses

- **Search reads the importer's own table.** The project importer already knows which verb each of
  that editor's rows becomes - \"go to layout\" is Go To Layout, \"create object\" is Spawn Scene
  Instance, \"every x seconds\", \"compare two values\", \"subtract from\" and sixty more. The picker and
  Quick add now search that same table, so a reader who types the name they have used for years
  lands on the verb the importer would have written, pre-selected when the whole phrase is typed.
  A row found that way says the phrase back after its name in quotes, and says nothing when the two
  names already agree. The one row that only stands in for something with no equivalent here - the
  window size - is never offered, and a test walks every phrase so none can point at a verb that no
  longer ships.

### Changed: CI runs as parallel jobs

- **One job ran every gate in a row, 20 to 26 minutes.** The full suite took 12-14 of them and the
  whole-project standing-contracts walk another 8-9, on one machine. Now six matrix jobs each run a
  sixth of the suite beside a sixth of the contracts walk, a seventh runs the timing tests alone,
  and an eighth runs the whole-tree gates (import, Doctor, pack audit, translations). Two steps went:
  the packs-and-demo contracts walk is part of the whole-project one, and the fast gate only re-ran
  tests the suite runs anyway.
- **`tools/verify_sheets.gd --shard k/n`** reads every n-th file from k, so n processes read the
  project once between them; shard 0 also runs the migration check, which samples the project as one.
- **The serial suite runs nightly** (`serial-suite.yml`, also runnable by hand): one process running
  every test after every other is the only run that catches a test leaving state behind for a later
  one, and a six-way split mostly never makes that pairing.

### Fixed: the Add picker in the editor holds every pack's verbs, not 142 of 4,477

- **In the real editor, 149 of the 179 pack scripts published nothing.** The registry reflected
  each pack by making an instance of it, and the editor will not instantiate a script that is not
  `@tool` - it runs no game code - which is most packs. Measured under `--editor`: 142 pack verbs
  reached the picker where a plain run found 4,477. Every test runs outside the editor, where every
  pack instantiates, so the suite never saw it.
- **A pack the editor will not instantiate is now reflected from its script**, which declares the
  same members. Where an instance can be made it still is. One door, `reflect_script`, now serves the
  picker, the Doctor's reading check, the successor map, the pack update dry run and the provider
  preview, so none of them can drop a pack the others see. Measured under `--editor` after: 4,477.
- **`script_reflection_parity_test`** asks every shipped pack both ways and holds the script's
  verbs to the instance's, field for field and in order.
- **The same trap, two more places**: the provider wizard read a healthy first draft that publishes
  nothing yet as \"cannot be instantiated\", and `EventSheets.verify_pack` called a sound pack
  unparseable, both only inside the editor. Both now ask whether the script compiled.

### Fixed: the editor starts in about 11 seconds, not 30

- **The plugin compiled the whole editor half of itself at every editor start.** Godot sets up the
  debugger tab that already exists the moment the plugin adds its debugger bridge, and the bridge
  used that moment to empty the Run's stores - the hit counts, the timings, the state trail. The
  state trail named the state watch and the fact scanner, which between them reach the compiler,
  the registry and everything the dock uses: about 546 scripts compiled before the editor was
  usable, whether or not a sheet was ever opened. Measured on the same machine and checkout with
  `--editor --headless --quit-after 1`, twice each: 29.8 s and 27.7 s before, 10.5 s and 11.1 s after.
- **The stores now start over when a game starts**, from the debugger session's `started`, which is
  the Run their comments always meant. The editor keeps one debugger tab between runs, so emptying
  them when the tab was made meant a second Run added its hit counts to the first's. A second copy of
  the game joining a run already under way (Play as host + client) is not a new Run and empties
  nothing, and which copy each window is gets read as it starts rather than once at editor start.
- **The state trail reaches the watch and the fact scanner by path**, so loading it costs the trail
  alone.
- **Held by the boot lint**: the debugger bridge and the trail may not name those classes in code,
  and the hook the editor calls at boot may not load anything - by class or by path, since a path
  string names no class and slipped past the old rule.
- A cold session still compiles the editor half once, now at the first sheet tab instead of at
  every start - so a session spent in scenes and scripts never pays it.

### Changed: tests and checks another test already made are gone

- **An audit of all 820 test files**, each file checked for an assertion no other test makes.
  Eight had none: `while_loop`, `icon_roundtrip`, `const_roundtrip`, `gap3_body_lift`,
  `variable_export_badge`, `variable_export`, `preview_autounlock` and `variable_row_format`.
  Every check they made is made, as strictly or more so, by a test that stays. The one case only the
  byte gates still covered - a global marked not exported emits as a plain `var` - is now pinned in
  `codegen_parity_test`, so the fast gate keeps it.
- **`VariableRowFormat` is removed.** It was lifted out of a widget that no longer exists and was
  never wired into the editor, so its test guarded code nothing runs.
- **Duplicate checks inside 44 tests that stay are gone too**: a check another test already makes
  on the same path with the same or a stricter expectation, or one that cannot fail - a test
  computing `clampf` itself, a hand-copied lambda standing in for the shipped template, a
  `has_method` asked after the method already ran. Each file names the test that still pins what
  left it. Where an audit found a cover weaker than claimed, the check stayed; and the leak test's
  emission paths now also pin that the action is written at all, not only that no `host.` form is.

### Fixed: including a shared sheet into a script that already has the handler

- **A helper include no longer writes a second `_process`.** Including a shared sheet as a helper
  into a script that already had its own `_process` (or `_ready`, `_physics_process`, `_input`)
  reported success and wrote a second function of the same name, which Godot refuses to parse. The
  one forwarding call now goes at the top of the function that is already there, in its own indent
  and with its own argument names; a header it cannot safely add to (a one-line body, a header
  broken over lines, the wrong number of arguments) is refused with the exact line to write by hand.
  The member the include adds also stopped landing inside a function whose body held a comment.
- **A shared sheet included as the base keeps running.** In Godot 4 a script's own `_process`
  replaces its base's unless it calls `super(delta)`, so a shared sheet included as the base had its
  Every tick events stop the moment the script had a tick of its own - measured, the base ticked
  zero times. The include now writes `super(...)` as the first line of every such function the
  script already has. A tick added later without it is a warning in the Doctor and the quiet amber
  state on the event that overrides it, with the sentence in the help strip.

### Changed: the head bars are flat, one line, and say nothing twice

- **The head is painted the way an Include row is painted elsewhere.** The bar a file opens on,
  and the Input, Instance variables and Behaviors bars under it, sat on a purple wash one and a
  half rows tall, wore a row of filled word pills, and trailed a grey echo of the very lines they
  folded. They are one row tall now, on one quiet flat tint (`head_bar_color`, a token on the sheet
  theme, stated by every shipped theme), the counts and kinds are plain words - `Behaviors · 34 on
  this object - Spring 10, Tween 10, Sine 8` - each still a door into its pack, and nothing trails
  the bar: the folded lines are read by opening the fold, not by squinting at its echo. The words
  `reads as events` left the bar for a file that reads whole; only a count of script blocks that
  did not is said, and it still walks them one per click.

### Changed: a behavior pack opens on its verbs

- **Four bands instead of a wall.** A pack opened to read used to open on its implementation -
  eleven variables, a script block and the loop that counts a timer down, with the two verbs you
  clicked the pack for last of all. It now rests in `Settings` (its `@export` knobs) and `Verbs`
  (every published function as its own block, then the triggers it fires), both open, over
  `How it works` (the lifecycle and tick events, and the Helpers folder) and `Internal state` (the
  private variables, the constants and the script blocks), both folded.
- **A verb reads as the verb the picker offers.** `ƒ Flash  seconds  action`, not `ƒ On Flash` - a
  published verb is not a trigger, and the kind word beside it says whether it acts,
  answers a question or hands a value back. An authored
  display sentence is read whole (`ƒ Blink  pattern  for  seconds  s`) rather than repeated as
  input chips.
- **A trigger says when it happens.** A pack's own signals close the Verbs band, and each one now
  names the verbs and events of the same file that emit it - `emits jumped  fired by Jump` - read
  off the rows rather than off the declaration, which says nothing about when anything fires.
- **The plumbing reads in sheet words.** `set_process(true)` / `set_process(false)` are
  **Start ticking** and **Stop ticking**, with the physics tick named, and the host guard folds onto
  the tick's own line - `⟳ Every tick (draw)  host is valid`. Inside a pack the System object and
  the class the file is spelled with both read as the name the picker offers the pack by, while the
  node the pack rides keeps its own name, `host`.
- **All of it is a reading.** The file keeps its own order for the code view and for what the
  compiler emits, both packs re-emit byte for byte, a file that draws its own structure with
  `#region` fences turns the reading off, and a pack you are authoring rather than reading is
  untouched.

### Added: breathing room is a theme setting - Compact, Comfortable, Spacious

- **Six numbers decide how much room a row gets, and all six are tokens now.** Row height, the air
  inside a cell, the gap between two events, how far a sub-event steps in (and whether a rail line
  is drawn down that step), the object-name column, and the border round a whole event. They live
  on the theme resource the project owns, so a density travels with the sheet instead of being a
  constant inside the plugin.
- **Three starters ship, and Comfortable is the new default.** *Menu ▸ View ▸ Sheet theme ▸
  Density* offers Compact (32 rows become 28 - exactly the numbers the editor shipped with, for
  anyone who liked it that way), Comfortable and Spacious, tightest first, with the one this
  sheet's spacing already matches ticked. Picking one copies its six numbers onto a duplicate of
  the sheet's own style, so every colour that style states survives untouched.
- **An existing theme reads Comfortable unless it says otherwise.** The six numbers are tokens on
  the theme resource, and a `.tres` written before they existed states none of them - so it takes the
  new defaults and every sheet it dresses gets the extra room. Nothing about the sheet's BYTES
  changes, and Compact is one click away with the numbers the editor shipped with.
- **A starter is a file, not a look we chose for you.** Duplicate one of the three
  `EventSheetDensityStyle` resources in `addons/eventsheet/themes/density/`, change the numbers,
  save it beside them, and it is in the menu the next time View opens - no code, no registration
  list, no restart. The Theme Editor edits every one of the tokens alongside the colours.

### Added: the event card, the gutter and the rail

- **An event is one bordered card.** Both lanes, every row of an OR stack, the padding, the gaps
  between cells and the band under the shorter lane sit inside a single 1 px border on a slightly
  lighter ground, so \"which event does this condition belong to\" is answered by shape rather than
  by counting indents. The event's number stays outside that border, in the gutter, where a margin
  mark belongs.
- **The rail is a token.** A sub-event hangs off a line drawn down its parent's indent step, and a
  sheet that wants those pixels back turns it off with the rest of its density.
- **All of it is paint.** The card, the gutter margin and the rail reserve no width, are never
  measured and are never hit-tested - a theme that draws no card lays out identically to one that
  does, and the event's click target is the whole card either way.

### Added: the Classic Sheet theme starter

- **For a reader coming from another event-sheet editor**, `Classic Sheet` in *Menu ▸ View ▸ Sheet
  theme*: bordered event cards on a lighter ground, numbers in a margin outside them, bold group
  bars, green pinned local-variable rows, a blue plate before every object name, values in bold,
  disabled actions struck through, and a 40 / 60 lane split. It is an ordinary theme resource like
  every other one - duplicate it and change anything you disagree with.

### Fixed

- **A long comment was drawn through the echo of the line it writes.** The echo is the note's
  own words with a marker in front, so a note long enough to reach it ended up as two long texts
  on one line, neither readable - and a file whose closing paragraph is hoisted to the top showed
  it every time. The note now fills to where the right-anchored run starts, and wraps to the same
  edge its height is counted against.
- **A right press beside the cells outlined one thing and offered another.** The press selects
  the whole event, as a left press there does, but the menu was still built around the cell whose
  LINE the pointer happened to be on - so the outline said \"event\" and the menu said \"this
  condition\" for one and the same press. The menu now answers with whatever was selected.
- **A panel slid off the rail by its own button came back ticked in the View menu.** The tick
  read a second, private record that only the menu path and the fold signal ever wrote. The rail
  is the one record now - the header button, a divider dragged past the header and the View
  entry are three ways of saying the same thing - and the tick is re-read every time the menu
  opens. The private record is gone rather than kept in step.
- **Three rail panels could not be dragged shut.** A split container stops a drag at its
  children's combined minimum, and the Functions list, the Objects tree and the Picker preview
  each held 110 px open from the inside - so the divider never reached the threshold that slides
  a panel off the rail, and only the header button could. The rail decides the column heights;
  its panels bring no minimum of their own, inside or out.
- **The panel minimise button wore an en dash.** A plain ASCII glyph now, which is what the
  house rule asks of every other line of text in the repository.
- **A lost line continuation in the row renderer** left three raw tabs mid-condition. It parsed,
  and it read as a mistake because it was one.
- **A re-saved theme preset stopped being readable by the coverage gate.** Godot writes a
  `uid=\"uid://...\"` onto every `[ext_resource]` line it re-saves, and the preset reader looked for
  `id=\"` without anchoring it - so it read the uid as the resource id, mapped every block to no
  script at all, and reported a fully-stated preset as holding none.

### Changed: one head bar, and the description is a comment

- **The six bars a file opened with are one line.** `class_name`, `extends`, the Include bar with
  its coverage chip, the `##` description, the Input bar and the variables bar used to stack up
  before event 1. An opened file now rests under a single line - `InputRebindDemo extends Control
  · reads as events · 4 input actions · 1 variable · 2 script blocks` - and its ▸ opens every one
  of those bars again, unchanged, as the line's own fold. Each count is read off the bar it folds,
  so the line and the fold cannot disagree.
- **Each fact keeps its door.** The class it extends opens the base script when that base is
  another script of the project; the coverage words still walk the script blocks one per click;
  the ▸ brings back the Input bar with every control and its bindings, and the variables bar with
  every variable and its own sentence.
- **The file's prose is a comment row, drawn once.** The `##` block was a band that drew its value
  and its echo of the same line one over the other, and a pack that closes on a paragraph saying the
  same thing had that paragraph hoisted on top of it - the sentence twice, once of it doubled. It is
  a comment row at the top of the sheet now, the whole block as one sentence, and the paragraph a
  pack closes on is left where its author wrote it.
- **A control the Input Map has not got went quiet.** The Input bar used to mark it
  `⚠ demo_jump` and say `not in the Input Map` inside the sheet. It is a finding, so the sheet's
  only signal is the amber state on the head bar; the sentence is read in the help strip under the
  selected bar and in the Doctor's inbox, which already carried it.

### Changed: the Behaviors band counts by kind

- **Twenty-four children stop being twenty-four rows.** An object that composes many small
  behaviors opened as a page of repeated names before its first event, and a reader could not tell
  seven Springs from one. The band says the count and the kinds on one folded line -
  `▸ Behaviors · 34  on this object - Spring ×10 · Tween ×10 · Sine ×8 · Flash ×2 · Juice · Scenes` -
  with the counts as text chips, never widgets.
- **A kind is a door.** Each chip on the folded line carries that pack's own script, read off the
  scene line the behavior was recognised on, so clicking the word opens that behavior as a sheet -
  the same jump the Include bar makes, through the same field on the span. A chip whose scene line
  named no script stays a word.
- **It opens by KIND, with the settings and the nodes.** One line per kind:
  `Sine ×8  movement = \"vertical\" · period = 1.6 · magnitude = 18.0 · on Tile0 … Tile7`. A behavior
  tuned differently from its siblings keeps a line of its own, which is the whole reason to open the
  fold. Folded by default, and the fold is remembered like every other.

### Performance: a tab keeps its reading

- **Switching between two open sheets builds nothing.** A switch used to be a full load - every row
  rebuilt through the reading layers, the vocabulary rebuilt from the sheet's providers and the
  addon folder, the undo log cleared, the generated-code panel recompiled - so two sheets open side
  by side cost a reading on every glance between them. Each tab now keeps its own: the rows and
  their layout, the scroll, the selection, the folds, the lane split, its log of edits and the code
  the panel showed. On a behaviour pack of 1,149 rows a switch measured 5.86 and 5.90 seconds before,
  and 87 and 64 ms after.
- **Rows are built again only when the sheet changed.** Every tab carries a stamp: an edit revision
  bumped by the one refresh every sheet mutation ends with, and the modification time of the file it
  is stored in, read from disk at switch time. Either moving rebuilds the tab once - and only once.
- **The vocabulary is kept unless its sources moved.** The ACE registry is rebuilt when the sheet
  lists different provider scripts, or when the addon fleet, the bridge's registrations, the taught
  verbs or an annotated autoload changed; definitions are immutable and shared across tabs already,
  so keeping one is keeping a list of sources, never a second copy of a descriptor.
- **Undo survives a switch, and an open.** The log is no longer cleared when you look at another
  sheet, and opening a sheet from disk no longer clears it either - opening a third file used to
  take the first two tabs' history with it, because the host editor keeps one stack for the whole
  dock. Every edit is tagged with the tab it was made on instead, so an undo of an edit made
  elsewhere brings that tab back and lands there instead of over the sheet in front of you, and
  the History panel shows the steps the tab on screen was edited by rather than a merged list of
  every tab's.
- **An undo says where it went.** Landing on another tab now names the sheet it landed on
  (\"Applied undo/redo on player.gd\"), and a step whose tab has since been closed says that rather
  than letting Ctrl+Z do nothing without a word.
- **The status line stopped saying \"Loaded:\" on a switch**, because a switch is not a load. It says
  nothing at all; a file that changed on disk behind a tab rebuilds it once and says so honestly -
  \"Changed on disk: player.gd - these rows are the editor's copy\", because the rows were built
  from the sheet the editor is holding and the re-import is still the file watcher's to offer.

### Added: the object's variables are Inspector rows

- **\"Instance variables · N\" is a band with rows under it, not a button.** Selecting a node whose
  script is a sheet used to show a button and a sentence listing the variables that were NOT in the
  Inspector; the Inspector now carries the variables themselves - one row per declared `var` /
  `const`, with the name, the type in the sheet's own plain words (\"number\", \"table\", \"list of
  text\"), the initial value, and a pencil that opens the sheet on the line that declares it.
  `const` declarations and `@onready` ones are rows too, because they are values a reader comes to
  the object looking for.
- **The rows open instantly.** They are the same light scan of the script's own text that printed
  the count before, so a selection still costs no compile and no sheet open - the object popup's
  census is not needed to list variables, and it keeps its job for the canvas click.
- **Typing an initial value writes the declaration.** The value goes through the sheet's own undo
  funnel, the one its variables table writes through, so the line that lands is the line the table
  would have written, one Ctrl+Z takes it back, and every other byte of the file is untouched. A
  declaration written in the inferred spelling (`var mode := \"idle\"`) is rewritten in that same
  spelling, source text and all.
- **\"+ Add instance variable\" opens the sheet's Add variable dialog**, so a variable born in the
  Inspector carries every field one born on the sheet does.

### Added: every showcase describes itself on the Start page, and a platformer that pathfinds

- **A showcase card's line comes from the showcase.** The Start page has always walked
  `demo/showcase/` for its folders, but only fifteen of them had a genre and a pitch written on the
  page; the rest read \"Showcase - A playable example you can open and take apart.\" Every folder now
  gets its line from the about comment its own sheet opens with, cut down to the first thing it
  says - the first sentence, the colon that introduces a list, or the \"and\" that opens a second
  clause, whichever comes first. Fourteen pitches written for the page are still there and still
  win, because they read better on a card than the sheet's own opening clause; each retires the day
  its showcase says it as well.
- **The genre is the one fact the page still writes down**, because nothing in a showcase says
  which shelf a reader looks on for it - and the suite now pins that table against the folders on
  disk in both directions: a showcase with no shelf fails by name, and a shelf whose folder is gone
  fails by name. Path Chase's shelf is corrected from \"Top-down\" to \"Platformer\", which is what it
  has always been.
- **New showcase: Platformer Pathfinding** (`demo/showcase/platformer_pathfinding/`), the
  pathfinding pack's plain case beside Path Chase's feature tour. A side-view level with one gap
  and two ledges, a keyboard Player on the top ledge, and a Chaser wired in three actions: build
  the nav graph from the TileMapLayer, draw it, and Find Path To Node the Player once - that action
  keeps following, so there is no repath timer to write. The Chaser carries no movement code of its
  own; the pathfinder steers the sibling Platformer Movement through its `ai_move_axis` seam, which
  is why it accelerates, jumps the gap and climbs both ledges under the rules the Player plays by.
  The drift gate reads `showcases=29 drifted=0`.

### Changed: the rail rests, slides and gets out of the way

- **The left rail is a chain of splitters, not a stack of panels.** Open Sheets, Objects, Functions,
  Anatomy and the Picker preview sat in one VBoxContainer, each carrying a fixed minimum size, and
  the only draggable edge in the workspace was the rail-to-canvas grabber - which the editor theme
  only draws while the pointer is over it. Every boundary drags now, every grabber is drawn rather
  than revealed on hover, and no panel brings a minimum width of its own: the rail can be dragged
  to a sliver or out to half the workspace, and the width is remembered per project.
- **The rail rests at two panels.** Open Sheets and Objects are open; Functions, Anatomy and the
  Picker preview are folds, closed until you want them. A panel with nothing to put in it is not
  there at all: Anatomy appears for a behaviour pack, the Picker preview while the picker is open.
  The Anatomy list stopped drawing organs that count to zero - \"Triggers · 0\" is a counter, not a
  fact about the behaviour, and a column of them is what made the rail a wall.
- **Every panel minimises, and so does the rail.** A button at the right edge of each header slides
  that panel off: it takes no room, not even a header, and its name waits in a tab at the foot of
  the rail. Dragging a panel's divider up past its own header does the same. The chevron above the
  column tucks the WHOLE rail into a narrow edge strip with the panel names reading down it, and the
  sheet takes the width; dragging the rail's grabber into the canvas edge does the same. Nothing is
  ever closed - it is off screen, the tab says what is there, and View ▸ Panels lists them. Every
  fold, size and tucked panel is remembered per project.
- **Open Sheets groups duplicates.** Eight tabs on one behaviour pack used to be eight identical
  lines; they are one line with a count now (\"Flash ×8\"), and clicking it walks the tabs it stands
  for. An unsaved sheet has no path to be the same as, so it never groups.
- **The Properties bar arrives with the selection.** With nothing selected it was a 280 px column
  carrying one sentence telling you to select something. It is its splitter handle now, so the
  canvas has the width until there is something to edit, and it opens at the width you left it at on
  the first click of a condition, action, object or group - the way the Inspector fills when a node
  is picked. The sentence is said once, in the status bar. Dragging the bar into the right edge
  tucks it away behind a chevron, like the rail.

### Added: the words grew from nouns to sentences

- **The Words page has a preset row.** Menu > Tools > Words now opens on one dropdown that sets the
  whole vocabulary at once: **Godot words** (the engine's own nouns and verbs), **Familiar words**
  (the nouns table below it - the same thing View > Familiar Words has always switched), and
  **Event-sheet-editor words** (those nouns *and* a handful of verbs). A fourth reading, *custom*,
  is what the row says when the switches and the words you typed do not add up to one of the three;
  it is a reading rather than a thing to pick. The row follows the switches rather than remembering
  a choice of its own, so flipping Familiar Words from the View menu shows up here as the
  vocabulary it actually produced, and the live preview under the table now renders a trigger and
  an action as well as the nouns.
- **A handful of verbs read in the other editor's sentence.** With the third preset on, a row says
  *Create object* where Godot says *Spawn a copy of*, *is overlapping in family* where it says *is
  touching*, *Set disabled* where a hand-written `set_process(false)` reads *Set Every tick (draw)
  deactivated*, and a loop over a group reads *For each item in family \"enemies\"*. The picker's rows
  carry both names at once - *Create object - Spawn A Copy* - so the Godot verb never disappears
  from the place you pick it.
- **Nothing is hidden and nothing moves.** An alias is display text: the ace_id, the codegen
  template, the emitted GDScript and every stored byte are untouched, the shipped wording is always
  the \"off\" side, and the generated-code panel and the tooltips keep Godot's spelling whichever
  vocabulary is on. Off is the default, and with it off the words seam hands a row back exactly the
  text it was given - the reading gate is byte-identical either side of the change.
- **The search answers to either name, whichever vocabulary is on.** Type *create object* and the
  picker offers Spawn A Copy; type *overlapping* and it offers Is Touching Group. You never have to
  know which words the editor is currently in to find the row you mean.
- **The alias table is a drop-in CSV, not code.** `addons/eventsheet/words/verb_words.csv` ships the
  words the plugin knows, in the same shape the nine translation files use - one row per alias, with
  the key, the word, what it names, the shipped name and the shipped row's opening words. Any CSV
  under `res://eventsheet_words/` is merged over it, so a team can re-word one alias, or add their
  own, without touching a script and without losing it to a plugin update. A suite gate holds every
  key to a shipped verb in the registry or a reading the editor composes, and every \"off\" side to
  the shipped wording character for character.

### Changed: one event, one outline, and the whole card is the click target

- **A selected event is one shape.** Selecting an event whose conditions are OR'd used to draw a
  filled row and an outline per cell, so a block of three questions read as three selected
  conditions. The selection of an EVENT is now a single frame around the union of everything that
  event is drawn as - both lanes, the number in its gutter, and every row a statement reads as when
  it takes more than one - with the cells left flat inside it. Clicking one cell still selects that
  cell alone, and it still wears its own outline, which is what tells the two selections apart.
- **The whole card takes the press.** The gutter, the padding, the space between two cells, the
  band under the shorter lane and the gap beside an OR'd condition all select the event they belong
  to. The gap between two events belongs to the nearer of them - its top half to the event above,
  its bottom half to the one below - so there is no longer a strip of sheet where a press clears the
  selection instead of moving it. Ctrl still toggles the event in a multi-selection, and a box
  selection begins only where there is no card: above the first event and below the last.
- **The lane divider and the object columns are dragged from the header band.** The
  \"Conditions | Actions\" band above the sheet grows a grabber on each boundary and the ↔ cursor to
  say so. Down the sheet those boundaries are lines: a press within five pixels of one used to start
  a resize on whichever row it landed in, anywhere in the file. View ▸ Reset Lane Split puts the
  split back at the theme's own share.
- **The band's columns line up with the sheet's.** The header spans the whole workspace while the
  canvas starts after the left rail, so \"Conditions\" and \"Actions\" stood a rail's width to the left
  of the lanes they name. Both labels, the divider and the new grabbers are measured from where the
  canvas actually starts.
",
"version": 1
}
